      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>"MP McDOUGM-L'S PATH= SELF-RAISING FLOUR THAIIT THE EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30 _1902</head>
        <p>"MP McDOUGM-L'S PATH= SELF-RAISING FLOUR THAIIT THE EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30 _1902</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WIZ NATIONAL COUNCIL'S APPEAL FOR FUNDS</p>
        <p>The main subject of interest among Free Churchmen is still the education crisis. The majority of 237 by which the second reading of the Bill was carried has not daunted Free Churchmen at all. It has rather served as an inspiring challenge, quickening activity, deter- mination, and earnestness. The storm of indignation against the Government grows more violent day by day. Meetings are being organised by the Free Church Councils from the Tweed to Land's End. The National Council has circulated leaflets against the Bill to the number of nearly 3,000,000, and is unremitting in the work of inspiring and directing the fighting forces. Of course it has been involved in heavy expenses, which have been met up to the present out of the ordinary resources of the Council. It is felt, however, that the great fight has just begun, and that it is of the utmost importance that, while the Bill is being discussed in the House of Commons, clause by clause, the work of agitating and educating. should be kept up un- ceasingly. An appeal, signed by Dr. Townsend, Dr. Monro Gibson Rev. Thomas Law, and Mr. Evan Spicer (the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee), has been issued, asking for immediate donations to the extent of several thousands of pounds. The amount aske4 for, having regard to the resouces of Free Churchmen and the immense importance of the issues at stake, is not large. Subscriptions should be sent to Mr. Spicer or Mr. Law, at the Memorial Hall, Narringdon-street, London, Fk.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>DEL- HORTON.</head>
        <p>DEL- HORTON.</p>
        <p>The election of Dr. Horton to the chair of the Congregational Union for 1903 was ex- pected. His majority over the other two can- didates was simply overwhelming. Mr. Chater, of Southport, and Mr. Clarkson, of South- ampton, are able men and have done good work, but their reputations are almost entirely local. Another consideration which weighed in the ballot was that 1903 is the turn of London. There is an unwritten rule that the Chairman of the Union should come from the provinces and from London every alternate year. 1902 was the turn for the provinces. Unfortunately, Dr. Caleb Scott, owing to serious illness, was unable to take the chair, but the duties of the position were admirably discharged by Dr. Barrett, of Norwich. Dr. Barrett is truly a Master of Assemblies. There is no man more skilled in the management of an excited, eager, tumultuous meeting. At a memorable sitting some years ago it was Dr. Barrett who saved the Twentieth Century Fund. It is safe to say that, if Dr. Horton is granted health and strength, his year of office, at least as regards his platform appearance, will be brilliantly suc- cassful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>MR. GEORGH WHITE, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. GEORGH WHITE, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. George White, M.P., is the new Vice- President of the Baptist Union. The office of. Vice-President carries with it, in due course, the office of President, upon which Mr. White will enter in April next. He will be the third layman who has sat in the chair of the Baptist Union, his predecessors being Dr. E. B. Under- hill, who died in May last year, and Colonel Griffin, who died a few months ago. Mr. White, who is 62 years of age, has had an inte- resting career. He is a native of Bourne, Lin- colnshira, and came to Norwich when he was 16 to be a junior clerk in a leather and shoe factory.. He is now head of the firm, Howlett and White, which employs a thousand men. Mr. Gladstone, on his last visit to Norwich, after hearing Mr. White speak, said, "You need o no farther for a Liberal candidate for this city." Mr. White has been a Sunday School teacher for forty-five years. Every Sunday morning he conducts a class of grown-tp men, with a membership of two hundred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>YR. WALTER ,RUNCIMAS, M.P.</head>
        <p>YR. WALTER ,RUNCIMAS, M.P.</p>
        <p>The June Free Church Chronicle con- tains an interesting sketch of Mr. Walter Runciman, the successor of Mr. Mark Oldroycl, in the representation of Dews- bury. Like Mr. Oldroyd, Mr. Runciman is a Free Churchman by birth and conviction. His grandfather was a Wesleyan, so also is his father, and he himself follows in their footsteps. He had a brilliant career at Trinity College, Cambridge, although, oddly enough, he did not even Speak once at the Union debates. Mr. Runciman, whose home is at West Denton Hall, is a member of Brunswick-place Wesleyan Chapel, Newcastle. When about four years 1 ago he was appointed a member of the Wesleyan Committee of Privileges, he was the youngest person on that august body. Mr. Runciman first came prominently before the public in 1899, when he and Mr. Emmott won a notable vic- -tory for the Liberal party at Oldham, defeating Mr. Winston Churchill and Mr. Mawdsley. He received a rare chance of distinction when he was chosen to move the adjournment of the House of Commons to discuss the publication ,of the Spion Kop despatches. This effort, his maiden speech, was warmly commended in the House and in the Press. Mr. Runciman has a graceful manner of address, and his speeches have a literary charm which makes them plea- sant both to readers and hearers. He is not yet thirty-two years of age, and Front Bench- men on the , Opposon side predict great things of him.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCH CORONATION SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The National Council has prepared an Order of Service of the commemoration of the Corona- tion of King Edward VII. for the use of Free Churches and' Free Church Councils. ?It is fully expected that on June 26, or on the following Sunday, practically all the Free Churches will hold a Coronation service, and in many places the Councils will arrange for united religions commemorations of this great event in the life of the nation. The Order of Service issued by the National Council will be found admirably adapted for use at such services. It can be supplied at Is. 6d. per 100, and 12s. 6d. per 1000, on application to , the Rev. Thomas Law, Memorial Hall, London, E.C. Specimen copies may be obtained gratis.. The National Council is prepared to supply the music of the hymns and anthems on very favourable terms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EXT. NEHEMIAH CURNOCIC.</head>
        <p>EXT. NEHEMIAH CURNOCIC.</p>
        <p>Hosts of Methodists will hear with regret that the health of the Rev. Nehemiah Curnock, the editor of the Methodist Recorder, has broken down. In addition to his heavy literary duties Mr. Curnock takes an active part in the work of the great Committees, by which Wesleyan Methodism is .so largely governed, and his handsome, striking face is frequently seen in the pulpits of the Church which he serves so efficiently and so devotedly. A West-end specialist has ordered Mr. Conaock to take complete rest until the Conference which is to be held at Manchester in August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>GIPSY SMITH.</head>
        <p>GIPSY SMITH.</p>
        <p>'Readers who are tWnking. of the. Isle of Man as a likely h9liday resort will be interested to ;leer that Gipsy Smith, the famous evangelist, has consented to conduct services at Douglas during the latter half of August. The mission at Pontypool which Mr. Smith has just concluded was eminently successful. All the meetings were crowded, and the universal testimony-is that the town was never so deenly and so wholesomely stirred. Mr. Smith's auiobiography is now in its tenth thousand.</p>
      </div>
