      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>The fact that at the time of writing the calendar declares it to be May does not prevent us from proclaiming it as almost Christmaq-like so far as the weather is concerned. The dainty muslin frocks and blouses, with which the shop windows are filled seem altogether out of place as we view them wrapped in the furs which according to all precedent should now be reposing in the seclusion of a camphor scented box. Truly the weather authorities have set themselves to provoke womankind to despair in an altogether unnecessary fashion. However, there is nothing left for it but to remain quiescent and unmurmuring, and use the present time for overhauling our wardrobes and transforming many little odds and ends of lace. satin or silk into the hundred and one little adjuncts of dress, such as collars, jabots, ruffles or fronts, which will be useful throughout the longed-for summer time.</p>
        <p>The smart dress sketched is particularly suited for wear all summer. It is made of navy blue cashmere, and is trimmed with pipings of bright red satin. The collar is white covered with ecru lace and the buttons are of blue and white enamel. Red, white and blue are, of course, particularly suited for wear this season, as not only do the colours blend well together or offer a pleasing contrast, but they</p>
        <p>are also suggestive of our patriotic sentiment,. It is also within the power of most people to easily evince their feelings in this manner, for navy blue, whether cashmere, ses,ge, or linen, is always serviceable, and the ton= of white and red is easily introduced. Should the weather in June be warm, white, relieved by red and blue, could be substituted. Indeed one can ring the changes with these colours in innumerable styles at little outlay, and yet keep within the bounds of good taste. A very well-cut navy blue cloth costume I admired the other day was ren- dered exceedingly chic by the addition at seversl points of tiny dull gold buttons. It was worn with -a front of creamy lace and a burnt straw hat of a very simple shape trimmed with shaded red roses. It was very simply made, and yet the impression conveyed ,was particularly pleasing.</p>
        <p>The cyclists, whether male or female, have by now mapped out their future "spins" and the frailer sex are naturally occupied with whA to wear during a tour. .1t is only the</p>
        <p>merest tyro who would ever dream now-a-days of attempting to use thin summery material for her cycling skirt, for experience has tatr,.ht us, what may have escaped our notice, namely that a light material does not bang well, but streams forth in an unsightly fashion at either side of the rider. There is nothing which makes a girl so ridiculous and awkward-looking when awheel as a badly-cut skirt, which declines to hang in smooth folds at either side. There is really little excuse for .the ugly skirt, for a good paper pattern can be bought for a trifle, and by its aid a cycling skirt evolved from last winter's tweed costume, or from quite new material. The costume depicted is of a grey homespun strapped with itself, the little coat having revers covered with white silk opening over a simple blouse made with a transparent lace yoke. The blouse worn could be either of silk or cambric as most suitable, and the hat is distinguished by its neat trimness. An elaborate or large hat should never be worn when cyclin, as it is sure to become insecure, however firmly put on before starting.</p>
        <p>The simple hat illnstrated is suitable for ordinary daily wear. It is made of burnt straw trimmed with Sgured, pink foulard neatly arranged round the crown, and a smart</p>
        <p>bow at the side. It would look well trimmed with any colour. Much consideration is shown at present by fashion for those women who cannot afford to spend much money on their clothes, and yet are filled with the laudable desire of looking as nice as possible. Ready- trimmed hats are, of course, beyond their means, and they have to fall back on the plan of buying a straw hat and trimming, it at home. To their aid come the floral wreaths all arranged, together with a dainty ribboh or lace bow ready to be put on the hat and pinned, or tacked into place. There are countless shapes of hats, both old and new, which are much in vogue this season, for any style almost, if becoming to the wearer, can be worn. Even our old friend, the turban, is once again ea eeidenee, but hardly meets with general acceptance. The severe lines of the high, round brim are trying to. most faces, though when it is becoming the result is altogether admirable. This style of hat is generally trimmed with fruit.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE.</p>
        <p>#</p>
