      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Whether the Queen or the Princess of Wales is the premier Peeress of England is somewhat in doubt, as is also the question whether King Edward VII, belongs to the House of Hanover, or the House of Saxe-Coburg. The almanacs and leading authorities differ on the latter , Point, but if the Queen is not the premier Peers as by virtue of Her Majesty being the Duchess of Lancaster, I this distinction of course belongs to the Princess of I Wales. As Duchess of Rothesay, the Princess is also the premier Peeress of Scotland. After Royalty, the first Peeress of England is the Duchess of Somerset, failing a Duchess of Norfolk, and in Scotland the precedence belongs to the Duchess of Hamilton, and in Ireland to the Duchess of Abercore, there being no Duchess of Leinster. These things are fixed by the laws of heraldry, which determine the order of precedence of all the peers and. peeresses. _ .</p>
        <p>The Prince,and Prineces of Wales fully intend to under- take their share of the Coronation entertaining, and one of the most pleasing features in these arrangements will be the presence of some two or three thousand little children at Marlborough House on Coronation Day. They are to be invited from various philanthropic institutions, and not only will stands be erected for them to see the procession, but dinner will be provided, and each child will receive a Coronation gift in the form of a Coronation mug bearing portraits of the King and Queen and the Prince and Princess of Wales. Not every child can be a Royal guest, but this act of thoughtfulness and generos- ity will do an immense amount of good, if it induces well-to-do people to join in giving similar entertainments to the poor orphan children of their own neighbourhood on the same occasion.</p>
        <p>The feature, on the grand gala night at Covent Garden Opera House, at present arranged for June 30, will be the singing of the Coronation ode by Madame Melba, the treat Australian prima donna. On the arrival of the King and Queen, the Royal party, and the foreign guests, the National Anthem will be sung, after which the new ode will probably follow, the rest of the per- formance consisting of two or three txrerpte from lead- ing operas. The whole of the auditorium will be decor- ated with flowers, whilst the display of jewels will probably exceed anything of the kind ever witnessed oetore.</p>
        <p>The Fanmakets' Company received ite charter of in- csrporation from Queen Anne nearly 200 years ego, and it is therefore most appropriate that it ehould present a beautiful specimen of its art es a Coronation gift to her Majesty. The fan which the Queen his intimated her willingness to accept, will be composed of lace. It is being prepared by Miss Oldroyd, who wee the principal prize winner in the Lace Fan Competition, at the recent exhibition organised by the guild. The fan-stick which will be beautifully jewelled, is also being specially carved by another prize winner at the same Exhibition, and there is no doubt that the fan, when completed, will be one of the most beautiful specimens of art which money and shill can produce. Both large and small fans are being countenanced this season, and the newest are almost heartshaped. Silk or gauze, upen which appear hand-painted scenes of olden times, the portrait of a friend, or the life-sized bead of a favourite animal, are much employed for the mounts,so also is rich and rare lace that needs no further adorn- ment, and what is really remarkable in these days of over elaboration, is spared it. The bouquet fan is a favourite variety among young girls. This is a simple mount of silk, with an edging of silken roses, poppies, lilies-of-the-valley, or any other blooms that may ba preferred. When shut, and hanging on the wrist, it appears as ens beautiful flower. Ostrich feather fans, are distinctly on the wane. Fashionable lingerie is almost amusing this season. _Robes de suit are being built of white, sod very palest coloured silks, quite dOcollete, and with elbow sleeves, or shoulder straps only. Flounces of lace surround the skirt, which boasts a train of considerable length. Underskirts are more elaborate end costly than ever, and the same may be said of petticoat bodices which boast much in the way of lace and embroidery. The present idea is, to allow, as far as possible, one colour only to pervade all one's underwear, also might wear, knots of ribbon of the same colour, appearing on the embroidered, and lace trimmed pillow ape, the guipure bed slips being lined with silk of the same shade. American women who buy expensive laces and furs, in Europe, have been known to take rece,pts for less than their value, so as to be on the tight side, if the customs cfficials want to charge them too much duty en their purchases when they go back home. Others on the other hand, take a delight in raying a record duty on their personal effects, much the same, asour tea and tobecco merchants sometimes advertise the very largest payment of duty to the customs. The distinction of having paid the record duty on foreign made dresses belongs at present to Mrs fluntington, who recently en- riched her country to the extent of £6,360 in this way, but no doubt it will not be long before another American woman beats her with a payment of 010,000. Painted net is taking the place very much of the painted chiffons and muslins, in which the wealthy woman has revelled during the past two summers. Un- eouttedly the net has several distinct advantages over the chiffon and muslin. In the first place it wears much better, again it is more transparent, and there- fore—if properly handled—more effective, and again, having more substance, it hangs better. For our fashions nowadays we go to the stage, and, judging by the popularity there of the painted net gown, it will enjoy a wide vogue during the summer reason. The horrors which some of the people of Martinique must have suffered are too awful to contemplate, and no more terrible position can be imagined than that of the women and children who sought refuge on a bileand became surrounded by a rising sea of molten lava. The sufferings of the peeple of St. Vincent and Martinique must have been horrible, and they have caused alarm in those districts in Europe whieh bear evidence of former volcanic activity. There bas been a scare in Upper Auvergne in Central France, where, as both in Great Britain and Ireland, there have been active volcanoes thousands of years ago. There is no record of any eruption in this country, but there are the remains of vast lava streams in Scotland, and even in recent years the North of Great Britain has been susceptible to earthquakes. An opportunity was afforded to the King of Scamn of creating a favorable impression in the minds of all civilised peoples, by prohibiting bull fights on the °ces- sion of the festivities which attended his coming of age. That he did not do so is co be regretted, as moat peeple will say who have read the descriptions which have been nubliched of the encounters which took place in Madrid. It is said that "Cervantes laughed at Spain's chivalry but if that is so it will not be restored by such exhibi- tions as that which teems to have delighted the Spanieb ladies who were present, but must have filled the specta- tors from other countries with disgust. Graham Travers' " The Way of Escape" (William Blackwood) is not what one could call light reading. On the contrary it is a book which nails for much thought and speculation, and the impression it leaves ell the mind is, that it contains • story distinctly our of the common. With the career of Vera Carruthers, one quickly becomes interested and this interest con- tinues and crows until the final page is reached. Altogether "The Way of Escape "is a capital novel,and worthy of the author of " Mona Maclean."</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>TANGLZD ARGUMENT.</head>
        <p>A TANGLZD ARGUMENT.</p>
        <p>The opposite of a good thing is not necessarily a bad thing, neither is there any reascn why the reverse of that which is bad should be geol. To defer our duties, to put off till to-morrow that which should be done to- day, is ucquestionably bad; and it would therefore seem that to do to-morrow's work to-day, and .so get in ad- vance of one's dutimmust be correspondingly beneficial. But the argument won't work. Take the case of the drunliard who reprimanded his friend for his sobriety Quoth the bibulous one : " You lazily defer drinking until ycu era thirsty, whereas I drink in order to prevent myself becoming so." Superficially considered this sentence seems to proclaim industry, but the policy it revra!s invites criticism. But though duties cannot alwa; abe advan- tageously forestalled, calamity may,and very frequently iv, averted. To cure an ill is good, to prevent it is better. The most healthful among us are by no means those vihom Nature has endowed with the stronvest constitutions ; on the contrary, they consist for the most part of the folks who know how to take care of themselves, a wisdom which the robust frequently lack. Being well provided with a good thing, they are com- monly lavish of it. Wiser in her day and genera- tion is Mrs Mary Price, of Weetley. Stapleton, near Shrewsbury. Mrs. Price has tot suffered from any really serious illness, but was some time ago well on the road to do so. n Often," said Mrs. Price, on January, 1902, "I have had bad attacks of indigestion —the usual discomfort and fulness after food, ((Vowed by cutting pains in the chest and between the shoulders. At other times I have experienced a depressed, dull, worn-out feeling. and have been at a IrBS how to account for it. High-class professional advice, as well as numerons medicines which I tried, availed me nothing, ard it was not until I discovered Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup that I obtained any lasting benefit. Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup cured me, and since that event I have taken the precaution never to be without a bottle of it in the house. Should Ist 'any time feel in the slightest degree indisposed, or suspect a return of the old ssmptoms, a dose or two of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup raver fails to set me up again. It in a grand medicine, and I would like everybody to know whit it has done for me. - . . -</p>
        <p>Fact. are stubborn things in argument and net to be made ac.ay with, juggle with them how we may. It would be hard to find a fact that could illustrats tosttfr than the foregoing tie wisdom of taking th;ngs in hand betimes,particularly where bealth is concerned.</p>
        <p>CC MMERCIAL FAIL7IIF-ST—ACCOrding tr) "Knps Me, nantile Gazette," the number of New Bar kruptcy prr.. ccedings in Enolazd and Wale* gazettrd during the week ending May 24th, was 70. The nuniber in the correeronding week of last year was 88. showing a decrease c f 18, bEing a net &amp;cum) in 1902, to date, of 105.</p>
