      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EALL RIGHTS itZSERVEM.I</head>
        <p>EALL RIGHTS itZSERVEM.I</p>
        <p>AN OBSTINATE MAN,</p>
        <p>CLAUDE TYERS.</p>
        <p>• PART L</p>
        <p>It is a pity that actual events are not allowed— a,pparently the gods forbid—to work themselves up to an artistic climax by the exciting and appropriate steps favoured among writers of fiction. The present narrative is no exception to this rule, and my pen falters before the sea of commonplace detail through which it must wade to that ghastly form which lay in the gully among sodden leaves and yellow grow- ing fungi. Yet to understand the inwardness of the position that arose, one must hear from the beginning, and the beginning carries you to a fine morning in Nov- ember, about ten o'clock by time of day, when an old-fashioned country trap with two people was rolling down the road to 'Westford Farm. The old black mare needed no second intimation to pull up at the stone stile fronting the house, in fact fifty yards away she had etanged her usual clicking jog-trot to something like alevel stride preparatory, as her owner informed Xs friend, lir_ Short, "to coming in with style." "Hubo there !" shouted Ben Wilson, throwing the" reins on her back,. "Hubo there, Farmer Adams, all in bed, or gone to a funeral !" The house door opened, and a large robust man, with country air and country appetite stamped well on him greeted them heartily. "Ha! there you are Ben! Glad to see you, Mr. Short. My old mawther's getting a bit deaf, more's the pity, but she said there's Ben Wilson when we heard that hello: no mistaking your gentle pipe, Ben, my boy." " Wouldn't think I was consumptive to hear me, though people do say I look it?" replied Ben, laugh- ing till the tears coursed &amp;VII his red fleshy cheeks. "Of course, you'll come in and see mawther, be- sides taking a drop to warm you before we start ?" inquired Farmer Adams. The two visitors were standing near the stile by now; the tallest, Mr. Short, with a gun under his arm, for old Ben Wilson had come down by invita- tion to Westford Farm for a day's shooting, bring- ing Mr. Short with him. "Come in! I should think so and my friend Short won't take long to think about it," answered Ben Wilson, this pun on his friend's name, albeit of a very mild character, causing his fat sides to shake and the back of his neck to grow crimson as he led the way. . Ben 'Wilson was a hay and corn merchant; the most jovial man in the county his friends said, and their name was legion, always with a joke on his lips, to be suffered with as a price for his friendship, though its butt was as often as not himself. On the other side the hay merchant, it must be admitted, was hot-tempered, and—to use his own frankly en. pressed opinion—" as obstinate as a mule, sir." Mr. Short was a very tall gentleman, of a dis- tinctly town tallness as opposed to country tallnesS, where long limbs are generally evidence of greater activity and wiry strength than the average. Though he constantly drove about the country with the hay merchant, and took a more enthusiastic interest in rural topics than the natives themselves, he still re- mained an unmistakeable town mouse, in spite of his endeavours to assume the country cousin. Yet with this same amateurishness, he possessed a certain un- obtrusive, clear-headedness where business or such. matters were concerned, to which the hay merchant himself was not a little indebted at times. The chief bond, however, Which united them, lay in the stoutness of Mr. Short's belief in the old black mare, resultant it is to be feared, from a natural guilelessness which allowed him to accept Ben Wilson's 'toughest yarns of her without a tremonr. Some such credence was essential to remaining even on speaking terms with Ben Wilson. "Ton see that old mare, sir," he would say to a new acquaintance. "Don't seem much to look at do she ?" This was a critical moment for the new acquaint- aace ; if he delivered himself too frazikly, or showed signs of levity, the crusty side of the hay merchant at once betrayed itself and he waddled off mumbling. If on the other hand the man replied warily that the old mare might have been a ' good 'un ' once, or that she showed plenty of bone (which none could deny) his position was henceforth secure. Reverting once more to the two guests and their host, they found that old Mrs. Adams had already an- ticipated their desire for hot and invigorating bever- age, which steamed ready as they enfered. Between her and the hay merchant there was much cordiality; to her he was still, as her own son, a tender stripling, with whom it was well to joke but not to take too seriously; sometimes indeed she spoke of him as "child," which Ben would affirm with a wink was pretty hard lines on a man nearer fifty than anything else, and weighing fifteen stone or thereabouts. "Adams and me ain't to be glowed to grow up. Dlawther won't let as," the hay merchant often greeted plaintively, upon which " Illawther "shook her head, and anskvered when they reached her ago they'd give over wishing to be grown up. "Now, gentlemen, are we ready r asked the farmer slipping on a game bag. "Not quite Adams, Short is all right but I am short of a gun," he stopped to chuckle, "trigger broke off mine as I was cleaning" it yesterday, but you've got one to lend me I know.' "By all that's unlucky," answered the farmer, thumping the table, "I lent my second gun to Wat- kins of the Grange last week and he hasn't sent it back yet." "Well, you can send over to Watkins for it. And I'll tell you how we can manage so as not to lose any time ; let the fellow you send over to Watkins take the gun direct tolled Ash. Then you and Short, wit&amp; the two guns we have, walk through the to field to Red Ash, you may get a shot on the way, and I'll jog down Gipsies' Lane with the trap and meet you there." The Grange Farm Watkin's place, was about two miles away; Red the extremity of Farmer Ad's land lay in the same direction and was about an Mimi distance from either point. The only means' of getting to Red Ash on wheels was by Gipsies' Lane, so called from being a favourite haunt of the wandering tribe. It was next to impassable for a vehicle on account of its unkempt trailing hedges, and in heavy rains it became the bed of a lively terrent whose rushing waters scooped it into holes and banks. "Gipsies' Lane !" laughed the farmer. " should like to see you drive the old mare down it." Ben Wilson, it has been said, was an obstinate man, "Leavetendertha tto spot.ra e and this doubt as to his old mare's powers touched h a farmer, th' anld mare and nlo have pulled through worser places nor that, on dark nights too." . "You'll upset as sure as your name's Ben Wilson, the dip across the half way gully is in a fearful state." "Shan't go through, my lad," answered Ben in triumph, "I shall slip out into the field by the gate before it, and (*me into the lane again by the next." "N 9 don't do that, Ben," said the farmer, his bantering tone subsiding, "the lane may be bad but the field is worse, and besides that is Tom Reynold's land up there." The hay merchant grew crimson as was his habit under any stress of feeling, and he snorted, "Tout Reynolds ! Who cares for Tom Reynolds! won't eat me I &amp;pose if I do cross a few yards of his land, he'd find me a big mouthful if he tricd, I can tell you." "Well, go your own way, but for heaven's sake speak him civil if you do meet; yours is a devilish queer temper, you lmow, Ben." "Ben Wilson is a blamed fool often enough," as- sented the same individual with perfect cordiality, "and no one knows it better than himself. All the same I'll gO down Gipsie's Lane, partly not to waste a morning's sport, and partly to show you it can be done." He was in high good humour at having gained his end. They now sallied out, Farmer Adams going to call some one for his errand to the Grange. "Hi there, Bill! Bill Goose I want ye," ho shouted in a voice that rivalled Ben Wilson's. "Bill Goose?" interrogated Mr. Short, "a most peculiar name." "Quite a well known character," replied Ben with much amusement, "and, by the bye, he's, one of the ' short' family too—he—" his merriment en- tered the silent choking stage. "I suppose you mean he is a very small man?" queried Mr. Short, who was accustomed to his friend's humour. "No, he's taller than you are, and shorter," he gasped. At this moment fortunately Bill Goose him seli made his appearance in the doorway of a shed, a tall powerfully built man, in the ordinary garb of a country labourer; but alas, one glance it his face sufficed to reveal the "shortness" the hay merchant had alluded to. It was not a bad nor a very ugly face, its main characteristic: being an air of exag- gerated cheerfulness; indeed some people questioned whether Bill Goose, with his boisterous ready laugh at every time and every thing, was not better off than folks who had the sense to know when they ought to feel miserable</p>
        <p>A .</p>
        <p>"Morning, Bill, how's things down your part to- day ?" roared Ben to him. "Ha ha, I know you, Ben Wilson, ha ha !" Bi— Goose replied, throwing up his hands in a moc salute the village urchins had taught him. He had his friends and foes like other people, whom he re- cognised more by instinct than by reason. The hay merchant was one of the most favoured of the former</p>
        <p>" How is little dog' to-day, Bill Goose ?" In an instant Bill Goose's joy changed to intense grief. Bo—oo," he wailed loudly, " Bo—oo, 'little dox ' left poor Bill, left poor Bill." The farmer had rejoined them on hearing their voices. " Ay, 4 little dog 'is missing these two days, it's a great loss to poor Bill." In a lower voice he added, "I shouldn't be surprised if some one had shot her, she was a poaching little vixen." " Well, it's a confounded shame whoever done it, to shoot the poor fellow's dog that he made so much of;" said Ben in hot indignation. It was a strange sight to see this stalwart fellow with his knuckles in his eyes, bo-ooing to make the farm sheds re-echo, all in behalf of the absence of a small mangy cur, " little dog," as he called it, that used to follow him about.</p>
        <p>" Well, cheer up, Bill, 1 want you to go an er- rand," said his master good-naturedly, though winking at the other genitleznen. Bill Goose cheered up as requested immediately, and emitted a powerful ha ha of joy at the news. Going an errand was as- sociated in his mind with invitations to sit down in lofty kitchens, where people passed the time of day with him in a more or less witty fashion, a foaming pot of ale forming not the most unpleasant comple- ment of the ceremony. He had therefore no hesita- tion in abandoning his recent grief, in such respects Bill was in no degree behind other people. So his duties were explained to him clearly, and he was sent off with strict injunction not to loiter under pain of having his head cut off by Ben Wilson on return. After a while Bon Wilson himself scrambled up be- hind the old mare, Farmer Adams reminding him if he broke both shafts or his own neck to be sure to "sing out." To which Ben responded by waving his whip without looking back and lustily treating them to a few lines from that ancient ballad, The Bermer's Boy, as he jogged along up the road.</p>
        <p>Mr. Short and his host, gun under arm, turned into the fields through which they had determined to make their way to Red Ash, on a stray chance of "dropping across something" as the farmer said. It was not surprising that the hay merchant ha insisted on driving as far as he could. If you came across old Ben sampling a hay mow quarter of a mile from home, you had only to look into the lane and there was the old black mare switching her tail by a gate-post , if you caught him in his town office, and glanced through a side window littered with con-samples, there backed under a shed stood the old mare and trap ready. The apparent danger his friend was to run before they re-met at Red Ash, disturbed Mr. Short not a little. He broached it to Adams who replied : "Ben is pig-headed, though I'm his friend as says it ; but I was more chaffing than serious just now. There's not much danger if he does upset, with that old mare of his."</p>
        <p>"Still the Tom Reynolds you mentioned, is our friend on such bad terms with him ?" persisted Mr. Short.</p>
        <p>Adams threw back his head thoroughly to enjoy a laugh. "It's a regular family affair. Ben's mother and Tom Reynolds' mother used to live near one an- other before they were married, but by some chance they never met afterwards, at least not for fifty years or so. Then, I don't know how it started, but they began to be always talking how nice it would be to see each other, and have a chat on old times and the like, Well, Ben and Tom were good friends then, so they arranged it all tidy for the two old people, both of them passed seventy mind, and one day Ben drove his mother to visit Tom's mother. She was to stop a week, and have a real good time. Well when Ben came to fetch her at the end of this week, he found old Mrs. Reynolds and his mother had not spoken to each other after the first day. They quarrelled first, they do say, about the price of an old plum coloured frock, whilst Mrs. Reynolds was showing her things to her visitor. After this Mrs. Reynolds, who was about fifteen months the younger, said that for a woman of her age Eliza Wilson did not set her mind enough on chapel affairs, so they quarrelled over this again. Them old Mrs. Wilson said it was a pity that Tom had been such a sickly baby, her Ben had never a day's illness in his life. These were the last words they exchanged, till Ben came five days later. Ben's mother, poor old soul, has been gone these three years now, but the family have taken it up on both sides; the last time Ben and Tom Reynoldi met they nigh came to fisticuffs. So there's just a chance, if Tom Rey- nolds came on our friend Ben driving across his land and didn't get an over civil answer, they might be at it again." "What kind of a man is he ?" asked Mr. Short.</p>
        <p>"Oh, not bad natured, stroke him right way, got fiery red hair, and a temper to match, soon cools though pretty much the same as Ben himself." Thus talking they xeached Red Ash, without hav- ing had the luck to "drop across" anything as the farmer had hoped. An exception ought perhaps to be made in Mr. Short's case, who did actually stumble over a smallish brown object in a tall clump of coarse grass the same developing into a fine hare. Mr. Short's astonishment did not, however, allow him to fire before the hare, such was its length of limb, had disappeared from sight. Contrary to their expectations, for they had stepped DUG not to keep him waitin6, Ben Wilson was not at lied Ash. They waited five, ten, fifteen minutes, and then beginning to feel really anxious, started down the lane to meet him. They had not gone far before the "click, clieketty- • click " of the old mare as she ambled towards them caug/xt their ears. Ben," said the farmer after running his eye over him, half in jest yet with firm conviction, "you've had a tussle with Tom Reynolds, blessed if you haven't." "I've had no such thing," answered the hay merchant hotly. "Now, Ben, the whole truth and nothing but the tmth, my lad • you see what comes of taking your own way, whether or no, and going through the fields; if you'd kept to the }lines it wouldn't ha' happened," pressed the farmer in his jovial fashion. "I tell you I never met him, and how do you know I didn't keep to the lane." ' "Well, since you stuck to the lane I suppose I shall have to bear the blame of everything,". Adams answered quite satisfied at the hay merchant's words that his first supposition wag wrong. "Take a drop from my flask," said Mr. Short, " you look as if you want it" Ben Wilson accepted eagerly, it was evident some incident of his drive had considerably *disturbed him, his ruddy cheeks were quite pale, his clothes a Utile disordered, he had lost for the time the continuous stream of drollery that distinguished him. All this was as plain as a pike staff to his two companions who, nevertheless, had nothing to satisfy their curi- osity but the knowledge that in an hour or so, pro- bably this fit of silence would make way for hia usual good humour, and the explanation would be given in a roar of merriment, even though it told against himself.</p>
        <p>As Bill Goose had not yet arrived with the other gan, (Watkins might have been out or. his farm and have it with him, they decided to melee their way home for an early dinner,) by which time Bill would certainly have returned. They chose a line back about parallel to Gipsies' Lane. "Suppose you'll drive home again the way you came, Ben ?" said the farmer maliciously, and with a slight movement of his left eye to Mr. Short. But Ben only stared, gloomy and silent) They went in this order: along the top hedge, making his way from field to field, the hay mer- chant in his trap, lower down strode the firmer, next to him Mr. Short. About two hundred yards below the latter gentleman ran Gipsies' Lane. Mr. Short had a habit of handling his gun as if he did i know what to do with t, Adams carried his with the calm unconstrained air of a man to whom it was as natural as a pike handle; if ho did betray any uneasiness, it was when the restless barrel of Mr. Short's weapon swept round apparently in search of prey. Not a great distance had been traversed before from tinder Mr. Short's feet a covey of partridge arose with the tremendous whirr of a score of vigor- ous wings; but the sportsman, startled by the sudden noise, almost dropped his gun, and when he thought ofpring the game was skimming a distant hedge. Mr. Short was confused, the farmer laughed and roared out. "Frightened you, eh 1." adding cheer- fully, "never mind, soon get used to it." But not even a chuckle was beard from the direction of the old mare and trap. Mr. Short's luck was not yet exhausted; for a seetmd time up got the birds, in this instance they were not close enough to startle him into absolute passivity, so bringing gun to shoulder with a spas- modic jerk, he let fly both barrels straight in front of He was next aware of Farmer Adams shouting, •• Bravo, Mr. Short, you've downed one, but I'w afraid he's a runner." -</p>
        <p>Mr Short did not on the instant grasp what he meant by a "runner," but glancing down the field he saw the four dogs in frantic pursuit of a bird that fluttered and dodged with what speed it could to the cover of the lane hedge. Suddenly lit dropped to the around. there was a snapping nfiaws and whisk,.</p>
        <p>ing of tans, th-lui Sirott—a-ppEttred trafing back, Ills head and stump of a tail carried very high, and something brown in his mouth, the others got in his way admiringly. One dog alone still remained down there, an old collie almost past work, the farmer called him without effect.</p>
        <p>"Why drat the old fool," bellowed Adams and though the words were hard the voice was kind enough, "why couldn't he stop at home, he's lost his teeth, and sight, and smell, and now he don't know when he's called. Here Prince! Prince why, hang the dog, he's going into the old lane whether I tell him or not. I never knew him do such a thing La his life before," said his master aghast. Prince had in fact entered the lane just by the half .way gully that Ben Wilson had threatened to avoid by going through the fields on the opposite side.</p>
        <p>The farmer was too astonished to say anything; the. hay merchant had his silent fit; Mr. Short waited for the other two, so for a minute or so the three stood in silent expectation. It was about mid-day remember, with the sun at its highest, and the young dogs frisking about them yet from the silence there welled a sound that chilled each one like an icy draught sweeping through the sunlit air,—a doleful howl from old Prince.</p>
        <p>" How—on—oo," he wailed like some dogs do of a moonlit night, again and again, but now in the day it sounded infinitely more weird. " Confound, the old brute, I believe he's doing it to frighten us," the farmer broke out angrily. "Stop where you are Ben, we'll go and see what the matter is."</p>
        <p>Mr. Short and he made hastily down the field and pushed into the lane. Ben -Wilson eat in his trap like a stone image. "He's in the s,rully on the other side, pull yourself up here, Mr. Short.' The ground there slanted steeply down and up again, still they could not see the dog though he con- tinued his bowling. " The old fool must be in the bottom ditch, a sheep or something has tumbled in." He was yet seemingly irritated against Prince as the. agent of this nonsense, but his face had grown very pale. It struck him unpleasantly that Prince had found many eheep in his time, but never spoken like this before. The ditch referred to was a damp hole about four feet deep, water enough drained into it to keep a crop of fuogi growing around, drifts of leaves and ratting branches were swept there from both slopes of the valley, a mass of brambles clothed the sides. Here the old dog stood baying with uplifted throat. Yes, Prince had found something more than a sheep. They saw that the frost-whitened tufts of grass at the brink were dabbled with blood, and then, peering anxiously in, discerned the form of a man half concealed by the brambles, the head bent back, care arm twisted formlessly under him, just as he might have fallen or been thrown down. They lifted him oat, a great wound above the right tem- ple evidently from one crashing blow, told in itself a whole history. . _ . .</p>
        <p>Good God'!" said Farmer Adams as tbey gazed at the dead man's face, "it is Tom Reynolds</p>
        <p>(To be Concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>"FIGHTING EDITOR" AND THE "DUMMY EDITOR."</head>
        <p>THE "FIGHTING EDITOR" AND THE "DUMMY EDITOR."</p>
        <p>The "fighting editor" and the "dummy editor" were not always things of fiction even in this country. In the old days, when the Press censorship in England was rigorous to the last degree, the printer or publisher, whose name was given in each newspaper, filled the part of the "dummy editor." When the real editor transgressed the laws regulating political discussion in the Press, and rendered himself liable to prosecution, the Government pounced upon the printer or publisher whose name appeared in the newspaper. If a fine were imposed the editor or proprietor, of course, paid it. But often the printer or publisher was seat to gaol and endured vicarious imprison- ment, and during his sequestration the name of another " dummy was substituted for his in the newspaper. The most famous " fighting editor" in the annals of British journalism was the Rev Henry Bate, or " Parson Bate," of the Tory " Morning Post," who subsEquently founded the Whiz " Morning Herald." Bate was as expert with the pistol as with the pen ; and his motto as a journalist was "I never apologise. and I never retract." During his control of the " Morning Post," towards the end of the eighteenth century,loe published a couple of personal paragraphs concerning a lady. Captain Stoney, a buck of the time, constituted himself the lady's champion, and called upon Bate to give him satiafactioa. Bate ignored the challenge. One day, however, the two men met accidentally at the Adelptii Tavern in the Strand, and the Captain made it clear to the editor that the only alternative to a duel was a I horse-whipping. Bata sald,'l'm for pistols and swords." The two men ordered a room, into which they locked I themselves, and blazed ,away at each other with pistols but without any very damaging effect. They then drew I their swords and continued to fight till the doer was broken °pun by the police.—" Cornhill Maz3zlne."</p>
      </div>
