      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>How to make it Easy.</head>
        <p>How to make it Easy.</p>
        <p>" Women who find housework ta worry " (writes " Elise," in one of her " some " articles) " should not treat it as a necessary evil but try to fit themrelves to do it to per- fection, it is easy then instead of irksome." A practical woman,whose life has much in it to encourage wives and mothers, says the same in her capable, pleasant way in a Liverpool paper. Housework used to be a terrible trial. " I was often taken," she says," with pains in my stomach and across my bady. I had to go about the kitchen stooping—l couldn't stand up straight, and it is only through taking Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people that I am strong and well now. My doctor ordered me to bed, for the disease was ulceration of the costs of the stomach. I could not keep any food down, and I was so weak throuela lack of nourishment that I couldn't lift my hand to my mouth. The pain I suffeied cannot be described_ I bad a cupboard full of medicine bottles. Drugs eased me for a time : soon I wRs as bad as ever. The</p>
        <p>disease, called in medical language gastric ulcer, arises out ef neglected indigestion. Every time I ate anything I suffered intense pain until it came up, and then I would be as hungry as ever. But in time even hunger deserted me ; I became weak and ill through lack of sufficient nourishment, and I couldn't do my work for three years. It was perfectly miser- able.</p>
        <p>6:!e</p>
        <p>_ "Bow did it stop ? I'll tell you. Six months ago, I read in the paper about a cure by Dr. Williams' pick pills for pale oeople. The parson bad the same ailment as myself. I got a box, and after four pills I found grPat relief. I bad altogether seem boxes, but the fast box is only half empty. Dr Williams' pink pills acted In a marvellous way. They healed the ulcerated stomach, which formerly rejected all food, and I could take a stronger diet without suffering. The pills gave me a good appetite. I can now eat my ordinary food and enjoy it without pain, and lam as well as ever I was in my life."</p>
        <p>The speaker was Mrs Webster, of 508, Edge-lane, Liverpool, and her cure is one of these where Dr Williams' pink pills, by enabling the stomach to absorb and use the food, remove the cause of indigestion and increase many-fold the value of the food taken. Hence the impoverished blood grows rich and healthy, the tired back and nerves are strengthened and braced, and the weak, tired woman becomes strong and well. The pills are as valuable to men as women ; they have cured paralysis, lecomotor atm', rheumatism, kidney disease, sciatica, impoverished blood, scrofula, rickets, consump- tion of the bowels and lungs, arornia, pale and sallow complexion, weakness, palpitation, pains in the back, early decay, female weakness, and hysteric. They are genuine only with the full name—Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people—and are sold by chemists, and by Dr Williams' medicine company, Holborn-viaduct. London, at two shillings and ninepence a box, or six boxes for thirteen shillings; and binepence ; these pills are a tonic, not a purgative.</p>
