      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PARR'S BANK. LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK. LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Another Amalgamation.</p>
        <p>Mr Cecil F Parr presided at an extraordinary general meeting of Parr's Bank, Limited. held at Cannon-street Hotel, London, on Thursday, and moved the following resolutions 1—(1) " That this meeting deems it desirable to amalgamate the business of name's Leicestershire Banking Company, Limited, with the business of Parr's Bank, Limited, and that the direeters of Pair's Bank. Limited, be and are hereby authorised to enter into an agreement with the liquidators of Pares's Leicestershire Banking Company, Limited, on the terms of the draft proposal submitted to this meeting, and to cairy the same into effect with such (if any) modifications as the directors think expedient," (2) " That the capital of the company shall be increaied to £8,542,500 by the creation of 12,250 new shares of £lOO teach, and that such new shares he allotted in accordance with the said agreement for tuna gamation. The Chairman said the directors bad good reason for believing that such amalgamation would prove as advantageous to the parent as t the child. In 'ts con- ception Pares's Bank was very like their own. It was begun more than a century age as a private bank, with one or two partners. Then it developed just as their own bank had done into a limited company, which earned for itielf the same good reputation in Leicester- shire as he trusted Parr's Bank had earned in South Lancashire and Cheshire. Under recent legislation it , had now arrived at a position when it must be an eater lor Itself eaten. Parr's Bank was the eater, and they I had come to the conclusion that they were absorbing a I very good business which bad Peen wry prudently con- ducted, and had a good substantial reserve. Their lonoko showed that a profit was &amp;snared as far as any bank profit could be assured,over and above the amount they would be paid in dividsnd on the 12,250 shares allotted to them. He enlarged on the connecting link which the bank wou d prove to their Birmingham and I London business.</p>
        <p>Mr E W Nix -seconded the revolution, which was adopted,and a further resolution was also passed raisin the capital of Parr's Bank to £8,512,500 by creation of 12,250 now shares of £lOO each.</p>
        <p>Assault on a Girl at Lymm. At a special Court held at Lymm, on Saturday, before Mr W P Norris (in the chair), Dr Brabann, and the Hon J E Cross, Walter Daniels, of Heatley, was charged with assaulting Lizzie Leather at fleatley on the 19th inst. Mr T S Steel,of R erring- „. ton, appeared for the defendant—After hearing evidence, the defendant's solicitor admitted that the defendant had put his arm round complain- ant's waist, but nothing more. That of course was a technical assault. He asked the magistrates to reduce the (Marge to a common assault, and to deal with it sumniarily. He produced letters of good character from the Rector of Oughtrington, from the schoolmaster, and from the previous employ,or of the defendant—The magistrates, after retiring. said they bad decided to reduce the charge to one of common assault, and fined the defendant .£3.</p>
