      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED,</p>
        <p>Each Cabinet Council is now looked upon with redoubled interest, as, though up to the time of writing no definite news is to hand, it is now anticipated that almost any moment the welcome tidings of peace may be proclaimed to the public, who are awaiting it with the utmost eagerness. Still, as Mr. Chamberlain impressed upon his audience at Birmingham, there is on the other hand no absolute certainty of it, and equally easily we may all be disillusioned by some hitch in the negotiations which may throw us back into a state of active war. One thing is sure, and that is that in certain matters and upon certain points, the Government intend to be absolutely firm at</p>
        <p>'THE PEACE PROSPECTS: THE BUILDING WHERE THE BOER PEACE CONFERENCE WAS HELD AT VEREENIGING.</p>
        <p>all costs, preferring a continuance of this already spun-out war to any possibility of a recrudescence. On the other side, however, so far as we can judge from what has filtered through a rigorous censorship, it appears that the majority of the Boer leaders have shown themselves sensible of their position and amenable to reason, recognising the inevitable, which means loss of independence, if not such a totally unconditional surrender as was at one time thought. It is the Orange Free State (now Orange River Colony) leaders, who, unwilling to fight at first, are inclined to stand out for the freedom of their country, but they, too, must in time, if not at once, realise that they are waging a hopeless war. 3lr. Steyn, from all accounts, is himself a broken man and little better than an invalid in health, and, though we must all recognise the loyalty and stubborness of his persistence, it can only be con- aemned if carried further in the teeth of the majority of hi i colleagues. All certainly did not go quite smoothly at Vereeniging, but how far there were dissensions we are in ignorance. Gen. De Wet, though reported a leading irreconcilable • by many of the correspondents, appears to have made a most favourable impression upon the • British officers at Pretoria, who found him as pleasant in peace as unpleasant in war, and his • opinion is sure to carry immense weight, into whichever scale it is thrown. But undoubtedly peace is in the air and more probable than at any point since the outbreak of hostilities, and it is considered in the bect informed circles that this time it will really come to something.</p>
        <p>Hardly since the forged Parnell letter has that most austere and respectable of periodicals, the limes, whose opinion carries so much weight abroad as well as at home, treated Europe in general and England in particular to such a sensation as that contained in the pages of the second volume of its History of the War, published by Messrs. Sampson Low and written by Mr. Amery. Of this gentleman it may at once be said that, whether right or wrong in his striking statements, he has shown himself most fearless in using information which has reached him, with the result that his book has evoked a perfect storm of favourable and adverse criticism, as no room is left for a middle course. In itself it is Written in a calm and critical style, and carries on the history down to the battle of Colenso, at Which Colonel Long's battery was sacrificed. This,</p>
        <p>GENERAL SIR REDVERS DULLER, V.C.</p>
        <p>EVER= CRITICISED IN THE "TIMES HISTORY OF TIM WAR.</p>
        <p>for instance, is made the occasion of one of many attacks upon General Sir Redvers Buller, who, it is argued, ought under the circumstances to -have doubled the casualties of that fatal day rather than have abandoned the guns, as at the moment they were the greatest necessity of the army in South Africa, and could not be dispensed with whatever the cost. Other very trenchant criti- cisms of the original Commander-in-Chief in South Africa hark back upon thc question of the Ladysmith heliogram, of which the substance is Published. It is definitely stated that General Buller •did order Sir George White to surrender the town and only persevered in response to an Ultimatum by cable from the ilarquess of Lans- downe, then the Secretary of State for War. What the General's retort to this will be is at Present uncertain, but it seems hardly likely that things can rest where they are, and, unless the War Office relax the obligations of silence ‘11)011 Sir Redvers Buller, the attack must appear a trifle unfair. Other officers come under tm. "slash, and the early battles in Natal "r.dit _of nobody and the discredit of many. Altogether, this second volume is one of great interest and no little importance, while it is daring in the extreme.</p>
        <p>Already American geologias are on the scene of the terrible catastrophes in the West Indies, and English and French scientists will join then; shortly, when the causes and effects of the recent eruptions will be investigated with a view to deciding whether the stricken islands shall be abandoned or not. Meanwhile both Mont Pelee in Martinique and La Soufriere in St. Vincent continue in a state of more or less violent activity, and the</p>
        <p>---_</p>
        <p>)_</p>
        <p>n</p>
        <p>FORT DE FRANCE</p>
        <p>111 E CAPITAL OF 3IARTIN; (WE, WHICH DESTRUCTION EEOM THE CONTINUE] MONT PELEE.</p>
        <p>IS IN DANGER OF D ACTIVITY OF</p>
        <p>- - -- terrified survivors en the islands are living in Perpetual fear of an awful death. At Fort de fjalice especially, the consternation is indescribable, though it does not require a very vivid imagination to picture it. At times since St. Pierre was overwhelmed, the colossal columns of fiery matter ejected by the volcano have threatened , rt de France, where most of the inhabi- t...auts of Martinique have fled for safety, to uchan extent that the already qverwrought 'People have been in a perfect frenzy of panic.</p>
