      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>soldiers and police, men and women alike, have filled the streets, weeping, praying and rush- ing about distracted, while overhead the awe- inspiring volcanic clouds rolled relentlessly, raining stones still hot amid a swirl of ashes. Small wonder that the residentos of Fort de France and the thousands of rqiugees who have flocked into the city, are anxious to flee from the place, but the question for the scientists to decide is whether, once they have emigrated, it will be safe for them to return when Mont Pelee is again quiescent. This also applies to St. Vincent, where only one thought pervades the minds of the terror-stricken inhabitants—to get away as speedily as possible—a desire with which we can well understand and sympathise with. And, what is more, we can all assist tEem to attain this desire by contributing generously to the relief funds that have been opened. Certainly there never was a more deserving object.</p>
        <p>The corrugated iron structure at Hawarden known as St. Deiniors Library, has now been replaced by a handsome Gothic building, erected as a memorial to Mr. Gladstone, and in a very short time it will be the permanent home of the vast array of literature which the late statesman bequeathed to the public. The Library of St. Deiniol's was founded by Mr. Gladstone for the</p>
        <p>"",</p>
        <p>THE GLADSTONE MEMORIAL AT lIAWARDEN :</p>
        <p>THE NEW ST DEINIOL'S LIBRARY</p>
        <p>furtherance of learning, and to this end he gave the whole of hie valuable and extensive collection of books. The institution Inzs been working for some years now, and readers fnm the uttermost parts of the earth have availed themselves of the facilities for study which it affords. The new library occupies a capital situation on a slight rise, and from its grounds many miles of very beautiful country afford a charming prospect. The interior is divided into two large halls, between which the books will be divided under the classification " Divinity " and "Humanity." By the way the Gladstone memorial in the Strand- Holborn scheme will have a very prominent Position, and it is interesting to note that it will stand where a year or more ago it would have exactly blocked the east end of Bookseller's-row. Thus Mr. Gladstone's figure and achievements will be perpetuated on the site of one of his old ,• book-buying haunts.</p>
        <p>A better or truer description of a man was never penned than that written by Professor Moore, of Colombia university, of Lord Paunce- forte, whose death at Washington has just been announced. The Professor said of him: "He has settled many controversies ; but has himself created none. He stands to-day among the cliplo- rhatists of the world as the great practical expon- ent of the principle of international arbitration." This splendid eulogy Lord Pau ncef ote earned at the memorable Peace Conference at the Hague, where he repre- sented Great Britain so ably— services for which his coun- tr y rewarded nim with a peer- age. It is no secret that the Arbitration Code drawn up at the Hague was al- most entirely his handiwork. Lord</p>
        <p>PauncefoWsidea</p>
        <p>TIIE LATE LORD PATINCE BRITISH AMBASSADOR AT WASIILNGION.</p>
        <p>of a diplomat, if one may judge from his methods was that he should be a</p>
        <p>peace keeper or, where occasion required, a peace maker. He himself was both. When he went to Washington nearly thirteen years ago relations between Great Britain and the United States were anything but friendly, and there was no small danger of a rupture. The reconciliation which followed was very largely due to his wonderful tact and brilliant diplomacy, if the terms are not synony- mous. He sucmeded, so far as the Government at Washington is concerned, in dissipating every shadow of suspicion with regard to the policy of this country. He convinced the United States that they had no firmer friend than Great Britain, and he ended by establishing a confidential and trustful co-operation on all foreign questions of the closest kind. Lord Pauncefote's death is a great loss to England, and his post at Washington will be very difficult to fill.</p>
        <p>Verily, in addition to capabilities as an actor, Mr. Beerbohm Tree is a great impressario, and his latest feat is worthy to rank with his greatest. Put to it almost at the last moment before his production of Ihe 3lerry Wires of Windsor—vice Ulysses, deceased—and the illness of Miss Lily Hanbury, he postponed the production for a week, and actually managed to book Mrs. Kendal and Miss Ellen Terry to play the two merry wives, "Mistress Ford and Mistress Page." thereby adding two stars of the first 'magnitude to his already strong caste, regardless of cost and thinking MRS. KENDAL.</p>
        <p>MISS ELLEN TERRY.</p>
        <p>THE TWO GREATEST ENGLISH-SPEAKING ACTRESSES WHO WILL APPEAR AS THE "WIVES" IN "THE MERRY WIVES OF WINDSOR" AT HER MAJESTY'S. only of the reputation of Her Majesty's. To see these two ladies in conjunction should attract half Londondurimngr. rethTeCe wi Coronation be well repaid n wtieme,raenpdid or h t ifstobies hoped that and striking move. Miss Ellen Terry, as Mistress Page," should be especially pleasing, and will have a most charming stage daughter in Mrs. Tree, who is cast for "Sweet Anne Page." Mr. Tree himself will, of course, appear in his favourite r6le of "Falstaff," which,it must be admitted,d wads all the others in this Shakespearean play. This has been onite a notable week of first nights with Madame R6jane at the Imperial in her version of Za:a, which was preceded last Saturday night by Mrs. Lewis Waller's production of her version. Both have caught on well, and some interesting comparisons have been drawn between them. Mr. Terry is again busy at his theatre, superseding /Ify Pretty Maid, a comparative failure, with Lore in Idleness. Mr. Charles Hawtrey, too, having got rid of his failure, flhe President, has started well with .'There and Back, a farce by Mr. George Arliss, the clever actor, which, if not particularly brilliant, is amusing enough, and should do well. Mrs. Dane's Defence is promised very shortly at Wyndham's, and the revival of this great success will undoubtedly be yeryi popular.</p>
