      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>nu EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 30, 1902.</head>
        <p>nu EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 30, 1902.</p>
        <p>LONDON'S MILK SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>"There are old men still living who remember the days when East London, round Limeheuse and Poplar, was open country, before the invasion of Cookneydom, bad substituted the Commercial Road far • Gun lane, (the narrow cart-lane where King George's carriage etuck in the ditch)—the good old days when the whole stretch of land from the ' Pigott Arms 'to the ' Cherry Tree' at Bromley was covered by green ineadow3, shady orchards, and waving cornfi lie. In those days rail-borne milk was undreamt of, and London was self- dependent for its supply. Now, city and country are so closely jo rod by shining bands of steel, that the rugged Cornish farm and the sleepy Cheshire village, and even remoter corners of the United Kmgdom—over 300 miles from the Netropolis—coritribute twice daily to swell the supply of new milk for London. II Nor are we s;Illy dependent on the resources of our OWII is'and ; we are now importing milk from both France and Holland. but none as yet, in the fluid state, from Ireland—the isle of green pastureF. In winter time we receive a considerable quantity of French milk, which is shipped from Cheroourg and landed at Southampton Harbour, en route for Waterloo. At the French factory, before being sear off,' it is first heated to a temperature which destroys dangerous germs,and then serni-frczm to ensure its keeping sweet; and thus it is able to weather rough seas without sour- ing, and to arrive at its London destination in a condi- tion to rival our own country milk ; for the cows of the Normandy farmers produce a quality of milk which will easily pass our standard. _ _ . _ _ .</p>
        <p>" -The fe-cillties for milk transport from the Netherlands are so great that Dutch milk can be sold in London as cheaply as that from Essex. No official record is kept of the quantity of milk which London consumes, but careful investigation has from time to time been made by persons interested in the sub- ject, and from data supplied by railway companies and collected from other sources it has been estimated that the amuunt of milk consumed per head of the popula- tion has doubled in the last sixteen years. The estimated quantity of milk consumed in London per head of the population was : Li 1584-oas-fifth of a pint, or rather over ± 1 worth a day. In 1890 —One-fourth of a pint, or id worth a day. Ira 1901 —Two-fifths of a piat,or over 11 worth a day. Though other large cities requ're a tiii more liberal allowance—in Manchester the average daily quantity consumed being about half-a-pint, in Dublin and N. 3 W York about three-quarters of a pint,and in Bos-mn about 24-25 of a pint—yet that London shows a crowing appreciation of the food-value of milk is sufficiently evident.</p>
        <p>The net increase in the daily quantity of milk brought by rail to London is still mare str;king. In 1884 it was (approximately) 88 603 gallons a day : in 1900. 254.925 gallons a day. Paddingtoo Siation alone, which is the largest ' milk terminus ' in London, receives on an average 45,000 gallons a day. Tae arrival platforms are a remsrkable sight there at the height of the mid- night bustle. There is milk everywhere! The very pavement is slippery with millings of the opalescent fluid. Porters, contractors' agents, and oarters crowd and jostle one another amid a general burly-burly of trend hey, heaving, banging of churns and clattering of lids, mingling with the swish of milk as iris baled horn can to can. Such a bewildering scene of noise and movement is the nightly prelude to the advent of the morning milk-boy. From east and west, notels and south, comes the daily flaw of milk into London, to be dispersed through wide channels into thousands of tributary streams, which in their turn are distributed in millions of driblets over an area of some fifty square miles. The final subdivision is naturally a very unequal one : the cup of the rich is full to overflowing, while the portion of the poor is a ' mere epoonfu,. An average West End household con- sumes the whole produce of a single cow (or about two gallons) a day; In the East End, the Yield of one cue Ih‘as to be shared among perhaps thirty families. The herd reconred to supply London must number not less than 185,000 cows, and the army of workers engaged in the handling of these snimals,in the drawins, preparation, transport, and distribution of their milk must number at least 50,000. At the lowest etimata, London' milk bill exceeds £lB 000 per day (ix., £6,500,000 per annum), while New York, with only three-fifths the population of London, spends over £22,000 per day (i.e., £8,000,000 per annum). But, large as the milk item is in London's food bill, it is a far larger one on London's bill of health." " ' Consumers of elk are,' says Mr Long, • the most gullible members of the public,' and the London house- wife is no exception to the rule. She reposes a touching confidence in the milkman who so obligingly calls round before breakfast and again before tea-time to dole out the precious cream-coloured liquid, and she is quite I content that its past history shall remain a blank. Occasionally, however, her indifference is shaken by an outbreak of disease traced to the milk supply by a I newspaper scare headed • Tuberculous Milk,' or by the' alarming number of convictions daily reported for the sale of adulterated milk. A slight, insight into the con- dition of the milk supply would help her to guard against these dangers. in Lennon is complex in the variety of Its conditions. From the primitive dowtreeper to the large liability company there are many types of the genus 'milkman,' which illustrate various stages in the development of the trade. The number of cows housed in city sheds has dPeroaand since the early part of last century from 24,000 s to slightly over 4.000 in 1901 ; hence the old- fashioned ' cowkeeper dairyman ' is a fast disappearing type. Another type of milkman formerly in evidence was the suburban farmer, who used to drive his cart into town, carrying his milk direct to the houses of custo- mers, and who has now been forced back to the extreme fringe of the suburbs. The railroad now connects the core (listen.; farmer with the city, and if he possesses sufficient capital and enterprise, he is enabled to keep in his own hands the business of retailing by running a shop in town. A natural development, due to the extension of this kind of business, is the system of • contract ' As demand increases, the dairyman is unable to meet it from his own farm alone ;in this case he makes a bar- gain with other farmers to send him a certain quantity of milk every day, Many West End dairymen resort to this expedient, and most of them are prepared to furnish customers with a list of the farms they deal with, and to show the sanitary and veterinary reports relating to them.</p>
        <p>Such independent dairies generally sell milk of a high standard of quality, and cater chiefly for the well-to-do classea. These are outnumbered by the hundreds of smaller dairies which depend for their supply on whole- sale centrac:ors.The fluctuations in demand from day to day would entail ruin on the milk-seller in a very small way of business, if he were to contract wish farmers for a fixed quantity per day. He therefore buys from the contractor (or milk middleman) just what milk He requires from time to time ; and should his customers have the curiosity to ask from what part of the coun- try their milk comes, he would often be at a loss to tell. 'I he poorer parts of London are supplied almost entirely by this type of dairy and by roving ' pedlars,' wkius3 stock-in-trade is simply a barrow and a churn. The pedlar,who is soraewbatof a 'merchant adventurer,'eets his churn filled at the wholesale depot, and hawks a doubtful liquid in the streets at 2tcl to 31/ a quart. Milkmen in "hes e districts may have to make fourteen calls at one house to sell cI worth of milk! Small won- der that the temptation to 'take it out' in another way often proves too strong, and thus the poor are robbed of the nourishment they so much need. But it is not always the 'pedlar' who is the culprit. It is a well-Lamm fact that certain large milk merchants grade their milk according to the district they supply, so that their rich customers get the creamiest. while the poor who pay an equal price, get the p oorest quality. Wholesale contractors (of whom there are between twent,r and thirty), account for about half of London's tatal supply. They handls milk by the thousand gallons —buy from, perhaps, two or three hundred farms, and distribute to the public through the medium of the numerous class of small retailors above described.</p>
        <p>Such wholesalers are able to do the entire business of collecting, purveying, transport, and distribution at a wry small margin of profit, owing to the immense quantities of milk they deal with, some companies dis- tributing from 10,000 to 20,000 gallons a day. They are not, however. without formidable rivals in the trade in form of the large retail companies. These businesses go a step further than those of the wholesalers, for they , combine wholesale buying with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. The Rev. J. F. Matthews and the Byrom Hall P.S.A.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. The Rev. J. F. Matthews and the Byrom Hall P.S.A.</p>
        <p>In the absence of the President, Mr F G West occupied the chair at the gath, ring of men and women of the Liverpool Bgrom Hall P.S.A. Saciety on Sunday, whea the Rev J l Matthews, of Latchford, gave an addres7, tatrtg fr his subject " Why need I be a Curls han ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Cairo-street Chapel.</head>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>Thu"! were good congregations at this place of wor- ship on Sunday when the Sunday school anniversary services took place, sermons being preached, morning I and evening, by the Rev E W Lummis, the pastor. A children's service was held in the afternoon conducted I by Mr U S Knowles, of Sheffield, formerly of Warring- ton, and superintendent of the Sunday school at Cairo- street. Mr Knowlos was highly esteemed while in War- rington,and he had a very cordial reception, his remarks b -ing of an instructive and interesting character. Special hymns and anthems were admirably sung, re- flecting great credit on all concerned ; and the nonce- tons, which were in aid of the Sunday school, were good. The services throughout the day were of a bright and encouragin,v character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>lhe Rev. F. T. Reed's Farewell Sermon.</head>
        <p>lhe Rev. F. T. Reed's Farewell Sermon.</p>
        <p>Os Sunday evening the Rev E. T. Herd preached his farewell sermon as vicar of the Emmanuel (Reformed) Cnu rch, Warrington. There was a fairly largo con- gregation and the rev gentleman preached from the words "Now the G 1 of peace, t sat brought again fro the dead- our Lord J.:.SLIS, that great Shepherd of the sheep, t orough the bit) id of the everlasting covenant, mak , you p riect in ev.ry good work to th His will, working in you that which is well pleasing in His sight, through Jesus Christ ; to whom be glory fir ever and ever"—fieb. xiii. 2) and 21. de remarked that it seemed to him it would be difficult to find a portion of God's word which more fittingly summed up the kind of ministry which he had endeavoured to carry out amongst them for more than.te years. 116 hoped he would not say one single word that evening that could jar upon any heart, but they would forgive him when he said, in tin hope that tbas words might do good, that it bad not been his experience during toe ten years he had been their minister to realize the peace to wh'ch allusion was made Possibly the fault in gust ti a with bins. He had prayed much about the matter, and he was hoping by the sacri- fice of himself that that element in the church which he had in various ways trie i to overcome might be driven. out. It was perfectly true that, as long as they were in the body, baring different minds, having dill rent ways ! of looking at thinga.there woul I be differences of opinion on many points,but it was not necessary there should be opposilion to one arlorAlN' itt heart. It had been his loy during the last ten years to•eet before the congregation the Gospel of the grave of God, by which he meant Christ'e death on the Cross as an atonement for their sins. They hail to thick not only of a Saviour who loved and die I for them, hut of a Saviour who rose again from the dead, and who now lived at Gels right hand. H.-. 1 (ti, preachers knew that some of them were sad by reason of the parting, but they must be of good cheer and look from the human to the divine. As he looked over the pt he was filled with a 'sense of the shortcomings o his ministry, and know bow much better • minister he might have been. l'ilben they had done a I the, c sold they w-ue u 'profit- able servants, and be asked them Co remember, when they thought of the defects and shortcomin 4s of his ministry, that ministers were, after all, only under- shepherds, human beings with many imperfecions,and that whilst they ought to atrive with all their might to in-! imitators of Jesus Christ, it was not possible to be what He was, the chief Shepherd, the Great Shepherd who laid down His life for them. It had been his preacher') priiilege to try humbly, and he trusted to seine extent faithfully, to be See mender-ihephesd. He had sought to bring l'h^ congregation to Christ and to strengthen them in He most holy things ; there were some who hal not re,,- ponded as they might have done to one's desire for their salvation. He could not judge any one, but be would say this sincerely and truthfudy, that ne one could have come amongst them with a more sincere desire for their personal salvation and upbuildiog, and in closing his pastorate he commended them all to Him who was the chief and good Shepherd. In conclusion, Mr Reed affec- tionately said farewell, and asked them to remember that friecdships which found their centrel in Jesus, the friendship ;between true Christians, could never be broken,but were intended to I.st not only through this earthly life but the Heavenly life. Appropriate hymns were sung during the service, the last one, sung by request, being " G d be with you till we meet again." —Mr. Reed's successor has not yet beers appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Golborne-street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The Sunda.v school anniv,rsary s-Tvices in connection with this place 01 worsdip were held on Sunday, and wer, !largely attended. Lac preacher was the Rev. F G West,of LivPrpool, who delivered two powerful sermons to the morning his discourse was directed more especi- ally to the teachers and workers and was based on the words' The Kingdom of Heaven is like unto leaven. which a woman took, and hid in three measures of meal till the whole was Ie.INOW(i." Tie evuzing norm)/ was addrcassd Co the narents, the subj,ct being " What shall this child be ?" le the afternooe a service of song entitled " From Bethlehem to Olivet " was rendered by theehoir and scholars assisted by Mrs Williams who sang the solos in s 3.9, creditable manner. Mr B Few pre- sided, Mr 3 II Owen officiated as conductor, and 51r C Diwson,L.V C.M. ab y discharged the duties 'if organise. Greet credit is due to Mr Owen who had devoted much care and attention to the training of the children. At the evening service Mrs Williams sang " Light after , darkm.'s " with Plod effect. Tian collpesions for the day realized £l4 53 which is an increase upon last year's I amount.</p>
