        <p>Warrington Occasional Court.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY—Before Mr F A Frost.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED Taxirr.—lfroest—Ci;; 59, liamilton-street. IVar- ringtoi. was charged with stealing a brass tap valued 18 6d the property of George Lunt, from his yard, off Victoria. road. P.mltnn. between noon on the 19th and 10 a.m. on the 20th inst—lglizabeth Lunt said the tap was attached to a milkesn. and hal been pulled off it —Sergeant Mellor said that whe ,he arrested the prisoner and charged him with stealing the tap he said he did not think much about it when be ksok it.— rhe prisoner was remanded to the Warrington County Petty Sessions to be held. next Wednesday.</p>
        <p>LIVEDP3OL AND NORTH WALE3.—Encourapd by the increasing popularity f tbth steamers on the run to L'andudno and tne St aits, the Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company annouage 50030 extra ceil- iva, whieo will certainly meet the wishes of the molt exae-ting. In addtion to the regular daily (Sundays included) sai(ings or the S. 'nada) and S:. Dales, the Sanwdon will leave the stage on Saturdays,at 2 15 lo.m. hr Llandudno and the S:raits, bin g due back at 10.45 O m. . while every Sunday in June she will leave st 11,15 a.m. for Llandudno ;only, passengers returning by tan ordinary Et 'am9r, which is due back at 7.30 P.m. Spe.7.ial law tares are fixed for these Trips. Mena,i Straits, always beautiful, are perhaps to be seen at their best in such lovely weather as we Inave been enjoying , within the last day or two, and there is nothing more invigorating than the splendid sail b3tweeu Liverpool I atd L'audulnO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MISS CONSTANCE CR OSFIELD.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS CONSTANCE CR OSFIELD.</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding took place at St. Luke's Church, Jullundur,Puujaub, on April 30th, the con- tracting parties being Miss Constance Eleanor, eldest daughter of the late Mr John Croslield, of Walton Lea, Warrington, and Mr John Hugh Macauley, birrister-at-law, only eon of the late Mr Jeremiah Ryan, of blarlra4: The ceremocy was per- formed by the Lord Bishop of Lahore, assisted by the Rev A B F Cole, chaplain of Jußandar. The bride, who was given away by her brother-in-law, Major C M Bales, 41st Dogras, wore a charming dress of ivory satin with a Watteau train of the same material, covered with Brussels applique lace tucked chiffon eleeies and bolero a deep fl ounce of Brussels lace caught up with orange blos.oms, on the skirt. The veil was of Brussels net, fastened with a wreath of orange blossoms, and she carried a bouquet of gardenias. The train was borne by the two little daughters of MOO/ and Mrs Fayrer, the Misses Evelyn and Jocelyn, prettily dressed in pale blue frocks, with large leghorn hats. They wore gold bar and pearl horseshoe brooches, the gift of the bridegroom. The ceremony to ok place at 530 p.m., and the bridegroom was sup- ported by Captain McPherson, 41st Dogras, as best man. Despite the extremely hot weather, a large number of friends were present at the churce, which was tastefully decorate 1 with palms and white flowers. The hymns sung were "Oh perfect love," and " How welcome was the call' and a short address was given by the Bishop from the altar. After the ceremony, the bride's sister, Mrs C M Eales, held are :eption, and the wedding guests were photographed on the lawn by Mr flr.n- son, of Lahore. Colonel Garbstt propo;ed the health of the bride and groom, to which the latter responded. The happy pair drove ofLamid showers of rice, confetti, and shoes. to Peillair, en route for Mussoorie, where the honeymoon is to be spent. The going away dress of muslin, with chiffon hat to match, al-co the wedding dress were made by Mrs -Jacob Hunt, of 'Lahore By the wish of the bride, the men of the 2nd East Lancashire Eegi- ment,who had acted as choir, were afterward pro- vided with a Lancashire tea. Amoagst the presents were : . . . .</p>
        <p>Bridegroom. to bride. turquoise and pearl bracelet and brooch ; bride to bridegroom, silver cigar ant cigarette box ; Mrs John Crosfield, cheque; brothers and sisters of bride. piano: Mr and Mrs C Reynolds, cheque ; Mr and Mrs 0 N Nicholson, amethyst and pearl necklace and brooch; Mrs and the Misses Dickson, fish knives and forks and fish ,lies ; Dr and Mrs T D Dickson. fru, silver candle- sticks ; Miss McGowan, worked cushion cover and d'oyleys ; Captain and Mrs McKinsty, silver photo frame; Miss Mciiinaty, cushion cover; Or and Mrs I Markel, claret jug :I elr and 'Mrs Ramsden-Price, canteen I knives ; Miss Ryan, lamp ; Colonel astral,. silver lamp ; Mr and Mrs Dyson Jerpore. tea table; Mr and Mrs Thomas. silver candlesticks ; Mrs Lothian Nicholson. fan ; Major and Mrs Lissome ; fruit knives and forks : thr Rev P arid Mrs Mime% embroidered handkerchiefs ; Mrs C M Bales, worked tot cloths ; Mr RuAkton, lace handkerchiefs ; Captain and Mrs Boys's. book ; Major and Mrs Somerset. silver button- hook and shoehorn; Mrs S Brooke, silver vase; Bliss Massey, double silver photo frame ; Miss Gatehouse, sidebaard cloth and mat; Rev F B and Mrs Cole, lace duchesse and mats ; Miss C Broadhant, Irish' lace collar ; Mrs It Barlow. silver toast rack ; Kiss Bsrlow, real lace ; Miss A Vernet. books; Miss L Mackie, menu cards and pocket book • Mrs Watts, muff chain ; Mrs Garratt, silver box ; Miss iurkis, ma.queterle photo frame ;Sdiss L Barnett. silver card case ; Hajar and Mrs Payton. Turkestan carpet ; Major and Mrs Tapp, fruit knife; Mr Charles Bales C Sod iver jug and sugar bowl; Mr W R Moreland C S. sliver Lneknow bowl; Mrs and Mrs Anderson. Dora Ismail Khan table; Miss M Anderson, worked tea cloth: Miss B Anderson, salt cellars ; Madame Ruble silver Berlin dish : the servants and gardners Walton Lea sliver salt cellars ' • Mrs Kemp. brass photo frame : Mrs Plant, netted mats; Miss Worrall,crochet mats ; Mrs and Miss Stratford, table centre ; Mr Brown (1 5, lamp ; Mrs and Mrs Gatehouse, Tennyson's works; Rev A A McMaster. silver butter dishes; Captain MePherson,Chinese tea set; Major and Mrs Fayrer, butter knife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NIGHT AND MORNING WOMEN.</head>
        <p>NIGHT AND MORNING WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Machinists, tallorestres, and all women who have to work from morning to night to make en la meet cannot afford to consult physician when they get run down or out of sorts. N Attlee is then any need. In Charles Forde's Bile Berns they have a certain remedy for all liver and digestive Xeorders ; and it is out of liver de- rangement and indigestion that most of tho ailments connected with sedentary work arist. If the liver is sluggish, if you feel drowsy and tired, if you have fre- quent headaches, aiad fits of depression, r:st assured that you need some corrective. Bile Boone have stood the test of time. They will not fail you. Obtainible for one and Viree-hilf-ponce or two and nine per box from ycur own chemist, or port free by sending price ti the Bile Bran Manufacturing Co., 119 and 120, London Wall, London, E.C. Don't accept any substitute or anything represented as "just as good."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Death Rate</p>
        <p>. '</p>
        <p>According to the Registrar General's official re- turns the following table shows the rate of mortality in Warrington and other Lancashire and Yorkshire towns last week : Death rete. Death rate, Stockport 2OO Oldham .—... 26 St Helens 200 Burnley 240 Wigan 21.0 Blackharn ... 170 WARRINGTON. 19.0 Preston l5O Bolton 15.0 Huddersfield l6'o Bury ....... 160 160 Manchester ...... 19 0 Bradford Salford . BO Rochdale</p>
        <p>Tbe average for tte 76 great towns was 161 per 1,000. _ _</p>
        <p>Sheep-Worrying by Dogs. At a meeting of tha Cheshire Chamber of Agricul- ture, at Crewe, on Monday, a resolution was passed urging the adoption of a law by which all dogs should he kept fastened up from one hour after sunset to one hour before sunrise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Accident at a _Brickyard.</head>
        <p>Accident at a _Brickyard.</p>
        <p>A man named William Hubbard, who lives in Longford—street, Warrington, was working at Statham Brickyard on Tuesday afternoon, wen he had one of his arms taken off by getting it fast in Boole machinery. He was brought to the Infirm- ary, where the injury was attended to.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodists. On Sunday last, Legh-street Primitive Methodists celebrated their Sunday School Anniversary, when Mr J Kartshorne,Darleston, preached two excellent , sermons to large congregations both morning and evening. His addressee were especially suited to the children, and were, also heartily enjoyed by the ' elder people present. The children sang several Selected hymns with earnestness, and Mr Spruce, the conductor, is to be congratulated on the excel- lence of the singing. In the afternoon a Service of Song (by desire) entitled "Whiter than Snow" was given by the choir. Mrs Moores gave the connec- tive readings and the service was much apprecia- ted. The soloists were Miss Eva Miller, Miss Wood- land. Miss Bissell, and Messrs Corrin, Jones, Malts and all sang well their respective parts. At the evening service the choir and children sang a Choral March by Ernest Nichall, entited " Warriors of the Lnrd," and which was a decided success. 1 Mr Fred Podmore, M. 1.0 M., ably presided at the organ at all services. Mr J Corrin I sang Siddlels "Hark, hark, my soul "at the evening service with effect.</p>
        <p>"Truth's" Legal Pillory. The following appeats in this week's legal pillory of " Truth ": W arr in gton Police- Aldershot Police-court court. Before Messrs. Edwin Charles Salkel Monks and Smethurst charged with sleeping out James Clark, painter and having no visible charged with being means of subsistence. drunk and disorderly. Prisoner's right arm is with assaulting John ussless,the tendons being Unsworth and with dam- contracted through rhea- aging a door. Dsfendant matism, and he said he was in the employ of pt. his living by writing Unsworth, and after a little Greek, Latin, and creating a di4turbatice he Spanish with his left knockod him down and hand. Ten days,</p>
        <p>than kicked in the door of hi i shop. There were several previong convic- tion. Fined 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>POSITION OF CYPRUS.</head>
        <p>THE POSITION OF CYPRUS.</p>
        <p>The Colonial Secretary's " Amiable Difference " with Mr Pierpoint, In the Howe of Commons on Monday evening, the vote of £1,0)3 for a grant in aid of tho island of Cyprus occasioned some discussion. Sir A RLLITT pointed out that but for the tribute paid to Turkey Cyprus would be left with a large sur- plus aysilab.e for public works, iti!ta. 1 of having to come to tiat H mse for an annual voce. This isiand was an object lesson in colonial development. There was every hope that the experiments in irrigation works would succeed.</p>
        <p>Mr S Buxr ,eff thought that the promises of persenal liberty and justice made by this c flinty to tf.e Cypriotes had been amply fulfilled. At the same time the tribute to Turkey weighed like a millstone around I the neck of Cyprus. The money spent in Cyprus had been a gold investment bath for the inhabitants of the Wand and for tha people of this country. (Hear, hear). 111 should be in favour of providing increased means ot c,arnunication, especially with Egypt, ea that the pro- duce of Cyprus might be sent to that country. Mr R PIERPOINT (Warrington), wished to impress 01 the Ibuse the fact that steamboat communication with Cyprus was made specially diffleult ifictuse of the necessity of the island conforming to the requirements of the Turkish Government for quarantine against cholera, the plague, and other diseases. In regard to leprosy, he bslieved the island was less afflicted wit ) that di4e ;se than Syria. The party opposite had given toe isaad a Parliament—.a questionable and not very practical benefit, but the island was p3culiar in being a part of the Turkish Empire while subj :et to our flag and governed by our High Commis- Isioner and our oftieers, and yet the people could not claim :::;c) be subjects of the King. If the people could claim the protection of the Huhn Jack they would feel therzveves more settled, the tenure of Property woul 1 be much st-onger, and the island would be in every way in doh benefit?d. It was rather hard that Cyprus should have to pay 3 per cent. interest on the loan of £314,003. The average revenue was £lBO.OOO, an i nut of that the tribute amounted to nearly £93,003. More than £31,000 of that was paid in interest od the 1855 Crimean loan, guaranteed jointly and eparately by Eagland and France, but France had stopped paying interest. Arrangemsnts, he thought. migot have been made fer ready money to get rid of the tribute,and he could not 8:13 why this country should take upon itself the obligation to pay France's debts. He appealed to the Celonial Secretary to take all these matters int ) consideration Speaking for our colonies two years ago the Colonial Secretary said " we ask no ransom, we exact no tribute." But tribute was exacted in this case, and he would ask the C ilonial Secretary whether if the Convection of 1878 had concerned New Zea!and or Canada he w ould try to ant-ere:3 that welch would mean the payment by 1371cv Zealand or Canada of one-third of the reveries every year to bs spent slsewhere.</p>
        <p>Aft,r further discusrioa, Mr O'MARA moved the reductioo of the vote.</p>
        <p>Mr. efts:unser:am in reply, said, that, altlrai th much remained to be done, the island was in a da,- tioctly hotter position than when we first took it. H agreed that the communications with Egypt at present were most irregular aid unsatisfactory, but tie G worri- ment were now engaged in securing a regular service between Cyprus and Egypt. With regard co irrigation, the works were fully completed, and in every recpoct so far as the engineering part of the scheme was con- cerned,they were a great success. But up to the pres int time the results had been a little discouraging owing to the backwardness of the population of Cyprus. They called out for irrigation, but when it was sup- plied they were unwilling to take advantage of it. Ho , believed that the intelligent people of Cyprus, including the leading members of the Legislative Assembly, were prepared tie deal with the matter by fresh legislation, which would enable them to levy an irrigation tax. As to the queation of th 3 tribute, when tbis vote was under diacussion he always had an amiable difference with Mr Pierpoint with regard to the position of Cyprus. He always regretted that Mr Pierpoint put the case so strongly, because it encouraged the Cyprians to bele.va themselves ill-used individuals, whereas in big opinion they were extremely fortunate. We did not put the tribute upon them. We took over Cyprus with the full assent and approval of the inhabitants, subject to tae tribute which tbe 7 would have had to pay whether we had taken over the island or not. The result ' of our taking over the island was that the in- habitants, instead of having to pay £9O 000 a year, had to pay only £60,000, which was a reduction of one- third, at the expense of the taxpayers of this country. There was Ilso the further advantage that the taxation was now e illected honestly, and without any addition through the extortions of tax collectors, which in coun- tries adminliteied under Eastern Methods often amounted to as much as the tax itself. Therefore the Cyprians had nothing to complain of. The tribute was a tribute due to Turkey,which would have to b 3 paid to Turkey whatever happened. Mr Pierpoint also mixed up with this qeestion an issue which was absolutely irre!evant_ Lie mired up the fact that the tribute bad been applied not by Us, but by Turkey, to paying the interest on a guaranteed lean. That was a separate negotiation altogether' If it had not been applied to that purpose the people of Cyprus would not have reaped the slightest advantage. What became of the money after it was paid did not matter in the least to Cyprus. The ameeriment was rejected by 199 votes to 30 ; majority 169, and the vote was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WILD FLOWERS Al rHE WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>WILD FLOWERS Al rHE WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>Carrying out the plan to whieh we referred a week or two ago, a numb is of specimens of Wild Flowers ar, ezhibited, with the view of enabling voting visitcr3 the Museum to learn the names of the Gomm= spezies. The following are on view this week : Meadow Buttercups. (Ranunculus acris) Creeping buttercup (Ranunculus repaus) Bulb.)us buttercup (Ranunculus boltiosus) May-flower (Card' mine prateusls) Shepherd's-purse•(Capsella bursa-pastoris) I Common milkwort (Polygala •ulgaris) Red carapion (Lychnis diurna) Satin-flower (Stel!aria Hoiostea) 1 Gorse. Ulex euromeus Bird'-foot trefoil, Lotus cornlculatus Common hawtho-n, Cratcegus osyacantha Fool's parsley. lithusa cynapium Cross-wort bedstraw, Galuim eruclatura Ash, Fraxinus excelsior Forget-me-not, Myosotls palustris Germander speedwell, Veronica chamcelrys I Ground ivy, Nepeta glechoma ' Spttted lamiun., La.minna maculatnm Yellow dead-nettle, Lamium galeobdolon White dead-nettle, Lamium album Common rib-cross, plautago lauceolata Sycamore, Acer pseudo-platanus Birch, Betula alba Gnat willow. salix c iprea 'Horse Chestnut, Aesculus Hippocastanum Poplar, populus nigra Larch, Lana decidua Early mills, orchis mascula Turkey or moss-cupped oak, Quercus cerris Chimney sweeps, Lazula carnpestrls Cuckoo pint, arum macuiatum Blue bell hyacinth, scuba nutans</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PRIMROSE LEAOTJE.-dt the annual meeting of the Divisional Council, held at Knutiford, on Monday after- noon, Earl E,erton of Tanen presiding, the names of Mr J H Westbrook and Ur F:ederick Pearson, representing Stockton Heath and Wilderspool respectively, wEr3 sub- mitted for election to the council and these gentlemen were duly elec:ed. Mr Westbrook was prestnt at the meeting, but Mr Pearson was not able to attend.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Harry Morant, of the Bushveld Carabin- eers, ehot in South Africa by command oi Lird Kitchener, has left a legacy of satirical verse, in which he satirises the conduct of the war campaign. He heads the lines : "Butchered to make a Dutchman's Holiday." Two brothers named Hauglas,. bath fiiherrnea, were drowned off Scarborough on Wednesday. The one en- deavoured to rescue the other, who had fallen into the sea. -</p>
        <p>The lovesick old lady of Barnet, who has been peter- cuting a brewer's manager by her amorous behaviour for some time past, was on Wednesday sentenced to a month in Holloway. An unknown man has drowned himself in the canal at Union-street, Aneoats. He wore a top hat, and in his pockets were a pocketknife and twopence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>Erturs to the iZbitor.</p>
        <p>Hope Hall MISSiOrI, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Sir,--114ving heard that a rumour has ten circulated by some malicious individual that the Hope Hall Mis- sion. of wnich I have the honour to be th Superinten- that, was prohibited from entering the field hired by the Nonconformists for their Whit Monday recreation, I take the first opportunity of entering an emphatic denial ar a'net the assertion. I may say that tile Rev A Wells, superintendent minister, very kindly made way at the time of the parade fr us to go on the field. As how- ever we had a field kindly lent by Mr W Moores, it was unnecessary far Us to avail ourselves of the oppor- tunity. I again repeat that there is absolutely no l',ruth what- ever in the rumour that we were prohibited from enter- jog toe field.—Tnanking you in anticipation for inser- tion' I remain. yours faithfully. ALFRED JONES, Mssioner.</p>
        <p>'The Dean School Exhibition Charity^ in Newton-in-Makerfield. Sr.—There will probab!y be a Competitive Examina— tion withia the nex: three months for tho award of exhibition from this Charity t) Secondary or Technical schools. The successful candilatrs will be able to begin their work with the Michaelmas term (Sep*.ember.) Further particulars will be given in advertiaernents., I am, your obedient servant, (non) Clerk ani Treasurer.</p>
        <p>JOIN W. WATKIN3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>DUTTON MERCHANT'S SUDDEN</head>
        <p>A DUTTON MERCHANT'S SUDDEN</p>
        <p>DEATH.</p>
        <p>Expires in a Railway Carriage.</p>
        <p>As the 11.20 a.m. train from Ctieter to Liverpool was travelling toetwzen Trafford and Duaham o'th Hill on Monday morning, the communication cord was pulled, and the train brought to a standstill. Two ladies drew the guard's attention to an elderly gentleman who had died io their preseoce. A ticket,"Dsnbigh to Frolshand,'' was found in deceased's posse-don, and the train was. therefore, again sV; in motion and the body conveyed to the latter place, where it was identified as that of Mr John Do vier Irven, a Liverpool merchant, who resided at Dutton L Age, D3tton, nor: Runcorn.</p>
        <p>TriE INQUEST. Mr J C B ite, district coroner, c.mdulted the inquest at the Fro:lsl)am Folic Court, on Tuesday altarnoon. Xlr W Yowl WAN foreman of the jury. - . .</p>
        <p>. - Cyril Norris frven stated that ti 9 resided at Datton Lodge, Preston Brook, and decoased was his father. Dec,!ased was 69 years of age. He last saw his father alive on the 17.ta insr, s;nce when tl-,e deceased had been staying with his Eister at St Asaph. As far as witness iCUPW he had ben is his u-lual health. He had been fishing while away from hame. H 9 had olcasionally suffered from heart trouble durinm the last two or three years. He had not berm attended medically in this neighbourhood. H 3 suffored with his breathing when burrio.d. fie was on his wly home when he evpirsd. Amy Capon, stated that she re;ided at 16, North- :. )ad, Canary-in, hu: Was at present staying at 7, Rodney-street, Liverpool. She Was travel- ling from Chester to Liverpool be the train leaving Chester at 11.28. Deceased and two other ladies were in the same compartment. Deceased Was apparent.y all right when they left Chester. She heard him snoring soon after leaving the station, as if he were breathing heavily. She then saw there was something wrong with him, and attracted the attention I of those in the complrtment. Another lady pulled down I the communication card, and the train was stopped. She thought deceased was dead at that time. He was in the carriage when witness got in, and be then appeared to be quite calm. . . . . . _</p>
        <p>William Kay of Grassland-terrace. Ele!shy, stated that he was travelling by the 11.28 train from Chester on Monday. The train was stopped between Mickle Trafford and Dunham-o'-te-Elill, and witness's attention was c 'died to the deceavd in the next compartment but one. In witness's opinion tal was dead at that time. He was in a sitting p-isition in the corner of the carriage with his back to the window. Witness came on to Frodsnam with the body. ' FrlrJ Faulkner (juror.) : I should like to know if there is anyttlina missing ? Mr C N Iraren replied that he could net tell what &amp;ceased had when be started cut. The Coroner (to Mr Faulkner) : Have you any reason for suggesting that P—Mr Faulkner : We do suggest things at times that do come true, and I should like to have that fathomed ou'.</p>
        <p>The Coroner (th Mr CS Irven) : So far as you know there is no suspicion that he has not got all that belonged to him P—Mr Irven : Not the least. The Coroner : I don% think we need to suspect that. considering the company in which the gentleman was. The Coroner said he thought the jury would be satis- fied that death was cluelto natural causes, but if they were not satisfied as to the precise cause of death they were not hound to say what ic was. He (the Coroner) could not help expressing his sympathy with Mr leveio and the members of his family in the very sudden bereavement that that bafal!en them. It was not often that death visited them no suddenly, but when it did come in this way it was very hard tc bear. The jury returned a verdict :hat de sth was due to natural causes, probably heart failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Accident to a Bill Poster.</p>
        <p>A man named Tuomai Hart was standing on a ladder pnsting bills on the hoarding opposite the County Court at noon, on Wednesday, when the ladder slipped. Be fell to the grounu ani his left leg was broken. He was removed to the Infirmary for treatment.</p>
        <p>Beecharn's Pills Granted an Injunction. In the Ceancery Division on Tuesday, Mr Justice Buckley granted an injunction to Thomas Beecham the well-known pill manuracturer, at bt Helms, against James H Alcock, of Qleen's-road, Man- cliester, and Oldham-road, New ton Beath,to restrain the defendant and his servants from selling or offer- ing for sale as " Beecham's Pills" any pills not. manufactured by the plaintiff. • •</p>
        <p>Fire in Lnierpool Road.</p>
        <p>About ten minutes to eigut on Tuesday morning information was conveyed to Messrs Cro,field's fire depot that On outbreak of fire had occurred at Fern's Brush Works, Liverpool-road. The fire buzzer was sounded, and Superinieudent Gallon and half-a-dozen men proceeded to the works with a reel cart. They quickly got two jets to work, and in a quarter ot an hour extinguished what might have proved a serious fire. Great credit is due to the superintfmdent anti his men for their promptitude in turning out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>G RA PPE N HALL.</head>
        <p>G RA PPE N HALL.</p>
        <p>PRIMROSE L.—The Grappenhall Hat:ltttion was wll repre,ented at the meeting of the Divisional Coun- Cil, held ar Knutsf ord. on Monday atternaon,there beiae Presma':. Mrs Charlton Parr, (dame president). Mjsg Milling (eon treasurer), Mr Lay land, and Mr H J Westbrook. Eat Egerton Of Tatton presided. The nurnercial strength of the Grappenhall Habitation has been greatly augmented by the inclusion of Stockton Heath.</p>
