      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MAY 30th IJ2</p>
        <p>PEACE ANL) PACIFICATION. THERE is a ring of commonplace about the aphorism, "Peace is only the beginning of paci- fication." It is easy to accept the generalisation without quite understanding what in detail it means. The veil has hung so heavily over South Africa for two years past that it is difficult for anyone in this country to form a clear conception of its condition. But in general terms we know Pretty well what the state of the two former R - Publics must be. Lard MILN-Ea himself has de- scribed the utter devastation. The country has been swept, and every vestige of civilisation des- troyed as thoroughly as if a horde of Zulus had Marched over it. The houses, the farm buildings, the mills, the expensive dams and irrigation Plant, the fencos,the farm implements—everything nas gone. The c LW() have first been confiscated and then consumed by the army. That is a serious matter, for it is °big selected and acclimatised breeds which can thrive in South Africa, and cattle are the chief wealth of the country. As for the Beers themselves, of the men at least half are scattered in exile at the ends of the earth ; of the children, cvery family has lost at least one member in the horrors of the" camps." Mothers whose familes have survived will find that their husbands were among the slain. Husbands who have escaped our bullets will come back to find that their families perished on Mr. Bcodrick's "half-rations." It is a decimated and ruined race with which we have to deal—a race which has staked everything Or the idea of independence and fought with a magnificent contempt of danger and less which has extorted the admiration of 34r. CHAMBERLAIN himself. Something will be done to restore them financially, no doubt, but Years must pass before the country can even begin to recover its former aspect. Men in that con- dition are commonly critical, and inevitably they Will look back to the days of prosperity and free- dom before the war as to an ideal. At present it seems to be the intention of the Government to administer the country autocratically, placing net oers but more or less unsympathetic loyalist colonials in every office of trust. If thoy do well, these alien officials will still have to bear the odium fore inevitable misery that must long ritinue. If they do ill, they will aggravate the discontent to a point that must be dangerous.</p>
        <p>Bur the two late Republics constitute only half the Problem. Martial law has loosened the whole i'abric of society in Cape Colony,and brought about —save in the towns, which A DUD.; II have rioted in prosperity—a DREYFUS degree of material disorder and loss not much less than that of war itself. As the recent Blue Book shows, those who framed the regulations in which it was Conducted meant well, and cautioned the officers Whom they appointed against any sort of oppres- sion or persecution. Unluckily, there have fol- lowed the usual results of giving despotic power into the hands of military men with no legal training, and these as a rule militiamen or colonials who were often biassed and not always the most intelligent specimens of a class Which the "Times " used to call "our stupid o%cers." Take, for example, a really terrible case of injustice which has just come to light. The l'ictim was a Mr. SCHOE MLN, s Dutch colonist of °rlsiderable influence and a member of Parlia- l'aerit. One day a soldier rode out to his farm and °Pered him to bring in his horses to the military t'l36t. He obeyed that day, but finding no one receive the horses rode home again. The next ma. 3, the same thing happened—there was still no • t'lle at the dep6 t. The third day a trooper rode `4lt to inquire what Mr. SCHOEMAN. meant by (lisobeying orders. He explained, adding, with :I°;4le natural indignation, that if the militarY o'4l not choose to keep a man in charge at the tleOt they might fetch his horses themselves. For .is he was arrested, kept for two months, and at '4st tried on three charges. Two of them turned olit to be baseless, and on these he was acquitted, bet condemned on tha third charge of failing to bring in his horses. For this he was fined £5OO ahlid sentenced to six months' imprisonment with i'ard labour. It seems incredible, but in fact it is 6t as credible as the Dreyfus case, and the ex. _violation in both cases is the same. There was a Zeret dossier. Some speeches delivered by Mr. 210LINIAN in 1896 in condemnation of the Jameson `4id were laid on the table for the perusal of the Court. The prisoner knew no more of this than</p>
        <p>Dreyfus was allowed to know about the secret insinuations concerning his past. The old man was actually sent in convict's garb with shaven head to do scavenger's duty among liCaflir criminals in the streets of Capetown. It is a typical case, unfortunately. There are many more recorded in a pamphlet which has just been issued by Mr. F. MACKARNESS, a gentleman of high legal standing who has carefully weighed his evi- dence.</p>
        <p>RCT it is not merely the victims of these courts- martial who have suffered. Every district in Cape Colony was under a commandant, often a local Volunteer,or an ex-policeman, IVuoi.zsAtE or a Militia officer. These men PROSCRIPTION, were allowed to detain any resident of their district whom they chose to " suspect" of pro-Boor itendencies. They could consign a man to prison without stat- ing a case or specifying any charge, against him. As the case of Dr. REINECKE shewed, even the Supreme Court might order a man's release, but had no power to make its orders respected: In course of time this license became a syst em. Special detention'camps for" Undesirables " grew up. There were five or six of these, and one had over two hundred prisoners. About the others nothing is known. None of these men had been tried, or even charged. They were clergy- men, Members of Parliament, doctors—in fact the most respected Dutchmen in the Colony. But the man in the street suffered also. There is no conscription in Cape Colony; but •in many dis- tricts young men who did not join the Volunteer Corps to fight against their kinsmen were com- pelled by the commandants to sweep the streets, to work on the roads, and to perform work more disgusting still which is always done by Kaffirs. We have been at pains to give several thousand men direct personal grievances against us by this _— policy, and every one of them has relatives and friends who share his resentment. It would be hard to imagine any policy more fatuous or more unjust. Because the Dutch of the Colony sympathised with their brethren in the Republics, and believe the war unjust, as did at least one man in three in the Btitish House of Commons, we seem to have set ourselves to ruin them by fines, to exasperate them by destroying their liberties and to drive them into rebellion by treating them as we might treat native thieves. The more high-spirited rebelled. The rest will cherish a hatred not less profound because it could not express itself. If we are wise we shall pay no heed to the loud clamour for the permanent sus- pension of the Cape Constitution. The only hope for the future is to shew the men whom we have wronged that with peace our respect for law and justice has returned.</p>
        <p>In Lord Pauncefote the country has lost a great servant, and humanity at large a devoted and enlightened apostle of peace. While louder men have made names by provoking war it was his glory to have averted at least one fratricidal struggle. It was while he was our Ambassador to the United States that President Cleveland issued in 1895 the ultimatum on the Venezuelan Ques- tion which might (many have provoked a conflict. Again, two years later, he risked ever: thing in an effort to stave off the Spanish- A rnerican war. It was he who brought ideas ts : ;8 Hague Confer- ence while other delegates camt.. with credentials. To him is due the permanent board of arbitration w'sich might yet play a great part in the world's history if only the Chamberlains of all nations would allow it. We honour our war-makers more than enough. This unobtrusive idealist in harness deserves a tribute more lasting and sin- cere. -- Lord Rosebery occupies a position in our politics so detached and so personal that his utterances seem to disappoint each party in turn. The other day he issued a manifesto in which he not only repudiated Home Rule, but confessed that never, even when he sat in the Home Rule Government, had he been a sincere advocate of Irish self-gover- ment.That naturally delighted the Unionists,while Liberals were dismayed by its levity and cynicism. But the pendulum has swimg once more, and at the National Liberal Club this enigmatic figure contrived to make a speech which delighted his hearers, outraged none of their convictions, and produced a chorus of disgust and dismay from the Ministerial Press. Lord Rosebery, who said at Chesterfield that his only quarrel with martial law was that it had not been proclaimed soon enough, now takes his stand beside Mr. Morley in the Cartwright case, and declares him- self a champion of constitutional liberty. At Colchester he seemed to bless the Education Bill because it would add to the strength and dignity of municipal bodies He now assures us that he realises that it would do nothing of the kind, and accordingly he denounces it as a Bill which would "sterilise national education for a generation to come." But best of all, this statesman, who is before everything else an Insperialist,disavows Mr Chamberlain's plan for federating the Empire by abandoning Free Trade. For the Bread Tax he 11/2as nothing but contempt and indignation, and he is opposed root and branch to the bare idea of an Imperial Zollverein. All this is highly satis- factory. For the moment, not only in dealing with the Education Bill and the Bread Tax,but in the details of the pacification of South Africa, the Opposition is a solid phalanx. One might feel easy about the future if only Lord Rosebery, instead of preaching Liberal unity, would dissolve the "Liberal League."</p>
        <p>We are glad to learn that a rifle club, with tlie title "Appleton Rifle Club," is about to be formed 'in this district, Opportunities for teaching the 'young idea" how to shoot are uncommonly scarce in this country and gratitude is duo to the founders of such clubs as the one under notice. There should soon be an-encouraging membership for the club is quite ready for action Mr. T. H. Lyon, the president, having generously allowed the use of the spacious quarry at Hill Cliffo for a range. It is proposed to provide regulation tar- gets for both rifle and revolver shooting and every opportunity will be afforded members of acquiring some skill in marksmanship which, in time of national emergency, wovld undoubtedly be valu- able. Ladies are to be similarly encouraged in the use of fire-arms, and it may be safely predicted that any future invader will find Warrington an hard nut to crack if he attempts to take the town by assault. --- Local Assessment Committees would do well to study the prices which have been given by War- rington brewers for licensed houses at recent sales by auction. St. Helens seems to be an especial favourite of the brewers for the prices which have been given lately for public-houses have been enormous. Only this week a leasehold beerhouse known as the "Green Dragon" standing at the corner of Ormskirk-strest and Westfield-street, St. Helens has been secured by Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Company for £5,000. Coming nearer home we find the same firm giving 0,400 for the Rope and Anchor Hotel and some land adjoining at Woolston. If property of this class possesses such intrinsic value for the brewers, Assessment Com- mittees are justified in increasing the assessments proportionately. A slight increase in a public- house assessment is generally resisted by the tenant on the ground that the takings do not warrant any advance. There is always a difficulty in arriving at an equitable adjustmeat. The easiest way out of the difficulty seems to be to take into consideration the number of tied houses controlled by a brewery company wen re-assess- inc, the brewery and adding a reasonable sum to the assessment in consideration of the value to the brewers which is inherent in the tied house ysteni.</p>
        <p>.".„-.,.`..„.;',':.,'',`,:',1,•:'...",.«.;;;..'..,...'.;....-...',!</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30. 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30. 1902</p>
        <p>The " wheels of the chariot of Peace," for which the public everywhere have been most anxiously waiting, have tarried long, while the delay whicn was, of course, inevitable, served only to intensify the feeling. That all would " come right very soon" was a gratifying assurance, repeated daily. But meanwhile the days went on, Cabinet Councils were heffi in London with no result that was made known to the public, and the House of Commons seemed to be the last placa in which to look for information. Hence, as ['mays is the case under similar circumstances, many-tongued rumour was exceedingly busy. One of her stories was Vint the news was being kept back in order that the announcement of peace might be made on the date of the official celebration of the King's birthday— yesterday—though, little as might be the respect some people would have for ministers guilty of such childish folly, under the circumstances, it were cruel to accuse them of entertaining any thought even of that nature.</p>
        <p>While awaiting the official announcement of Peace,as between his Majesty's subjects and the people. of South Africa, the thoughts of the multi- tude naturally turned to the preparations, which are becoming more observable in all parts of the country every day, for the Coronatisn ceremonies. scarcely a day passes now that does not synchronise with the arrival of some notable personality, who is either an official repre- sentative or has been attracted to this country by the promise of a spectacle which will be unique of its kind. All the great Colonies and depend- encies will send representatives, many of whom will be statesmen whose enterprises in Empire- building have to be reckoned as of equal import with the most strenuous work of the soldier. And mention of the latter recalls a statement to the effect that the Boers will not be without their representatives en the occasion. Indeed, it would not surprise one greatly to observe certain of them figuring in provincial processions. It is a char- acteristic of the future Briton that he cherishes the most generous impulses twards a gallant foe, and that these impulses are all the more deeply stirred when the foe is vanquished. When the time for the Coronation arrives the Boers will have become " children of the Empire" also, and there is nothing leally extravagant in the idea that the great Boer General De Wet may figure in the Royal pageant. It is scarcely necessary to say that he would have h quite as enthusias ic a reception in the provinces as; he would in e Metropolis ; for his sterling qualities and s lendid courage have long been recognised by newspaper readers in every part of the country. But if he means to come to England for the festival to which all are eagerly looking forward, he will have little time to lose. Whether Lord Kitchener will be present at the Coronation is a point regarding which there is at present much speculation. There is, however, the bare possi- bility that the Commander-in-Chief in South Africa ' I may be able to join in the Royal cavalcade. 1</p>
        <p>Certain Boards of Guardians appear determined to justify the allegation that they consider them- selves guardians of the rates rather than of the poor. At Walsall the other day the Board ordered preparation of a return of every person who had been vaccinated at the public expense during the last three months, the object of being so vaccin- ated being to throw the expense of their vaccina- tion or re-vaccination on toe rates while capable of I discharging the obligation themselves. On the same day the Andover guardians agreed to publish in a " paupers' list " the names of all those who had been thus vaccinated. But while in this frame of mind one of the members of the Board called at the Local Government Office in Whitehall and took advice of the authorities there as to the advisable- ness of the step. Guardians who are so jealous of the rates are now informed through their colleague that the step proposed would be illegal, and would probably lead to their being surcharged the cost of publishing the so-called "paupers' list." The rule is that those who accept free vaccination cannot be classed as having received out-relief, and this ruling has given general satisfaction. Guardians are assuredly pursuing a one sided and short-sighted policy in meddling with the matter at all. All that they can reasonably demand, under the obliga- tion imposed by the authorities, is that thoQe who desire the attendance of the vaccinating offi 3er at their own residence should be prepared to pay for the attendance out of their own pockets.</p>
        <p>Raids upon professional Palmists have been unusually frequent and numerous of late in some parts of the provinces, and it is observed that when certain of the offauders have re-appeared after a term of imprisonment, they do not hesitate to announcs that " Bazaars will be attended as usual." It must have been a knowledge of something of the kind ',that induced the Stipendiary in the Potteries district this week, in a case brought before him, to advise the police to bestow sonm of their attention upon bazaars and other places of social and fashion- able resort, where the practice, he believed, was very prevalent. The case in question was dismissed the evidence showing that there was no intention to deceive. Cases in which there is no intention to deceive are probably rare.</p>
        <p>Another matter in regard to which publiccriti- elm is too often " lob-si " is that which relates to Sunday Observance. There is plenty of room in which the work of the Lord's Day Observance Society can be carried on to public advantage with- out trenching upon the liberty and the peace of mind of those who only have one day in the week to get about on,and by change of scene and healthy means of relaxation, both of body and of mind, lay up a stock of health for the ensuing week and benefit themselves generally, Sunday trading, as at present pursued in many towns and cities, presents an abundance of scope for reform of a kind that, judiciously prosecuted, would irritate and in- convenience scarcely anybody. One half of the Sunday traders only keep open on that day because the other half does so, and it has become the cus- tom, and many would very gladly close if it were not so. But to hinder working people especially getting abroad on Sunday is unwise, and from the average Englishman's point of view, intolerable. I Cardiff has just supplied an object-lesson worthy of being learnt and inwardly digested by other com- munities. The proposal in the local Coun2il for a Sunday service of trams was at first vetoed, after a heated discussion, by a majority of one. Then the public were up in arms, and members of the Town 'Council were prevailed upon to reverse the previous decision, by twenty vo.,es to thirteen. The majority was considered narrow, but. like Mercutio's stab,it has been allowed to "serve." And on Sunday last it was found that the "innovation was justified by its results. The public, it is said, took keen ad- vantage of the trains to enjoy the first real slimmer Sunday in the parks which abound there. The only "fly in the cream "now is that the public consider the hour for commencement should be fixed for 12 30 instead of two pm. But Po far as the public indignation which had been predicted by opponents of Sunday trams is concerned,it is said by the local papers to have been entirely lacking.</p>
        <p>It will be well for the public in all provincial towns to make up their mind that the motor-car has come to stay, that nothing can keep it out of the country, and it is as useless for landlords and farmers, county councils and railway authorities, to oppose its progress as it wasfor our grandfathers to oppose the introduction of the steam engine. Much as some of us dislike its buzzing and bustling its twistings and turnings in the sometimes crowd- ed streets, and also its harsh and peculiar "hooting,' we have to acknowledge that it is a great and useful invention, and that we cannot have these things entirely our own way. The give-and-take principle commends itself here as it does in con- nection wi*h so many other " innovations " and unfamiliar objects met with in public thoroughfares at tbis period of the worM's history. A federation of dairy farmers' societies, under the title of the Cmtral Dairy Farmers' Association, is among the most recant of modern "innovations," and now represents some twenty established societies. The Liverpool and Manchester retail traders have in like manner formed the Dairymen's and Cvvkeepers' Federation and are inviting other dairy farmers to come over, co-operate with, and help them. It is felt by those immediately inter- ested in the milk trade that farmers and retailers ought to unite on a more satisfactory basis than at resent. So long as the object is not aggressive, but purely defensive as the promoters suggest,there is nothing to say against it, Our daily milk supply is in their hands, and all the public expect is to have the milk pure and at a reasonable price.</p>
        <p>NRW PRINTS AND MIIILINi. New Silks, Trim ming and Laca Collars, New Lace an! Ki I G 10,705. Beautifti Varietr,"—W. T. Willialtis 74, S.LTICEP-STREEP AVD 1, LSGII- - Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S C 113 E AM RIA C.K E R. S CRISP. AND CREA.A7</p>
