      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY TRIIILTNUS].</p>
        <p>It is not often that supporters of clericalism van- ' ture to uphold publicly beir belief in its efficacy 1 for the public good. When one enters the lists to splinter:a lance in its behalf you may rest assured that the lance has bad to be couched through sheer force of circumstances, and then not until it has I become absolutely necessary in order to withstand some serious attack, which threatens to have disastrous results. Clericalistic supporters gener- ally adopt the policy of looking neither to their right hand nor to their left but marching straight ahead with their eyes fixed on a goal to be reached at all costs. When a certain line of conduct has to be openly justified it is either because there are threatened d e fiections from the rank and file of their own pledged party, or that the great British public, which refuses at times to have too great a quantity of dust thrown in its eyes,is menacing the line of march with disconcerting disaporovai. At such times then silence is leaden ; and honeyed speeches are golden.</p>
        <p>These are the only accountable reasons why Canon Wood endeavoured to defend in a speech made last week the clericalistic spirit at present I brooding over the town. The reverend gentleman is by far too astute an observer not to notice the signs of the times in general, and the revolt against clericalism in particular. Were this present upheaval of thought against clericalism confined Solely to our own town it would probably be dismissed in the knowledge of conscious strength with a contemptuous flourish of the band. But every sectarian knows that the present leeling in Runcorn is but a faint echo of the wider revolt against clericalism now sweeping over the entire British nation—vide all the great speeches on the education question for and against the Bill, in our own town too it is well-known that there are many strong churchmen, I who were they unmuzzled would openly revolt against the tactics adopted by the ultra-clerical party which seek to undermine and dominate the best interests of the laity. Thus it is fund that clericalism, driven to bay, is, through threatened secession and public disapproval, forced, in order to preserve a unity of front and to'conciliate public opinion,to abandon a self imposed policy of silence.</p>
        <p>Referring, presumably, to what had appeared in these columns Canon Wood said that an attempt was being made by certain individuals to persuade the people that the interests of the laity were not the interests of the clergy. This the reverend gentle- man denied ; and asserted that the interests of the clergy and the laity were identical. The issue is a simple one and the Vicar has evidently grasped the point underlying my notes. As, however no attempt was made to prove the assertion the state- ment may be regarded as groundless. It would be an easy matter in things temporal—excluding the larger issues of the spiritual condition of the Church of England which, it is to be feared, is be- , ing permeated with Romanising doctrine and can no longer be regarded as a hulvrark of Protestantism— , to snow that, say in Runcorn and the adjacent town IVidnes,institutions have been unfairly tinged with sectarianism to the detriment of the public weal. One has only to mention the District Nurse Fund to recall similar instances which have taken place on both sides of the River Mersey. It is, then, per- fectly clear that clericalism has gained certain un- fair advantages while the town and therefore the public good have correspondingly suffered. Conse- quently it is as evident as one of Euclid's axioms that the interests of clericalism and the interests of the laity in these particular instances do not coin- cide withleach other with suchtmatheraatical correct- flees as the Canon would have us suppose.</p>
        <p>There is, however, a wider qusstion at issue in the statement made by the Canon 'than the mere depredatory exploits of clericalism for party aggrandizement. Has the church as a body the betterment of the people at hear? From the stand- point of creed Yes! From the standpoint of religion ; of education ; of social betteraoet, No 1 Thirty years ago the eiucational welfare of Britain which really means the intellectual supremacy which, in turn, counts for:existence in these high pressure competitive days—hung in the balance. But the church dignitaries did not throw their weight on the side of a universal s 3 stem of educa- tion with religion devoid of creed ! So for thirty years the anomaly of two distinct competitive systems has been seen and England has meanwhile been torn with the conflicts of sectarian passion. The parson blocked the path of progress then with his nogmatic, theological , wall in the same way as he threatens to block it to-day. So great an authority as JR, Green, the historian, himself an Anglican minister, testifies to the interests of the laity being undermined by the craftiness of the Establishment. •</p>
        <p>Writing in 1867 to Mr E A Freeman, a brother historian, Mr Green, who was always an hi,torian first and a clergyman second,discusses, in a private letter, the causes hindering reform. He alks, "What hinders reform r The want of education among the people. Alia what kinders education hut the pre- sent attempt at a se:tarian and not a national system? Awl what hinders a national system of education but the Church? People may now boast truly , enough that they withoold the spelling-book. The ' present system of education has done much—yes, but it has done all it can do. No mere quarrels about conscience clauses can touch that matter. Nothing can touch it but a general system of com- pulsory national education supported by a national rate. I wish people could see the waste of the present system—half-a-dozen schools, British, National, private, where one good large school would suffice at one-third of the total expense. with double the present results. But what chance is there of such a change? Just , none whatever. The clergy know that a thoroughly educated pe3ple without any uneducated classes Tvould be the ruin of their Establish»zent. And so they fight every point; the conscience clause is a little thing but with them it is a fight for life. They won't win in the long run—but lam sick of looking forward to a free England which will appear about a century and a half after I am dead. ' And so more alcd more I can't help looking to the West. There is the world as the world will he. Tcere are most the things one hopes for and cares for and lives for. There is a people—there, and not here in England."</p>
        <p>Such is the utterance of, perhaps, the greatest his'erian of the English people. A man, too, who, by his profession would have naturally been pre- disposed to view the clerical party with partiality. Do the interests of the laity count for aught when compared with the Church's love of secular power? In the foreg3ing letter let the answer be read. In its utterance it is almost prophetic. In the West, America, the land of the free, peopled by the Pil- grim Fathers whom Clericalism hounded out of our land, education has reached such a pitch that its citizens, by sheer mental forco, are destined to dominate the world. For, as certain as mind rules matter so the educated man willtule his fellows. It is your educated millionaire, like Morgan, who creates your trusts, and your combines against which the dull untrained intellect of the Briton stands aghast.</p>
        <p>But what would the deceased historian have said of the present Education Bill which the Rev 1. G Fry, vicar of St, James, Upper Edmonton, speaking at the Enfield Ruri-decanal Conference, denounced as a subtle and a crafty bill? As untair to Non- conformists ; as a church job and nothing else; and one he hated from every point of view. A Bill which, if pressed, would stir up religious embers and would blaze into a fire throughout the country It is to be feared that Mr Green would have lost all hope of eter seeing an enlightened and a free England.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows that Canon Wood is a generous hearted gentleman, and that personally he is as hard a worker as anyone again4t the forces of evil. But men must stiffen their moral backbone when there are evidences that there is an uncompromis- ing spirit ;abroad which seeks to place a particu- lar meet foremost over other religious and civil rights. May I, in all respect, ask the Vicar to refute the letter quoted and show how clericalism has ever benefited the laity ? The matter is an urgent one and in the words of an ancient Greek philosopher "let us have truth though the heavens fall."</p>
        <p>As I write, the results of the County Council Sehblarship examination have been issued. It is a significant fact the Victoria-road Board School actually possesses six youths who won the qualify- ing marks against one sci,olarship only obtained by all the voluntary schools in Runcorn. An equally significant and pleasing feature is that out or the six Board School scholars who passed two belonged to the Establishment, Evidently their parents</p>
        <p>appreciate the value of good training, and further, must be well satibfied with the religious education given. The teachers and the scholars deserve the highest congratulation on their success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF FOOTBALL MEDALS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the officials, Executive Cora- mittee and the players of the Runcorn Football Club spent a _very pleasant evening at the Wilson's Hotel, under the presidency of Mr Samuel Taylor. The meeting was convened for the purpose of pre- senting county championship medals to members of the first team, and the medals and cups won by the "A" team during the past season. 1 his in- teresting function, which was performed by Mr Taylor with his characteristic geniality, was pre- ceded by a supper which did credit to Mr Wild- goose's ability in the catering line. The following were the recipients of county championship medals: Messrs S Houghton,T Wilkinson,T Warder, J Butterworth, J Jolley, J Richardson, S Walker, W Lightfoot, W Woods, II Farmer, and J Langley. Junior cup medals were handed to Messrs T Wilkin- son,, F Dutton, II Price, E Hesketh, R Brown, Joseph R Faulkner, G Williams, F Darlington, W Wilson, 0 Houghton, W Huughton, W Poole, E Tomlinson, W Thomas and J Dixon. BLAuKTOOL SIMMICIt CiA5l2, commencing June. at .3 Kirkham' Fa; m.—Breaktast, Dinner, Tea and Supper every day for a week and a good camp Lei for 15s ; week-end 63 6d.—Apply H W URIFFIN, Secretary, 153, Islington. Liverpool.—Advt. RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS AT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the members of the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd V.B. Cheshire Regiment attended divine service at Runcorn Parish Church. They paraded at the Drill Hall at ten o'clock, and when the roll was called it was found that there were present :—Two officers (Captain Geo. If Ashton and Captain Timmins), one sergeant- instructor, eight sergeants, three lance .sergeants,24 band and buglers, 72 rank and file, and one retired veteran (Quartermaster-S'ergeant Hankinson) ; total 111. The fine weather and glorious sunshine furnished just the right conditions for showing up the men's bright helmets and scarlet tunics,and they bore a distinctly smart and soldier-like appearance. Their progress through the streets, led by the band playing inspiriting musir, furnished an attraction for crowds of residents who lined the streets. The service at church was conducted by the Rev H B Firth, and the Vicar, Rev Canon Maitland Wood, preached an appropriate sermon. The service closed with the singing of 'Onward, Christian soldiers. On emerging from the church the Volunteers,under the command of Captain Ashton, marched back to' the Drill Hall and were dismissed. SUCCESSFUL RUNCORN STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>The result of the examination for the County Council scholarships has just been made known. There were 396 candidates, against 26'.i last year. Of these 265 were boys, and 131 girls; 303 came from urban districts and 93 from rural districts. The 396 candidates were drawn from 93 schools---62 in urban districts and 31 in the rural parts of the county—and these only represent a quarter of the number of schools in the county. Sixteen per cent of the whole number of candidates gained the qualifying number of marks, the students number- ing 63. Of these 54 were boys and nine girls. It is interesting to know that of the 37 scholarships three are taken by Runcorn Victoria Board School Boys, viz. :—William W Wishiade, 6, Byron-street Runcorn ; Thomas Albert Lunt, 10, Curzon-street, Runcorn; Arnold Shaw,llalton-road, Rancorn. The following local students gained the qualifying num- ber of marks, but there were no scholarships for them : Geo. Ralph Clare, 25,Asbriclge-street, Run- corn National Schools ; Ernest Pendlebury, 18, Halton-road, Victoria-road Board Scheol ; William G Draper, 45 York-street, Runcorn, Victoria-road Board school ; Herbert Goodier, 38, Stonehills-lane, Victoria-road Board School. The boy at the tDp of the list—George Bowden Parkes, of the British School, Altrincham—got the highest number of marks ever obtained at any examination, viz. 483, out of a possible 500. SONS OF TEMPERANCE, RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>A meeting of the" Life-boat 'Crew "Division was held on Saturday night in the Greenway-road School, under the presidency of Mr Thomas Clarke, After the routine business had been transacted, Messrs James Johnson and Thomas R Whyte, who had been the representatives to the National Divi- sion, held at Bolton in Whit-week, gave their report which was of an interesting character. The order is making very satistactory prog,ress, numerically and financially, and is taking a foremost place as a temperance beneficiary order. On Whit Sunday afternoon a number of the order and friends assem- bled on Victoria-square, Bolton, and, headed by the Borough Prize Band, marched in procession to the Parish Church, when the Vicar of Bolton (the Rev II Henn, id A), preached an appropriate sermon. The business of the division commenced on Whit- Monday morning, when the various reports were given. These showed that the past year had been full of activity,no less than 4,901 having been added to the membership. The adult branelies had increased by 43, the juveniles by 42 sections; the juvenile membership had increased by 40,143. The capital had been augmented by more than .C2o,ooo,and the mortality was less than 6 per 1,000. It was stated that klr W Whiteman had been invited to represent the order at the Corona- tion festivities. There was an interesting discus- sion on the report by the following members:— Messrs Thomas J Holt. D Bisbrown,Thomas Dutton and the Chairman. Taey were considered very satisfactory and accepted unanimously. Mr D Bis- brown said that most of them would be aware that Mr Thomas Cooke and his family were under the shadow of a great sorrow. Mr Cooke, a few days ago, had received tidings of the sudden death of his daughter, Mrs J R Shipley, in South Africa, and he felt sure that their hearts would go out in sympathy to him in his bereavement. The late Mrs Shipley in the past had been much interested in their work and had furthered the interests of their order in many ways, especially at their de- monstration. lie begged to move the following resolution : "That this division of Sons of Temper- once expresses heartfelt sympathy with Mr Thomas Cooke and his family in their sad bereavement, and that a letter of condolence be sent by the secretary." This was seconded by Mr Thomas J Holt, supported by the Chairman and Messrs Thomas R Whyte and James Johnson, and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FRODSHANI.</head>
        <p>FRODSHANI.</p>
        <p>A. MERCHANT AND HIS CARRIAGE.—At the Eddisbury Petty Sessions on Monday, before Colonel Lascelles and other magistrates, Edward Moseley Williams, of Manley Fiodsham, who was described as a Manchester mer- chant, was summoned at the instance of the Inland Revenue for keeping a carriage without a licence. It AM stated that the defendant was licensed for the carriage last year. and although notices were sent to him he failed to renew the licence. When applied to on March 26 the defendant stated tha'l he had taken out a licence in Manchester.—Mr Lowry, Inland Revenue officer, stated that the defendant kept no establishment in Manchester, and suggested that the reason he took out a licence there was to hide the fact that he had failed to take out a licence at the proper time. The de- fendant had adopted every device to avoid payment, and had told anything but tho truth.—The defendant was fined 5 and cats in the Case of the carriage and £2 and costs in the case of the manservant.</p>
        <p>• Mr Yates, the Manchester County Stipendiary, in sentencing s Milos Platting railway porter to six weeks' imprisonment for stealing stockings, said the public had no conception of the extent to which railway companies were robbed. Thousands of pounds worth of goods were stMen. and nobody could Mai nut the denredators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>Po stal Arrangements.</p>
        <p>The Postmaster-General has decided that, with the view cf securing the übservance of Coronati:m Day as a general holiday at all Post Offices throughout the United ! Kingdom, the postal and telegraphic arrange- ments on that day shall be the same—generally speaking—as en Christmas Day in England, that is to say, all Post Offices shall be open to the public during the same hours as on Sundays. At offices (not being town sub-offices) which.do,not open at all on Sun- days, attendance is to be given from 8 a m to 10 a m for the sale of stamps and registration of letters, and in the case of telegraph offices tor telegraph business. Town sub-offices will not be open at all. At all offices one delivery both of letters and parcels is to be made; and one collection, which should be that for the Night Mail and take place at the usual week-day hour, except in the case of places served by rural Post- men, who will be allowed to return to their headquar- ters as soon as they have finished their delivery. Friday the 27th June is to be observed as an ordinary Batik Holiday, and the arrangements are tiler:dote to be precisely similar to those regularly in force on Bank Holidays. BRUNNER, MOND, AND CO.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LIMITED.</head>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Continued Prosperity. 32 Per Cent, Dividend.</p>
        <p>The shareholders of Brunner, Mont, and Co., Limited, held their thirty-seventb annual meeting on Wednesday afternoon, at the Law Association Rooms, Cook-street, Liv( rp3o!. Sir John r Brunner' M.P, presided, and amougst those prereat were Dr Ludwig Mond, Dr D B Hewitt, FR S, Messrs H Brunner, J F L Brunher. Roscoe Scanner, Thomas H Forgan, C J Galloway, CM Holland, Gustav Jarmay, E Milner, A Mond, N Mond, and H A Scheltaa?.</p>
        <p>The report and balance-sheet account showed a balance to the profit and lose amount on the working of the year ended 31st March, 1902, of £430,430 193 41 which, with the a moult. of £36,930 15e 71 brought for- ward. made a total of £517,361 141 lid. I: Wig pro- posed to pay dividends at the rate of 7 per cent on the preference shares and 321 per cent on ordinaav shares, and to carry forward a balance of £37,701 4371 The Chairman congratulated the snareholoers on the result of the year's workines. The work had teen carried on by the company's td in a thoroughly satis- factory manner, and their gross profits were consider- ably in excess of those of last year. During tho year additions to the amount of £l6 960 had been made to freehold land, and £86,403 to the works. 'This was rather less than nad been the case for sornefyeirs past, and be locked forward to even smaller figures infthe year to come. The invest- ments were s:ightly larger than a year ago ey an amount of £lB,OOO shares in Dr Mond' a Gas Power Cor- poration and Small Staffordshire Mond Gas Company, which came to them, in accordance with Dr Mon.l's agreement with the company, without payment. On the patents accounts exactly £2,500 nad been written off ,land a small amoect of instalmeuts for patents written off. In regard to the stocks of material, he need say very little. Some stocks went up and some came down, and the fact that they rea'- ised £16,000 more than last year was a me,e accident. Debts owing to the company were larger than last year on account of the increase in their business. The capital account would he found to he increased by the amount of 33 per share on 662,830 £1 ordinary shares, making those shares half paid. Tee reserve fund had increased by the amount added to it a year ago—£69,ooo. The debts owing by the company had increased in the same fashion in proportion to the increase of businese. Tne balance due to the bank was a trifle more than last year. The profit and loss account was £19,000 less than previously, whice was duo to the fact that last year they reduced the balance carried forward by writing off that amount to reserve fund. This was, uowever, a mere matter of book-keepiug. Tee directors pro- posed this year to adopt again a policy which had boon familiar to the shareholders for several years, up to some four or five years ago. Tory propcsad to place £60,000 to suspense account. He eloped toe sharehold- ers would rem33rnher that for several years they had pot large sums aside to suspense account. Brunner, Mond and Company had always been a progressive concern and must remain so (hear, hear) and they must allow for improvements in their plant being paid for out of the profits, as they had doce in years gone by. He could assure the shareholders that the balance sheets of the company showed the more uncomely rather than the comely side of the business. The care taken never to anticipate a profit and always to be prepared for future expenditure was very great—one might almost say was extreme in the affairs of the company. Next year, as in other years, they would have to pro- vide for improvement in their plant, and the directors recommended the meeting to adopt the proposal to carry £60,000 to suspense account. Having sympath- etically referred to the death during the year of Mr John Crosfield, who had been a director of the cernpany since its incorporation, and chairman of the board during the firet ten years, Sir John formally proposed the adoption , of the repert and balance sheet and the recominenda- tion of the directors that dividends be paid at the rate of 7 per cent, on preference shares, and 321 per cent. on ordinary sharer. Dr Mond seconded the reaolution. 113 remieded the shareholders that this year the company attained its majority. It was regitt.red in the spring of 1831, and those who had gone to the trouble of cainpariog this year's balance sheet with previous ones would have noted with satisfaction that the profits earned were now higher than what they had been in any previous year in the company's history. (Hear, hear.) It afforded him special pleasure to report that along with this flourish- ing condition of things theia works were being kept in the most perfect state of effialeacy possible. The posi- tion of the company after twenty-one years was as strong and stronger thin it had ever been before. (Dear, bear.) The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>On thn motion of Mr C M Holland, 'Reeoncied hr Mr Alfrei Mond, the fo/lowing were r,-eleeted directom : Mews E Milner, C J Galloway, Dr Hewitt, II Brunner, and Hugh Bell.</p>
        <p>Mr George Jones moved. and Mr seconded, , that the directors bn paid £1,200 for their services eur- lCM the year. This was resolved. Mr 17 Coghill propos-d that Messrs T W Read and Co., be re-appointed auditors at a remuneration of £725. Mr L B Wells seconded, and expressed the hop,. that the chairman would be eq ial to any combination or monopoly which might be formed calculated to do the : company any injury in the distribution of its goods to various parts of the world. Tne resoluCon was carried.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner, referring to Mr Wells's observation, raid it was quite true that every man of busineeF, especially everyone situated in the North of England, was considering whether the re,!ent amalgamations and the" Morgan •eering "of English businesses was going to affect him prejudiciady. Dr Mond had said that the position of their company after twenty-one years was stronger than it had ever been, and he had pleasure in confirming that, after what Mr Wells had said. On the proposition of Dr Mond, seconded by the chairman, a vote of thankl was passed to the compans staff, and a similar compliment having been accorded to the chairman, the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Frodsham Fetty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Frodsham Fetty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Before Masers John Octcleston. (in the chair) and W N</p>
        <p>Hutchins.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO CITILDRI-.:N- HoOsn. labourer was charged with neglecting his three childred whose ages ranged from 17 months to 7 yeara on the 17th March and other dates.—tilr A Pletcher, Northwich, prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. The evidence showed that Inspec- tor Hackett had had the case under observation for some time. He had warned the prisoner some twelve months ego to reform but he had neglected to do so. The house was constantly kept in the most filthy condition and the children were half starved from want of food,—Prisoner was sentenced to a month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>ALLEG.ED TREFr OF A RARE —Charles Wyatt was charged with stealing a Belgium hare, valued at 5. 60. from the goods yard at Frodsham station on the 25th May.--The eVidence showed that a number of Belgium hares were con• signed to a person in Frodsham, and that while the crate containing the hares was at the station one was taken which was subsequently traced to the house of the defendant.— Defendant pleaded that he saw the hare h-ose in the yard. and some boys were throwing stones at it. He took It home to prevent it from being killed.—Defendant was given the benefit of the doubt end discharged. ALLEGED THEFT OF MONEY EHOM A FATHER.—TwI young men named, Cyril Clarke and Walter A Clarke were charged with stealing in cash the sum of Ei Es 60 from the " Whale- bone Inn" Frodsham, moniea, belong to their father on the 2;th May.—Mr Turton. Chester, defended. The Bench asked If the parties could not come to some understanding inthe matter.—Er Turton replied that the defendants had taken nothinz away from the father and could therefore come to no arrangement. The prosecutor said he would be satisfied if his sons wonld give a written undertaking not to interfere with him any more, Mr Turton sail it was realty a CAlle f another court.—After prolonged discussion the case was adjourned. A MIXED UP Row.—Oilbert litmus summoned John Patti- son for assault on the 19th May; Henry Einans summoned Edwin Cobb for a similar offence ; Gilbert Blinn' and Henry Flows were summoned by John FaWson ; Harriet Patti son. wife of JONI Patti3on and the daughter-in-law of Edwin Cobb summoned Henry "limns, John Pattison was summoned by Henry Birnas.—:The evidence show -4 that on Whit Monday , the above mentioned party met at :the " Whale Bone Inn," From some cause or other a row commenced after which a free tight followed Irt which several of the plaintiffs and defendants received severe blows. Most of them had had drink.—After cOnsiderable investigation the Bench dismissed the ease against Henry llamas by Mrs Pattlson, and the others were bound over to keep the peace for six months in sureties of £5. The costs In each case amounted to 123 61 which the defendants were ordered to pay.</p>
        <p>.4.4i1, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn Faotball Club was held on Thuredry evening at the Bethesda Lecture Ball for the purpose of receiving the secretary's and treasurer's report and to elect the efflux's for the ensu- ing year. Mr Imison presided, and there were also present :—Messrs C Po wnall (treasurer), J Ellison (secretary) W Jones (chairman of committee), A Abel (assistant; secretary) and others. There was a fair attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The report of the Secretary (Mr J Ejison) congratu- lates the members upon the excellent position the club occupied in the Northern League. To finish third in such a tournament was a highly meritorious perform- ance, especially considering that the club was most un- fortunate in regard to accidents to players. Only twice was tho team beaten in Yorkshire—by Hull and Hudders- field—and but, two clubs could claim dual victories, namely Widnes and Warrington. In the Northern Union Cup the defeat at Cast Mord in the third round was very unsatisfactory. The winning of the County Championship by Cheshire reflected the highest credit upon the Runcorn players, or whom no fewer than 11 had qualified for medals. Irt the Northern Lague and West Lancashire League the team had played 32 matches won 19, lost 11, drawn 2, and scored 37 goals, 49 tries- -221 points; scainst 22 goals, 30 tries-134 points. In the Northern fiction Cup, 4 games were played, 3 won and one list, scoring 5 goals. 5 tries-25 points against 1 goal 2 tries-8 point. The defence bad been the strongest in the Nnthern League. The chief scorer bad been J Butttrworth who had 14 tries and 5 dropped goals to his credit, H Farmer had kicked 15 goals. The balance sheet shows total receipts £1,114 6s Id, which includes suhseriorion and season tickets £152 ' 194 6 i:;:pate receipts, £624 7; 10,1 ; and Northern Union cup ties, £217 is 51. 'fbe chief items of expenditure are insurance of players, £53 3i 61; lost time, £407 ; referees and touch judges, £5l is 4d ; gatemen and police. £34 14s ; and refreshments, £735; ; footballs, jerseys, &amp;?„, £73 184 31; salaries, £2O 19g. The expenses of the Cheshire Junior Cup were £32 87d, and as the receipts amounted to no more than £119; 6d there was a loss on the competition of £2O 19s id. The yearl concluded with an overdraft: on PArr's Bank of £62 43 7d, but the assets nf the club exceeded the liabilities of the club by £129 124 3d.</p>
        <p>THE' " A "TEAM." With respect to the " A " team the report prepared by Mr Abel, stated it was the worst experienced for many years. Though the forward play was quite up to the mark the bacas were poor in the extreme. The ' early matches did not give satisfaction at all. From Febluary to November not a single match was won. Having, however, got a strong combination together by the end of the season they had the satisfaction of carrying off the Cheshire Junior Cup sad tho hand- some gold medals "flared by the Cheshire Committee. Too total number of matches played was 31. In the Lancashire Combination tie figures were : Played 19. wen 5, lost 12, drawn 2 ; scoring 11 goals, 25 tries, 97 points, against 23 goals, 35 tries, 151 points. Friendly matches:—Payed 7, won 5. bit 2, drawn none; scoring 9 goals, 13 tries, 57 points. against 8 goals, 4 triss, 23 points. Cheshire Junior Cup ,ies : —Played 5. won 3, lost none, drawn 2 ; scoring 6 goals, 7 tries, 33 points, against 2 goals, 2 tries, 10 noints. These figures gave a margin of 3 points againit the club and an average score of 6 points per tinatcb. A very pleasing feature at the end of last season was that the players willingly signed on for the next. (Applause). Mr T Jones,chairman of he Committee, proposed the adoption of the report. He thought it was eminently satisfactory. It was pleasing to think that Runcorn could still put a taam in the field able to cope with the best of Lmcashire and Y.srkshiro. He was pleased to think there were plenty of young players coming for- ward.</p>
        <p>Mr Linaker seconded, and the report was approved. THE TREB.SURER't4 REPORT. The Treasurer, Mr Pownall, in submitting his annual reeort thought a little comparison with last year's balance sheet would be most appropriate. This year there were 304 member compared with 290 last year being an increase of 14. This, though not a large number, was eminently satisfactory when losses and deaths were taken into consideration. They would re- member that Messrs R Abel and C A Speakman had died during the year while Messrs Jordan and Hamilton had left the town. The former gentle- man had, however, sent his subscription and still took a deep interest in the club. Joseph Nickson, another subscriber, captain of the " A" team had also died and though a player continued his subscription to the last. The subtcribers to the stand numbered 61 being a decrease of 14, but 39 season tickets had been issued for the year being an increase of 22. The total number of subscribers was 372 com- pared with 327 last year being an increase of 4-5. The amount received for the same was £152 113 63 being a net gain of £36 103 81. The gate receipts and the takings at the stand totaled £627 7s 10J,the receipts for the Reason last year being £527 us 10d an increise of £96 163. had it not been for the bad weather, ex- perienced last season -avh.,n important matches had to be postponed there would undoubtedly have been a mach greater return. The two postponed matches wt 3 Swinton and Broughton Rangers which were played at toe end of the season when intereaa in the competition was dead only realized £ll 31 51, whereas la:t year they realised £ll7 184 101 a difference of £76 15) 51. This addition to the income would have rendered it unnecessary to have asked Mr Taylor,the president, to have guaranteed the overdraft at P4rt'S. The Northers Union Cup Ties produced £217 la 51 or £37 153 3 less than the previ- ous season. On the debit ade £53 33 6.1 had beenpaid for insurance a difference of £25 7s 91 from It year. Travailing expenses amounted to £ll4 61 an increase of £1563 51. Lost time £35213 41,being £5B 8 I 101 in excess. This additional expenditure had been caused through the long distances to be travelied,and the players having to leave early each Saturday. Ths Cheshire Janiar Cup Competitien cost the club £32 8s 7d ; deducting £ll 91 61 Runcorn's proportion of tua gate and expenses, the nett amount was £2O 9s Id. This rather heavy expenditure was caused by two extra games having to be played with Stockport " A " team before a victory was declgred. The amount at the credit of tbe club was in all £72 2s Id la's than last year. The sum of £42 18141 had been written off for depreciation, and the valuation of the club property had been put as low as possible.</p>
        <p>Oa the motion of Messrs Lightburn and Weston, the report was approved. ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The following gm-lei:nen were appointed :—President, Mr S Taylor ; treasurer. Mr C Pownal ; secretary, Mr J ; asistant secretary, Mr A Abel ; auditors. Messra T C Cooper and E Loot ; captain (Ist team,) Houghton ; vice-cat t iin,B W,l;cer ; captain GI team), T Wflkinson,vice-nact sin, H Pre. —.</p>
        <p>. • The n Imes of the c9mmittee will appear in cur next issue.</p>
      </div>
