      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>DEFRAUDING THE RAILWAY COMPANY.-AN EXP.N.SIVE RIDE.</p>
        <p>At the iWarrington Police Court On Friday, before Mr Hepherd (in the chair) and other magistrates, James Whitby, Primrose Vile, Mill-lane, Heatley. was summoned -for travelling on the London and North-West,rn Hallway from Arpley to Latchford on May 9th, without previously paying the fare, with intent to avoid payment —Mr 'Henna prosecuted and said the delendant was la chemist's assistant and held a contract from London-road, Manchester, to Latchford. On the date in question he was seen to arrive at Arpley Statien by the :rain due at 3.4 p.m.from Manchester. He tendered 11c1 excess fare from Latcoford and this was accepted. For certain reasons be was wa'ched when be went back. He left Arpley Station by the 5.8 train but he did not takeaa ictiet to Latchford. On arrival at Heatley the tickets were c Alected and defendant produced his contract, but ten- dered no excess fare. He had therefore travelled from Arpley to Latchford without paying the fare (1;o1.) When seen afterwards about the matter defendant said his father gave himn money to pay the fare but he forgot to pay it. He had done the same thing b:fore, and: he had told the Station master about it afterwards, and his father had paid the station master.—Mr Timperiey. the station master at Heatley, said the defendant's father had never paid him money, but the defendant had on several occasions paid the excess fare.—A fine of lOs and costs (ill NO was indicted. WELL trzrovrx FOOTBALLER SUMMONED.—WIFE OBrAINS A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SEPARATION ORDER.</head>
        <p>SEPARATION ORDER.</p>
        <p>John Fish. the a-r-rir-g-6n footballer. who I resides at 6. Princess-street, appeared at the Ccunty Borough Police Court, On Friday, in answer to a summons charging him with asauiting his wife, Fanny Fish, on May 19th.— Mr Browne appeared for the complainant, and the defendant wes represented by Mr bteel.—At the outset Mr Browne intimated that he and Mr Steel had been consulting with a view to settling the case without calling evidence. He (Mr Browne) suggested that the defendant shout.' consent to a conviction for aggra- vated assault and a separation order.—Mr steel said there were reasons why it was not advisable that the defendant should consent too conviction, lie euggested that the com- plainant should withdraw the summons and then a deed of separation could be drawn up.—Mr Browne : The deeds are useless in the case of workingmen.—Mr Steel formally pleaded guilty to an aggravated assault----Mr Browne Infortned the Bench that the complainant was a fourbona weaver and could earn 13s a week. bbe had one eland. Defendant received 30s per week from the Football Club in addition to his wages from Metiers Fletcher Russell and Company by wh..iria he was employed. He was the leading football player. —Defendant said he had been earning only 15s per week dining the winter. He was a range titter end had been working only two or three days a week —Air Browne And then he gets paid by the Football Club—The Chairman: He makes more by football than by working.—Mrs Fish said she would be contrnt with 10s per .week aliznony.—The Bench ultimately made A separation order with an allowance of Lb per week, defendant to pay He costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FllDAT.—Before 35! Heoherd (in the chair), Councillors</p>
        <p>Smethurst and John Ronerts. and Mr Thornton. SLEEPINO Our.--John Thomas liolroyd was charged with being found sleeping in a stable at ;he " Bagle and Child yard that morning and was sent to gaol for 14 days. CIIATIGE OF THRFT DDIMISSED.—Mary Ann Campbell, 2, Oliver-street, was summoned for stealing three chnirs, a table, and other articles nf furn.tute value £2,the property o! James Bucliley Meltor, 22. Pinner's Brow on the 12th inst.— Mr for the defendant and pleaded not guilty. Mellor stated that on the date In question he gime home for his dinner at one u'elo,lc and found that alt the articles had been tiken.—A neighbour deposed to seeing the defen- dant, who is a married daughter or theicomplainant, take the furniture.—The defence was that a short time ago the defen- dant dyed with toe complainant and bought the Roots from several shops. he had now had a dispute with the complain- ant and consequently took the furniture away.—Rvidence having been given the magistrates dismissed the ease.</p>
        <p>IMPRISON:Q-1,-NT FOR WIFE Ai:leer:LT.—John Richardson. 41. Wellington-street, W:l3 summoned for assaulting his wife. Alice Richardson. on May 20th —Complainant stated nit her husb:nd, who was continually drinking, seized her by the hal, of the head and severely ill-treated h-r. She asked to be separated from him.—The magistrates sent the defend- ant to gaol for a month for the assault, and granted the wife a separation order with 7,6 d per week alimony.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Befo7e Mr F Monks (in the chair), Alderman Francomb, Councillor Smethurst, Messrs 6 Wright, Hay- ward, and Thornton</p>
        <p>Boys i.e TRoUßLr..—John Griffin. Buttermarket-strectand emes Byrnes. t 5, Tanner's.lane boys, were charged with stealing from the worleshod of William Rothweld blacksmith, Bowe-street, sometime between eight o'clock the previous Thursday night and 7.30 on Friday mort.ing. the sum of Is 9din copper.--Tha Chief ConstaNe sell the workshop was locked up at eight o clock on Thursday night, and at 7.30 the following morning, flr Bothwell found it had been entered by means of breaking the iron fastener on the doer, A box inside the shop. which Was secured with a lock, had been broken, and the amount of money mentioned in the charge abstracted from it. Information was given to the police, and the matter was inquired lath by Detective Barlow who, as the result of his investigations apprehended the prisoners' When charged they at first denied the offence. but subsequently Grilffin said he got into the shop, broke the box open with a bar of iron, ana stole the money. Byrnes said he woe in company with Griffin and received ninepeace his share of the money.—The moglstrate3 ordered Grifnn, who hscl previously been before the Court. to be confined in the cells for three days, and sen- encod Byrnes to twelve strikes with the biroh. WANTED To FirlllT.—Wil lam Dunbobbin, jun., 6, Parr stroet. and Thomas Moffatt 45, Lord Nelson-street, were summoned for using violent and abusive language towards each other in Horsemarket-street and Scotlanctror.d where- by a breach of the peace might hare been occ,ssioned.—Dun- bcbkin did not appear Turner said the defendants be- haved in a very disorderly manner. They used very bad language and wanted to fight.—The Chief Constable ex- plained that Dunbobbin was a soldier and at the time when the offence NVILA committed was on furlough. His leave had expired end he had had to njoin his regiment. —Defendants were each timid ss.</p>
        <p>MINOR L.A.3l:3.—WlZabetb Topping, 21, Warburton-street WWI fined 51 for h,tving been drunk and disorderly in Warburton-street on May 17th.--George Crompton, 23. Clitt^,n-street, William Taylor, 18. licheol-brow ; William Rowland, 28, School-brow, and Frederick Rowlinson. Crossley-street. were fined bleach for gaming with a box of dice ir: Hylands-yard on May 18th.Detendarus were in!ormel that they were liable to be seat to gaol Or three months without the option of a fine —Albert Robert., 18, Refuge square, and 1 homes Burgess, 108,P9erpoint-street. were tined 2s 63 each for playing' a game of chance called knock-over with tops for money " gdward-square. Bewsey. on may 18th. They were prosecuted:so that it might be publict7 known that the gars.e is illegal.—P C tawart proved the two last cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Bef ore Messrs H Neild (in the chair). John White, J M Bdelsten, and Joseph Knowles, CRITELTT TO A HORSE.—.Richard L Warburton, farmer, of Hatton, Was summoned for having on the 2ih May ill- treate i a horse by causing Mtn t, worked whilst in an unfit condition. Samuel Rutter. also of Hatton. and in Mr _ Is SocuritY in d sea snips on the toetay.s._ - - :doming</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>M'A CI ""</head>
        <p>M'A CI ""</p>
        <p>iTILE</p>
        <p>Small Pill. Small Dose. Small Pnes</p>
        <p>Rier—ii PILLS • -,,utely</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SCZODONT</head>
        <p>SCZODONT</p>
        <p>Will Sweeten tin:, Breath all day. and make an the difference bar eiOrclutely cure Sick Headache, tween— eilicusness, Torpid Liver, Indi- 6°04 Teeth and Bad Teeth, gest:cu. Ccnst:pation, Dizziness. IF/hi:4i Teeth and Yellow Teeth. :Furred To Igoe. They Touch the Pretty Teeth and Ugly Teeth er. Complete in Toilet et? Vat las sure Zest Pde Farizta uct* l'owdu,</p>
        <p>Warburton's serviee. was summoned for working the animal whilst in the condition deseribed.—P.C. 263 (Leigh) stated that he was on duty, on the 2.lth of May, irethe Chester-rood Walton Superior, when he met the defendant Rutter in caarge of a bay horse attachei to a light spring cart, which was empty. Ha spoke to him and found that the animal was very lame round the off-fetlock joint, which wee very much swollen. When he touched it the horse seemed to be in great pain. He subsequently saw the owner of the animal who said that, it had been destroyed that morning.—Mr Warburton said the animal had been in the same condition las seen by the officer for some years. He had no desire to work a horse which was not in a fit condition to be Worked. as he had three good ones in the stable at the time. It had since been destroyed.—The magistrates decided to dismiss the cue on payment of the costs. DOG OWNEM us TROUBLE.—The following persons were fined either for keeping dogs without a licence or for allow- ling them to be at large without a muzzle and collar :- 1 Alfred Smith, Walton Supellor, 2s fd and costs ; George Welsby (who did not appcar), Stockton Heath, is EH and costs ; Joseph Mooreroft, Fairhurst-street, Warrington, 3s 6d ; Joseph Millington, Marsh House-lane, Warrington, 3s 6d ; John Todds Latchford, 3s 6d. . .</p>
        <p>A CYCLIST'S CIVILITY.—CharIea Shaw, of Northwicb, who was represented by his brother, vras summoned for having on the 11th May ridden a bicycle on the footpath —Superin- tendent Bunion proved the case, and said the defendant was very civil and stooped when requested to do so —The Chair- man said that as the defendant was civil he would only ba fined 3s. _ .</p>
        <p>WITHOUT A LlOgren LiMP.—James Gordon, employed at Winwiek Asylum, was summoned for having on the 9th May, at Stockton Heath, ridden his bicycle without a lighted lamp attached to it at 12.3 U am.—The Clerk (Mr Henry Greensll) read a letter from the defendant, who stated that be was unable to be present as he had a Board meeting to attend at tha asyluna.—Defendaat was fined in Ise costs, 34 64. EGAMING AND TRESPASSI!.G.—James Closwickl New on- by-Dare3bury, John Malone and Patrick Donoghue, Hatton, wore summoned fcr gaming on a, pub tic highway with coins. Patrick Malone was eharged with eldlog the defendants to commit the offence. It appeared that one man had been engaged by the defendants to keep a sharp look out for the police," but as the police when the came on the scene were in plain elotnes they wore not recognized.—The defendants Were fined 54 each.—Peter Brocklelm-st pleaded guilty t tres- passing in search of game at Whitley OR the 2nd May on land in the p ion of Mr F H Aeton, without the con- sent of the owner —Mr Mynakyrne said ttrst on the day in question he saw the defendant on his shooting ground. lie found the snare produced. The defendant had a rabbit in his possession which he hart just caught. He had been snaring for years to witnasee kry,wledge. but on this occasion defendant was caught red-handed.—A fine of 106 ' and ccats was inflicted. _</p>
        <p>THE REV. B. W. LUMIII9 AND HIS Do.—Ada Humphries. of Walton (a domestic servant in the employ of the Rev If W Lumnsis) was summoned for having on the 2 th May allowed a dog to be at large at Stockton Heath without a muzzle.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.—The Rev B W Lurnmis. Unitarian minister. of Cairo-street, Chapel, War- rington. explained that it was a big St Bernard dog with a neck as thick as its head, and two or three times it had succeeded in eipping the collar. That morning while he (Mr Lurnmis)was at breakfast a tradesman came to the door. Humphries had left the house, all 4 there was no one in charge of the back premise,. The tradesman went to the (door and immediately afterwards the dog ran out, having slipped its collar. Witness went out and called the dog beck and locked it up again. He Woull take care that in nature no off .-nce of the kind was csmmitted.—ln answer to the Clerk, Mr Lummls stated that he was the owner of the dog. —The Clerk : Then you are responsibile.—The Rev B W Lumrnis : lam willing to be responsible. If you had sum- moned me instead of the girl I should have said "G uilty."— The Clerk : The Act says that the person in charge at the time is liable as well as the owner.—The Rev B W Lum mil Ada Humphries was not in charge of the dog. She had only met it accidentally. It had followed her. It was not that she was in charge, The Clerk : That does not make any difference She was in charge for the time being.—The defendant was fined 3s 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MoNnAr.—Before Messrs W Timmins (In the chair), E</p>
        <p>Imison and A R Norman.</p>
        <p>NEGLECTING A MAINTENANCE ORDER —Thomas Wood was charged on remand with neglecting an order made by the Bench in Joy last year requiring him to contribute Ts 6d per week to the maintenance of his wife and children,— Since the case had been before the Court on the 2nd inst defendant's wife stated she had received 16s which reduced the arrears to £7 24.—The Bench allowed the case to be with• drawn on defendant promising to keep up his payments. A GANG OF OAMEaTERs.—WiIIDIM Griffiths, 17, William Whitlow. 19. Herbert Shuyter, 23, John Warder, 21, and William Farrell. 18 ware summoned for gambling with coins on Sunday, 4th May, on Runcorn Hill —William Autrobus was summoned for aiding and abetting.—The police evidence showed that Constables Wylie and tiowlands in consequence of numerous con plaints went to Runcorn Hill on the morn- ing stated and there found in a hollow, the defendants, with about tea more, playing at pitch and toss.—Antrobus was acting as a scout and they aould not gat near for some time in coosequence. Eventtrilly tlas police officers reed° a rush and captured one of them from whom they got some of the names.—Griffiths, Farrell, and Antrobus pleaded not guilty but the chairman said the Barlett had no doubt but they were actually playing. Griffiths and Farrell were duel 10, each; Antrobas was discharged, and the other defendants were fined 7s 6d each. _</p>
        <p>PITCH AND Toss —Thomas MoDonald. George Shaw, Thomas Rushton and Samuel Johnson, four boys, were fined 26 6d each for playing Ditch and toss in Hartley-street on the 4th inst. ou the evidence of Sergeant Gregory. CARD PLATERS.—Henry Gardner. 19, and Tnotnas Jones, 20, were summoned tor gaming with cards for money on the Warrington New-road on the 14th Isday.—PstriCk Doyle was summoned for aiding and abetting.-3ergesut Gregory stated that the defendants were in company with about eight others playing "biliker," while Doyle was their look out—The case against Doyle was dismissed and the other defendants were fined 7s 61 &amp;We. ' " ' • "</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION Acr.—Fr nezlecting to send their chil- dren to school Thomos Woods and Owen Roberts were fined 76 63 each ; Daniel North and William Evans. 10J. SENT TO A TRAIN:NG SHIF.— OR the application of Mr Liehtborn, cleric to the Runcorn School Board and the evid- ence :of school attendance officer Bowyer, Cleirge Samuel Lea, son of Sarah Ann Lea, widow, was sant to the training ship Clio until he was 16 years of age. AN AAS AT LARGE.—John Pemberton on the evidence of Sergeant. Bennett, was._flned 23 6d for allowing his ass to</p>
        <p>stray on the highway at Sutton. CHIMNEY FruNG —For this offence Wiltiam RoMason and Ellen Olayes Were ordered ta pay the costs 43 ed. ALLEGED CRIIELTT TO A CAT.—George Duekett. a fish- monger, Church—street was summoned for cruelly ill-using a cat by urging a dog to worry the same at Runcorn on the 11th inst.—The evidence showed th it on the date mentioned Jane Fish, mite of John Fish, in consequence of what was told her by a man named Thomas Letther, went to the defendant's Ford. She there saw her cat being worried by the defendant's dog and heard someone hissing the dog ou. Defendant was Lhera with someone else and he refused to call his dog ofT —Sam Fish. son of Jane Fish, corroborated, —The defendant swore that he never set the dog on at all. Re heard someone shout cats and then found that his terrier had seized the cat by the neck and another do z by the back. It was no use calling the dogs off when the cat was quite dead. ie had lost over one hundred chickens through the depredations of cots—Other evidence, for and againsl, waa called the ease after a prolonged sal nt beini; dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DEAFNESS AND CATARRH.</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS AND CATARRH.</p>
        <p>The remarkable reports published by the Drouet Institut, show that nearly one halt cf the people who are Deaf attribute the cause of detective hearing to celis in the head or to the extension of catarrh,trom the nose and throat 0 the ear. The general opinion that such forms of Deaf nets are incurable is also the subject of comment in these reports and the scrooge- t argument against this opinion is contained in the fact that the Institute is able to publish the records of from 30 t ) 40 cured cases each month. That these cases are of the moat obstinate form is shown by the following :—Mr Robert Allan was deaf for 16 years. The DsafuEss came on after a cold in the head and gradually grew worse. The ncse and threat wore affected by catarrh, and there were occasional noises in th‘s ears. The c implete cure resulting from the Drouet Treatment is confirmed as follows :</p>
        <p>Pow, lanettown, Stromnei.. Orkney. ME rch 29tta, 1902.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,--Just a few lines to let you. k.,0%, that I am keeping all right still. lam telling lots of people at my cure, and thoy think it is a wonderful t Ana. Ido nvt know how to thank you for the trouble you 'have taken. —Youra truly, R. ALLIN. . _</p>
        <p>Those who are interested in deafness, or diseases of the ear, nose or throat, can ob:ain a free copy of the "Journal for the Deaf," with a " Suppteraeut taming detailed accounts of 43 cases cured during the past month, by addressing the Secretary, Drouet I9sti- tute, Regent's Park-road. London, N.W. Each Journal is aecompanied by a Patient's li.port Form winch enables applicants to receive full particulars for apply- ing the treatmont at borne. Any advice by correopoau- once is absolutely free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Great Sankey Schcol Board.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey Schcol Board.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the School Boatd was held in the Schools on Tuesday afternoon. There were pre,ent : Me•iers Halton (chairman), J Fulton Smith and Perks ; W D Jeans, jun., (clerk), and G Clarke (headmaster). The Clerk said tie balance in the bank in favour of the Board was £lB6 161 4d.—The Headmester presented the following report : Week ending May 3rd, on books 303, present 280, average , 253.3 fees 16s 2d ; May 10th, on hooks 300, present 281, average 263 1 teas lis 4d ; May 17th, on books 25. present 276. average 252.6 feeo 17s 2d ; May 24th, on books, 296, present 280, average 25P7 fees 16s 4 monthly average 255.1 fees £3 79.—The I Chairman said that be and the Clerk attended the meeting of school managers at Warrington, called to consider tbe question of a uniform holiday for the echoole, and were sorry to find that there was a good deal of friction between the different schools They were quite agreed to fall in with the views of !the majority, but one large school stood aloof and the whole scheme for a uniform holiday, he under- stood, had collapsed —The Clark read a letter from the Town Clerk of Warrington stating that the meeting unanimously decided that a uniform holi- day was desirable, and that all school managers should be urged to adopt a four weeks' holiday from July 11th to August 9113.—1 t was &amp;tided to give the children a holiday from the day before Corona- tion Day to the following Wednesday. The ques- tion of the summer holidays was left over to the next meeting.</p>
