      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LYMM MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL the Rain Spoils a Pretty Pageant.</head>
        <p>LYMM MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL the Rain Spoils a Pretty Pageant.</p>
        <p>Wretched weather:spoiled •.the'Lymm'if ay Queen Festival which took place on Thur.dayllast week. By holding the festival in Whit-week favourable climatic conditions rare generally ensured, but this year the hopes of the promoters were doomed to disappointment, for throughout the day rain fell in a continuous downpour. Notwithstanding this,three or,four thousand people visited the quaint Cheshire village, but real enjoyment was out of the question. The ground WlB soaked, the roads were thick with mud,and the characters in the procession presented a sorry spectacle. The untoward weather was the more regrettable because the organisers of the festival, whose presi- dent is the Rev C Ernest Watson, Congregational minister, had made special efforts to make it more successful than the fourteen filch have preceded if. The characters in the rocession were more numerous this year than be ore—as a matter of fact, they numbered about 150—and several new features were introduced. Headed by members of the Lymm Congregational School and Band of Hope, the procession, which was formed at the old Cross, included four " giant " policemen. 'characters familiarised by nursery rhyme from Beauty and the Beast to:the Babes in the Wool,youthful representa- tions of the seven Ed war ds of England, mounted characters representative of the Colonies and various nationalities, maypole; butterfly, and Italian fl )wer dancers, a number of cyclists in fancy dress, and a good show of comical characters. Last of all came the Royal cortege with the May Queen (Miss Gertie Rigby) preceded by the Crown and sceptre bearer, and followed by her two train-bearers and maids of honour.</p>
        <p>The procession proceeded to the footbald field, where the crowning ceremony was performed with a ceremonial that was deprived of all effectiveness by the rain. By this thus all the characters pre- sented a more or less bedraggled appearance, and one could not help feeling sympathy for the dis- comfort they suffered. The programme, however, was gone through before a fairly large number of people more or less protected by umbrellas and macintoshes who watched with apparent interest the Maypole dancing, and the carrying out of a programme which included acrobatic performances, flower dances, an exhibition by chair balancers, contortionists, and gymnasts, and roller skating extraordinary. The evening programme was gone through only in part, the majority of the visitors, who came from Manchester. Warrington. and intermediate places, wisely returning thither long before nightfall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
