        <p>FOR INFANTS, INVALIDS, AND THE AGED., THIS delicious and highly nutritive Food has been used with remark- able success in the rearing of Infants, and by delicate and aged persons for many years. It is distinguished by the ease with which it can be digested and absorbed, and is quite different from any other Food obtainable.</p>
        <p>Ranger's F3od is sold in Tins by Cl:omisis, eli., ovcPywhore.</p>
        <p>.fse</p>
        <p>Presentation to a Warrington Works Foreman.</p>
        <p>I The employes in the-eiiii.lie-e.ring department at ' the works of Messrs N Greening and Son. Limited, Scotland-road, assembled the ether evening for the purpose of presenting Mr Charles F. Taylor, late foreman of the department, who has received the appointment of assistant works manager at Messrs C 11 Parsons and Co, engineers, Newcastle-on-Tyne, with a hanisome Davenport writing desk. Mr W Payne presided, and, after thanking the men for the honour they had done him in asking him to occupy the chair, called upon Mr. Armstrong to make the presentation.—Ar. Armstrong gave an outline of Mr Taylor's career with the firm, and said how sorry they all were to lose him, although glad that he was taking up a better position. It would be very gratifying to Mr. Taylor to know that all in his department showed their esteem by subscribing to the testimonial, and he was sure he voiced the feeling of all present when he wished him " good health and a prosperous career "in his new sphere of labour. Mr Armstrong said he could not let an occasion like this pass wiilout a word of advice to the apprentices, and told them to work hard anti study so that when similar positions opened out they would be prepared to fill them.— Mr Taylor,chanked the men most sincerely for the handsome pressnt and the kind expression of their esteem and goodwill. He should always treasure the present and look back on the happy years be had spent amongst them. He had been there a little over eleven years, and felt the parting very much. He thanked them for the help they bad given him, and hoped they would give his successor, Mr F Monks, the same help anill he was sure they would work happily together. A I vote of thanks to the committee closed the pro- ceedings.—The desk, of walnut with brass mount- ings, was supplied by G and J Weaver, Bridge- street, and had a silver plate with the following in- scription : "Presented to Chas. F Taylor (fore- man) as a token of esteem and. goodwill from the employes in engineering department, Messrs N Greening and Sons, Ld., Warrington,on his leaving the firm, Ma 23rd, 1902."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington are still " going strong," and on Satur-1 day accounted for Liverpool in no half-hearted fashion. It is in the attack that the main factor of Warrington's strength lies, but on Saturday there were some splendid batting achievements by several members of the team, as the substantial total of 155 (innings declared) goes to show. The vigour of the attack is demonstrated by the fact that the Liverpool eleven were all dismissed for the small aggregate of 44, leaving a margin in favour of the Warringtonians of 111. Lynes was in brilliant form with the ball, and accounted for 7 wickets at a cost of 17 runs. With regard to the batting, E. A. Connor performed the most striking feat of the afternoon, his innings being productive of 74. He hit freely all round the ,wicket, iraparting much vigour into his strokes. His &amp;id hits included nine fours and four threes. A meed of praise must also be accorded to P. B. Hawtin, who was responsible for a capital 37, which included four boundary strokes. J. Gibbon contributed 19 in praiseworthy style.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool team was captained by the Anglo- Australian, IL G. Garnett, and included two profes- sionals Goodwin and Oldknow. Mr. Bibby won the toss, and Warrington batted first on a wicket which for an hour was quite easy, but under the influence of the sun became exceedingly difficult in the later stages of the match E. A. Counor and P B Hawtin were opposed by Goodwin and Oldknow. The first-named batsman soon got to work, and after placing Goodwin for three he hit Oldknow for four and three from succes- sive balls. The score rose rapidly, Hawtin playing a cautious game, scoring the major portion of his runs on the leg side, whilst Connor hit all round the wicket in his well-known lively style.</p>
        <p>At 35 B C Harrison took the ball from Goodwin, but both batsmen showed a partiality for the new bowler, with the result that he crossed over and took the ball from Olknow whilst A E Kidman took up the attack at the town end. Kidman's deliveries came in for some rather rough treatment, and the score rose rapidly. Ringing cheers greeted Connor when he drove Harrison for four and the telegraph board signalled that he had made 50. Runs came apace and the original bowlers resumed. Twice Hawtin placed Goodwin to the leg boundary, atnd the century was hoisted.</p>
        <p>At 107 the long partnership came to an end, for Hawtin, failing to get a thorough hold of one from Goodwin, was taken at deep mid off. J Callon came next and had only a brief stay, adding three when he was bowled off his pads. Lynes came next and some tall hitting wee seen. After Goodwin had faHed to hold a big hit far out Oldknow failed to catchua skier, but at 123 the professional played on.</p>
        <p>Disasters now befel the locals, for Briggs was sent back at the same total, whilst at 124 Connor's long inn- ings came to a close, Garnett taking an easy catch at the wicket.</p>
        <p>The bowlers now made the ball turn, and the bats- men were often in ' difficulties. J Gibbon forced matters, but both J Bibby and B Silcock were soon dis- posed of. Gibbon was taken at point at 153, and the aggregate reached 155 when the closure was applied.</p>
        <p>Liverpool were left with an hour and a half in which to save the match, and commenced their task with H G Garnett and T S Cole, the bowlers being J W Bibby and Lynes. A disastrous start was made, for with only three runs on the board Cole was smartly run out by Podmore. A R Hughes joined his captain, and twice confident appeals were made against Garnett for a breach of rule 24.</p>
        <p>Runs were obtained with difficulty, and at 22 Hughes had his off stump knocked out of the ground by Lynes. A loud cheer greeted the dismissal of Garnett, who failed completely to time one from the home profes- si nal.</p>
        <p>Following this success, Lynes carried everything before him, and after Hampson had taken a fine catch at th 6 wicket, Kidman was palpably leg before the following over. Edmondson added three and was bowled at (35,) and at 41 a smart return bylßriggs sent (YDwyer back. E Ashcroft and Goodwin were bowled by successive deliveries, and Oldknow whipped in with only five minutes to play. , '</p>
        <p>Connor, who had relieved Bibby, had the new comer in difficulties, and at 44 bowled his middle stump out of the ground.</p>
        <p>-- Warrington's improved form is evidently attracting attention, for the attendance of spectators on Satur- day was the largest which has been seen on the Arpley enclosure for some time. Should the present good form continue, the team will no doubt receive that measure of public support which is so essential to its success.</p>
        <p>Warrington second eleven did even better than the senior contingent, their Liverpool opponents being dis- posed of by the overwhelming majority of 125. The Warringtomans went to the wicket first and when the total had reached 185 for the loss of seven wickets the innings was declared. S H Bishop, (78 not out) E Pier. point 456) and T Houghton (15) were mainly res- ponsible for this highly creditable aggregate. In an hour's time the Liverpudlians were dismissed for 67, the bowling of Bennett, Houghton, and Pierpoint work- ing great havoc.</p>
        <p>An exciting game was witnessed between Sankey Wire Mills and Platt Bridge I.M , the Wire Mills eVen- tually running out winners by the narrow margin of five runs. R Cook (24) gave the best batting display for the visitors, whilst Burton was in first rate form with the ball, taking seven wickets for 19 runs.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists suffered defeat at the hands of Edge Green,ll3 being the adverse majority. The second eleven had to bow the knee to Stretton, who gained a victory to the extent of 58 runs. For the winners Newsll (28), Ellison (13), A Jones (11), and J Broady (10), did best service with the bat, J Callon (11), being the top scorer for the Baptists.</p>
        <p>Crosfleld's had an easy task in defeating Warrington Wesleyans. Batting first they compiled the total of 74, to which the Wesleyans responded with 41. Dutton (32), Unsworth (13) and Dutton al) batted well for the winners, as did F Light (19) and Hewitt (11) for the Wesleyans.</p>
        <p>-- • Peter Walker and Son, timited opened their ground when the sergeants of the Ist V.B. South Lancashire Regiment were the visitors. The home team gained a brilliant victory, as the scores-101 tinnings declared) and 27—show. .T Milner (22w E Allen (12i and J Cain (11) made acceptable batting contributions for the winning team, whilst capital bowling displays were given by T Wright (3 wickets for 4 runs) and T Allen (4 wickets for 8 runs.)</p>
        <p>Cycling. An inquest was held at Oxford on Saturday on the body of James Dearlove, aged seventy-seven years, a retired brewer,who was knocked down by a cyclist and died from shock following the injuries he sustained. Kathleen Kelly, a student of Lady Margaret Hall, said she was cycling with a friend on the I/Bey-road. Whilst placing her hatband in her pocket and looking down she suddenly felt a great shock and she then found she had knocked the old man to the ground. A verdict of "Accidental death "was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>. __.._.. Mr Stanley Wilenn, deputy Town Clerk of War- rington, was one of the candidates selected to appear before the D)ncaster Town Council, on Tuesday, for the dual appointment of Town Clerk and Borough Coroner. The salary is £540 per annum. There was originally a large number of candidates for the position, but it was reduced to six. , The following candidates appeared before the Cotuitil :—Mr R A D Tovey, solicitor, Doncaster; Mr C G Brown, Cardiff; Mr G 0 Plant. Darlington; Mr 8 Wilson, Warrington; Mr G E Bridges, Cheltenham; Mr F Collis, Stoke-on-Trent. In the last round but one the voting was Tovey, 12; Wil- son, Warrington, 9. Eventually Mr Tovey received 17 votes, and there being none against him he was declared elected. Mr Tovey was a member of the Town Council, and rebigned his seat to make the application.. _</p>
        <p>- .61r Frank Booth, of Sankey-street, has been appointed organist of St. John's Church, Higher Walton, in place of Mr Busby, who is removing to Buxton. Mr Booth, who is an accomplished per- former on the instrument, has officiated as organist of St. James' Church, Latchford, for 12 years. Daresbury School Board Election, Mr George F Ashton, the returning officer in the Daresbury United District School Board election has lamed a circular to the effect that, as lessrs John E Seeker and P Tickle have withdrawn their candidature in the above election, "the remaining candidates will be declared elected and that no poll will be takeniThe new,Board will therefore consist of the following seven gentlemen :—Th owes Billing- ton, farmer, Newton-by-Daresbury; Thomas Smith Burgese,estate agent,Walton Superior; John Cooper, corn merchant, Walton Superior; Peter Gleave, provision dealer, Moore : Charles William Phillips, licensed victualler, Ring o' Bells, Diresbury ; Rev Frederick Clifton Smith, Daresbury Vicarage ; Richard Latham Warburton, farmer, Hatton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PROPOSED RIFLE CLUB* An Opportunity for would-be __,....... Marksmen.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED RIFLE CLUB* An Opportunity for would-be __,....... Marksmen.</p>
        <p>A rifle club, with the title "Appleton Rifle Club.") proposed to be formed, and has in tact taken deDnite shape, far Mr T R Lyon is announced 89 president. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and Colonel Ridgway, V.D-. vice presidents, while a clmmittee has been got ter gether composed of Messrs V B Davies, Hugh Darby - shire, J Ellieon.A Lister Kaye, Seger Owen, A Pimblnar J E Seeker, J Timiley, A Warburton, and J J Whitley The bon treasurer is Mr C Maclver, Bold-street, the boa secretary (pi° tern) Major Roome, Drill Hall, and tee armourer, Mr T Daintith. Toe details of the scheme which have been circulated are as follow This club is about to be formed for instruction in the use of the military rift: and the encouragement 0' marksmanship generally amoinst Its members. in order that in times of national emergency the knoso ledge so gained may. if required, be of some assistance ice tbe defence of the country. The club is primarily instituted for the benefit of those reeidents in the neighbourhood. who from various causal are unable to joist any local military body, and thereby avail themselves of a more advanced military education, but members of local military bodies are also eligible for membership. T. H. Lyon, Esq., has most generously provided tin site for a Range (Appleton Quarry) to the Club, all promised a 'donation of £lOO towards its constructioo. 'with the sole provision that the tenants of the Appleton Estates shall be admitted as members, with a reduced subscription . A good aodpiafe Range on the above site, for a lenglil of 100 yards (quite sufficient for the purposes of Elle club), has been designed b 7 Lieutenant Coloael H Crook, who is an expert in such matters, ana who in his report says On the whole 1 think Well of your club scheme, and it is tortunate that you have so favourable a site." It is proposed to provide reguis- tion and moving and vanishing targets for military rifles and suitable targets for rook and other miniature riles and for revolver shooting.</p>
        <p>A certain number of Military It!fles will be purzill by the Club for the free use of its members. Pri, rides may also be used, if passed by the Commit The services of an Instructor and Markers, &amp;a., tor ra duties will be secured.</p>
        <p>At present the membership and subscriptions of the dub will be arranged as follows :—Appleton Hall estateS (cottagers), 2s 61 per annum ' • (farmers. &amp;c), 53pet annum ; Stockton Heath, Walton, Daresbury, ace Grappentiall districts (100 members), 103 per annum ; Warrington (200 members), 101 per auburn. The committee decide all applications for mernbershiPf and have power to decline any application. No one nev 16 years of age will be eligible for membership. Sub- scriptions to be payable on election and subsequentii annually on Ist January. Shooting Dave, Bye-laws, and Club Regulations will be arranged by the committee. If a full membership of the Club is guaranteed it i$ hoped that the range will be opened by Mrs Lyon at the beginning of June next. A ladies' branch will be formed later, of which Mrs Lyon bas kindly consented to become lady president. Any donations towards the purposes or the Cicti (which will be most gratefu ly received) or subscriptiong are to be sent to C Maclver, Esq., Bold-street, War- rington. and applications for membership. ecqtziries • &amp;c,, to Major Rowse, Drill Hall, Warriagtoa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DEATh OF MR. T. HAWTHORN.</head>
        <p>DEATh OF MR. T. HAWTHORN.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will deeply regret to he of the death of Mr Thomas Hawthorn, who pasE away at his residence, 1, Lovely-lane, at half-pi nine on !zaturday morning. The deceased gent man, who was in his fifty fifth year, had been c( fined to his bed for a considerable time suffari from heart trouble, Dr, Naden being his medil attendant. The late Mr. Hawthorn vc</p>
        <p>born in Glasgow on the 28th of May, 1847, 80d came to Warrington from Edinburgh in 1860. 118 wee for some years a glass cutter,aud officiated a 9 Secretary of the Flint Glasecutters' Society; but for about a quarter of a century be had been ill business in Lovely-lane as a baker and provisioo dealer. He was a deacon of Wycliffe Cougrege- tional Church and took an active interest in tbe work of the church, the Sunday school, the P.S. Society, the Band of Hope and other temperance movements. He was also treasurer of Wycliq Cockbedge Mission By all the workers associate," with Wycliffe Church, Mr Hawthorn's loss will be keenly felt. The deceased gentleman also took' great interest in the work of Wycliffe Day Schools, being one of the raanager_°' lln politics be was a Liberal. He a member of the Council of the Liberal Associatiou, , vice-cbairman of the Whitecrose Ward Committee. I and e member of the committee of the Free church Council. He also took an active part in the forma' tion of the Trades and Labour Council many years ego,and was a director of the Ist Warrington Star- Bowkett Building Society, so it will be seen that his life has been a busy and a useful one. 10 Wycliffe Church, on Sunday raorning,the Rev Joh° Yonge made a sympathetic reference to the late Mf Hawthorn who had laboured so long and so faite- fully in connection with the church. It vote of condolence with the bereaved relatives Wee _ _</p>
        <p>palsied at the meeting of the 13.§..A.—&amp;i.e.iv. Sunday afternoon. Mr Hawthorn leaves a wide gad six children—four sons and two daughters with whom the deepest sympathy ,is expressed their sad bereavement.</p>
        <p>IHE FUNERAL. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on TueScitv: afternoon, amid many manifestations of regret. 'f•a° Rev. John Yonge impressively conducted the service' the principal mourners were 51931111 JOEIVI2 Hawthorof John Longth-w Hkwthorn, Charles Edward Hawtharl cud Harold Hawthern (sane), and the Misses Helen ar'.; Mary Hawthorn (daughters). Mr Clark. leauffal",::, (brother-in-law), Mr C H Longsbaw (nephew), Mr JOl,- Portitt (brother-io-law), Mr Edna d Porritt (nepheO! Hitchio, London, Mr John Longehaw (brother-in-law; and Mr Harry Longshaw (nephew). Among others a' the graveside were Alderman Roberts (aeputy-mayot), ' Councillors William Roberts, 'll. It Roberts, Brougli? Barton, James Evans and Peacock; Mr J W Hsyttlairu J.P., deacons of Wycliffe Cburcb,drepresentstivea of OS Sunday school, the Si ar-Bowkett Building Society, 110 the Wllitecrose Liberal Ward Committee. A large nuta' bar of beautiful floral tributes had beat forwarded, jo" eluding one each from the Liberal kesocittiOn, deacons rf Wycliffe Churob, the infints' school W;cliffe, and the larger schoil.</p>
        <p>- Water Polo.—St. Helens v. Warrington-.ot, On Tuesday night these teams met at "fe, 'Boundary-road Bath,St Helens in a friendly fistu'ro In the first half the St Helens men, playing fr°,/ the shallow end, had things entirely their own 111:13-re and at the interval led by five goals to nil. game was more even nfterwards,but the Saints bewg the tipper band, scoring twice to Warringt°'4, single goal. scored by Asocon. The scorers tor 1, Helens, who won by seven to one, were Sharroc'" (3), Walker (3), and Lomax (1).</p>
