      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>UNITARIAN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>THE UNITARIAN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Paper by the Rev. E. W. Lummis.</p>
        <p>The most interesting part of the proceedings on Thurr- day last week in connection with the British and Foreign Unitarian Association's anniversary in Lonoon was a conference, at Iwhich a series of papers recording the writers' experiences in the rejection of ether creeds and faiths and the acceptance of Unitarianismwere recorded. Dr Blake Odgers, K.C., who presided, gave the Con- ference pleasure by announcing that the King had com- manded that toe President of the Associstion, the Rev R A Armstrong, should be invited to the" solemnity of the Coronation ' at Westminster Abbey on June 26. The first paper sub:flitted WIG' by the Rev E W Lummis, of Warrington, who gave his experience of the change from Anglicanism to the Unitarian faith. The Church of England. he said, was a winsome mother, with more than a common power of making her chil- dren love her, and those who had felt themselves forced to quit her ministry looked back upon her with wistful eyes, not as fugitives, but rather as exiles. To them the Church of England was not merely a department of the State, the creature of Tudor and S;uart enactments, or merely a linsey-woolsey web of ill-sorted theologies woven in the Erastian loom. She was a great society of worshippers, which by the study and practice of more: than twelve centuries had attained an eminent supremacy in the art of worship. The credentials of the Church depended upon the doctrines which she affirmed in her creed. He could never again belong to any church whose bas's of membership was the profession of a creed. In Unitarianism.he had found more than peace for:his own soul ; he had found a cause which made for the best good of humanity. To that cause he bad dedicated himself, and in its service, with Goa's help, he would pass his life. (Applause.) Other gentlemen who related thnir experiences were die Rev W Wooding, of Stoke N:lwiogton, Lond,ri " Congregationalist to Unitarianism ; " the Rev G V Crook, of Taunton, " Methodist to Unitarian : " and the Rev L de Beaumont Klein, of Liverpool, " Roman Catholic to Unitarian." One of the best-received papers was that of the Rev Alexander Webster, of Aberdeen, who stretched his conversion from Presbyterianism to Unitarianism. The Chairman moved a vote of thanks to the writers of the several papers. Mr Armstrong, the newly appoin'ed president, in seconding the moticn, referred to the papers as a splendid joint deliverance to send to others who might be seeking the same rest; and peace to help them on their way. The vote of thanks was cordially awarded. . . . . .</p>
        <p>At a naPeting which was open to ministers only Mr Hodgson Pratt submitted a paper on " Tentative sug— gestions, based on personal exp:rience, as to some of the Cluses which have restricted the progress of Uni- tarian churches." The day's proceedings concluded with a conversszione at the Royal Institute of Painters in Water Colours, at which Dr Blake Odgers and Mrs Odgers held a large reception.</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE AT WYCLIFFE. OWEN —KERSHAW. On Thursday last week, at the Wycliffe C Mgre- Rational Church, in the presence of a large company of interested relatives and friends, the marriage ' was celebrated of Mr Charles rhomas Owen, eldest son of Mr and Mrs Joseph Owen, Summerhill- terrace, Lovely-lane, Warrington, and Miss Emily I'4lthal Kershaw, elder daughter of Mr and Mrs James Kershaw, Kuutsford-road, Latchford. The ceremony was performed by the Rev J Yonge. As the guests were assembling, Mr W H Payton, Mae. Bac.. the organist of the church,with the choir of which the bride has been long asso- ciated, played appropriate nuptial music. As the bride entered the church on the arm of her father, who gave her away, it was seen that she was handsomely attired in white silk,witb Valenciennes lace, and a hat ot white satin and chiffon and plumes. Her shower bouquet was composed of lilies, camellias, white roses, and lilac. This together with a necklace of pearls was the gift of the bridegroom. She was attended by three bridesmaids, Misses Mabel Kershaw, sister, Cis,de Owen, sister of the bridegroom, and Is abet Moffat of Preston. The two former were in m uslin detain and black velvet picture hats and the latter in a Wedgewood blue dress and white chiffon hat with blue flowers. Their choice shower bouquets were the gift of the bridegroom. Mr Joseph Owen, brother of the bridegroom, acted as best mac. On the con- clusion of the ceremony Mendelssohn's " Wedding March " was played. Subsequently a reception was held at the Temperance Hall. Latchford, and this was numerously attended. The catering was in the hands of Mrs John Atkinson. The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to Bride, necklace of pearls Mr and Mrs Kershaw, cheque Mr and Mrs Owen, bedroom outfit Mabel and Harold, tea service °bode, crockery Cyril, crockery and brasses Mr Joseph Owen and Miss jowett, upholstered chair Mr and Mrs bavig Owen, pictures Reginald and Arthur Owen. flower vases Mr and Mrs Owen (Manchester), china Mr and Mrs Jackson (Wallasey). cheque Mr ens Mrs James Jackson (Olitberoe), ornament Mr John Westgarth, bedroom furniture Mr and Mrs Westgarth (Sheffield), table cutlery The Misses Westgarth (Sheffield). dinner cruet Mrs Thomson tAltrincham). cutlery Mr and Mrs if Thomson (Altrinchara), dinner service Mrs Tonstall (St Helens), brass stool Mr F Tunstall (outhport)toilet service Mr and Mrs Moffat (Preston), silver cake basket Mrs Crozier and Miss Moffat. hand-painted fire ecrem a photo frames Misses Isabel and Katie Moffat, trinket set Mr and Mrs Gabbstt (Liverpool). bedding Miss Gabbatt (Liverpool, china jam dishes Wycliffe Christian Rudeavour Society, copper floerer pot and silver sugar tongs Rev J and Mrs Yonge, Longfellow's poems Misses SC and F Wallington. linen basket Mrs E Wallington. hot watering Mr Piekavance and family. biscuit barrel and silver toast rack Mr and Mrs If ‘vnes, toilet service, clock and fire-irons Mr J Means and Miss Perrins. cutlery and bracket Mr and Mrs John Atkinson. bride's cake and towels Miss Atkinson, tidies Mr and Mrs fi H Pearson, fish carver Mr and Mrs W ii Payton, ornaments Mrs Payton, wine glasses Miss M. Payton. silver jam spoon Mr and Mrs A H Burgess. silver tea spoons Mr and Mrs A R Longshaw, vases Miss Winstanley, pin cushion Mrs Shaw, dressing jacket Mr W Beswick and family, breakfast cruA Mr J Albinson. washing utensils Mr and Mrs Adams. globes and picture Mrs Gleave and Miss L Wallington, trinket sef. Miss Dykes, cushion (r.r and Mrs (5 Morritil. brush and Crumb tray Mr Geo. Wilding. fruit dish</p>
