      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>OLDEST RAILWAY STATION IN THE</head>
        <p>THE OLDEST RAILWAY STATION IN THE</p>
        <p>" Patti-Croft," wished to board toe train, he %vette] go up to one of the sc-called policemen, who were stationed all along the line about a mile apart, and this official would stop the next second class train by waving his red flag by day or his light by night. The absence of intermediate etations at this early period and the fact that, at the Liverpool end, successive eniorgements, alterations, and improvements have completely swept away everything belonging to the original terminus there, while, at the Manchester end, tile line was diverted near the present Ordsall-lano Station and carried on to the Exchange Station, ex- plain why things have been allowed to remain pretty much as they were at Liverpool-read. It may here be said that the old Stockton and Darlington line, the only one constructed previous to the gLiverpeol and Man- chester Railroad, was more in the nature of a traml line than a railroad, and had no pmsenger station ; most certainly none which exists to-day. There can, therefore, be no room for doubt as to this Manchester' Station in Liverpool-road being really the oldest existing railway statien in the world.. On:y second in interest to the station itself is the remark- able old bridge by which the line is carried over Water-street. This is the oldest ircn girder railway bridge, if indeed it he not the oldest railway bridge of any kind, It was thought a wonderful work, and is described by a contemporary as the beautiful flat bridge, supported by cast-iron beams and partly by a row of Doric columns, which form colonnades and give its internal arrangement the appearance of a classical temple." The great stone bridge of two arches over the Irwell a little further on, a fine massive structure,is also an original constructien, save that it has been widened on the:north side. A curious feature in connectien with this bridge is the large number of of stalactites pendant from the soffits of its arches ; they range in length from six inches to upwards of a foot, and 'have ap- parently been formed by lime-impregnated water find- ing its way tietween the joint s of the masonry. The only place from which this bridge can be properly seen is on the river tow-path. A" padorama " painted on 10,000 square feet of canvas, and designed " to enable citizens of the Metropolis whose avocations had not permitted them to see a railroad to know what they were like," and exhibited at Baker-street rooms,London, in 1834, showed these two bridges just as they now exist.—" Manchester City Nerve."</p>
