      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WORLD.</head>
        <p>WORLD.</p>
        <p>Manchester's Interesting Relic. It does not appear to be generally known that Man cheiter possesses the oldest railway station in the world* Some few Americans are dimly cognisant of the fact and* at rare intervals, make it the object of a pilgrimage. This most interesting building, the original terminus of the Liverpool and Manchester Railroad. has scarcely been alters ld P,t all since its erection in,1830. The princi- pal front is in Liverpool-road, and together with the company's yard and warehouse, occupies the corner site at the junction of that road with Water-street. The main features are a large hall on the ground floor for the reception and booking of passengers and their lug- gain, a broad flight of steps up which everything ihad to be carried, and by which the passengers attained the waiting-room on the upper floor, thig being on the same level as the rails. There is no raised platforrx ,but simply a level platform of York flagstone. A large shed, mainly;of wood, extends for some distance beyond the station proper, but the part at which passengers would enter or leave the traits is entitely unprotected froth the weather. Opposite the passenger station are the goods warehouses, whicb, at the time of their erection, were thought very handsome, and wonderfully convenient , because they could be provided with a number of large doors through which the waggons could be rolled for 11 loading and unloading. It was in one of these ware- houses that a grand luncheon was prepared for the great is wonderful water proofer for neeple who took part in the openieg ceremonies of the. BOOTS &amp; HARNEsS. Sof- line in Septeinher. 1830, but the sad accident to poor % I'• e " ' e that very tens and preserves the leather Huskisson cast such a damper"pon the after proceedinas, .. et Pleasant odour allow pelfsh- of it A fr,w.and those the Vast notable.catne;t,e partake v.tTLD MEDAL log with blacking. . contemporary writer described the station as b "ia spacious building with a Gsecian front." Every- thing architectural was Grecian in those class. The AWAtiDS. mit "4 Tins 2c1.. 6d., if. 67'2/6 of Boot Maers a 4 k, SdePers, Ironmongers following eittract indicates some of the peculiarities of earlY Bl railway days in anchester :---.' We will suppose l' ' London. ' to ghteoasove,beo we traariri peol vti edt i withck et s , us h iacth the l I bbec foundokin te ' ,4 aec Martsfaetarr End DUZ^VIa, el ', . office,t the stranger having a o d 130114 with the seats allotted to uto : proceed up a flight of stairs and pacious apartment use 1 by passen- gers bdi ourselves numberedth eear r i aia a B gars waiting for any of the trains. It is com- modiously fitted up for comfort and convenience, On r : sheiast beingsmelo d i 0 fuosuenall (there were no screeching steamd, and the Bugleman having soun- dedwhistles in those happy times), we shall find ourselved proceeding, at an easy rate at first, which gradually increases as we cross the Irwell and pass the engine- houses and workshops of the company on ou'r right hand." The trains travelled at the rate of 20 miles an hour, and did the whole journey in one hour and a half. Toe fare in first-class trains, " four.insido " coaches, royal mail, was 63 6d, or with six-inside,. glass coaches, 6s. ; by second-class trains, glass coaches, 6;.; open carriages. 44 61 There were then practically no intermediate stations, in the modern ernes of the word. If a passenger, say at</p>
