      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Newton police buildings on. Monday morn- ing before Mr T J Gillespie, Thomas Delaney was charged with being drunk at Earleitown, and in default of paying a fine of 53 and costs was com- mitted to gao! for 14 days. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST AN EARLESTOWN MAN.</p>
        <p>At /Sewton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, Thomas Jones, of Houghton-street, Earlestown, was charged with indecently assaulting a young woman named Kate Eliza Webster, aged seventeen, of Clarence- street, Newton Common, on the 2.6 th inst. Ile was remanded to Warrington. THE M ISSING EARLESToWN SCHOOLBOYS_ FOUND.</p>
        <p>The two little boys named Capper and Simpson, aged four and five years respectively, living with their parents in Manchester-row, Vulcan Village, Earlestown, who were missing from their homes since Tuesday, were on Wednesday evening found by the police at Abram, who communicated with the Earlestown police, and the parents of the children on Thursday went to Abram for the wan- derers,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>_ We understand from Mr W Emmett of the Wagon Works, Earlestown that the Board of Direc- tors of the London and North Western Railway Company have given a donation of £5O to the local fund in connection with the Coronation festivities to be held locally. This, together with the days pay which the Company are granting all the work- men at the Viaduct Works will mean an appreci- able contribution on the part of the Company, MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL AT EARLESTOWN. The arrangements for this festival which is to be held on the Market Ground, Earlestown, on Satur- day, are progressing very favourably, but the whole success of the affair depends upon the weather. Given a fine day,the festival should be financially a success, and the proceedings on the Market ground will be worth seeing. A monstre procession will be arranged, and a parade of the principal streets of the town will take place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GOOD TEMI`LARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMI`LARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge held their meeting in the Wargrave Hall on Monday night, Bro Cutler, CT, presiding. At 830 the meeting was opened to the public, and the following programme was given before a good attendance of members and visitors : Song "While the days are going by," Sister Owens; phonograph selections, Bro Greening; song "The Vacant chair," Sister Rowley ; recitation, Bro J Warburton ; "The song that reached my heart," Sister lull; reading by Bro Clarke. Further phono- graph selections by Bro Greening brought a pleasant evening to a close. BOWLING HANDICAP, The annual bowling handicap for the Viaduct Institute Committee's prizes took place on Saturday afternoon. The handicap was open to all the mem- bers of the Institute, and the day being beautifully fine, there was a very large entry. There were 101 entries. and towards the finish the play was pretty close. The prizes took the torm of orders on trades- men, and the following were the winners :—Jogeph Spencer Ist, ..£1 ss; 2nd P Caldwell. 15s; 3rd W Fowlds, is Od ; 4th J Withington, 53 6d; sth T Glover, 4s; 6tn James Bedworth, 43; 7th James Pope, 4s. (Neu:go- ACCIDENT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning a cyclinz accident hap- pened with serious results to :Mr James Camm, tobacconist, Earlestown. He was about to cross the rcadwal at the bottom of Bridge-street when he saw a cyclist approaching. There seemed to be some indecision on the part of the latter, and in order to show him to pass Mr Comm remained stationary with the result that the cyclist pitched into him knocking him down. He was unable to rise and as it was found he was badly injured, a couple of men conveyed him to Dr Valentine's surgery, who found on examination that he had dis ocated his ankle and broken a small bone of the foot. He did not pass a very good night but he I was a little better yesterday.</p>
        <p>THE PROPO-SED COTTAGE HOSPITAL. The arrangements for the forthcoming bazaar in connection with the above are proceeding apace and there is every expectation that it will be a success. The dates fixed apon are July 17, 18 andl 19 and it will be held on the ground of the Earles- town Football club, the Committee of which have very kingly placed the ground at the disposal of the Bazaar Committee for the three days. This will be the first open air bazrar held in the district, and its novelty will no doubt attract many visitors. There will be plenty of amusements for the public and some excellent entertainments under cover and if the clerk of the weather will only smile propiti- ously on the three days, a big success will be assured, which of course means the success of the hospital scheme. The committee are prepared to receive assistance in kind and financially. THE VAGARIES OP EARLESTOWN TOWN HALL CLOCK</p>
        <p>During:the past week or so the clock in the tower of the Earlestown Town Hall has been committing all manner of vagaries. The chiming arrange- ments have all gone wrong. When the crock should have struck twelve, it simply chimed the quarter hour and the other night whilst striking eight, it, at the same time chimed a quarter to eight. Finally the clock stopped altogether. On making an in- spection it was found that starling had built their nests in the clock tower and the birds in visiting the tower have alighted upon the mechanism of the clock and upset the arrangements. One bird was found with its leg fast in the cog wheels and this had stopped the clock. On being released the works were again set in motion, and at present the clock is working all right. MARRIAGE OE AN EARLESTOWN JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>At St John's Church, Earlestown, on Thursday, the wedding was solemnised of Mr Thomas E Clough,for nine years representative of the "Earles- town Guardian" and Miss Lucy K Millington. by the Rev J Carson, rector of Newton-in-Makerfield, assisted by the Rev T R Matthews, vicar of the parish, in the presence of a fair number of people. The bridesmaid was Miss Stronger, and the best man Mr Harry Makeham, both of Earlestown. Among many valuable and useful presents was one from the staff of the "Mid-Sussex Times," with which paper Mr Clough was previously connected, and another from the committee of the West Lanca- shire Homina Society, namely. a beautiful gold pin, set with ruby and diamonds, he being secretary of tbe society. ILLEGAL BACI-A.TELLE AT EAULESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Newton Petty Sessions, before Mr in illespie and three other magistrates, John Pearson. of Orford-street, Earlestown pleaded guilty to keeping a bagatelle board for public use without a licence. Sergeant Lings said that on the 6th inst. he visited the defendant's restaurant in Orford- street, and on going upstairs found some boys play- bagatelle. Defendant had no licence for its UEEI.- Mr Riley, of St Helens, who defended. said the table had been there for twenty years. About eighteen months ago when Pearson bought the house and goodwill, the table was included. He was perfectly unaware that it was illegal. Mr Riley asked, in view of defendant's good character, that the case be dismissed on payment of costs —The Chairman said they would dismiss the case on payment of costs on condition that the table was not used in future unlbss a lisence was taken out for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CAMBRIDGE LEMONADE.</head>
        <p>CAMBRIDGE LEMONADE.</p>
        <p>Carefully prepared ftect Sicilian Lemons. Guaranteed to contain the natural refreshing constituents of the Fresh Ripe Fruit, free from any added acid or other injurious ingredient. A Grocer writes The Cam- bridge Lemonade is far and away the best I have sold yet. . . . A customer to whom I introduced it has within the last six weeks used four dozen. She said ahe had used every other Ismonade she could get, but CAMBRIDGE BEATS ALL." 50 per bottle to make two gallons. ChipPrn and Snno. 12A.. Hiatnn, Cambridge.</p>
        <p>A Rusholme youth, of 17, named Henry Marg. has committed suicide by taking carbolic acid b.cause he had been discharged from his employment as an errand boy.</p>
        <p>WANTED experienced GENERAL, plain conking. agerl • 20 to 30,f0r family of three.—Apply FARRINOND, 16. Upper Di,vonson streat, Wigan</p>
        <p>W ARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIA TION FOOTBALL LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>• . The GENERAL ll&amp;RBTING of the abera will'be held at the At.tagute or GRANBY, on THURSDAY, 3YNR sth, arr 7.30 Pro. Clubs Intending to join must attend or *tit Mr LLOYD, 2, Egypt-street, Warrington. at once. J. LLOYD, Hon. Sec:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>The 2nd V.8.-3Ou-t-I;'l7n-ca.shi-r-e-R—e-g-i-m—ent, St. Helens, have received from the War Office a Maxim gun and carriage, and steps are being taken to arrange for a :Maxim gun section, which will consist of one officer,one sergeant and fifteen men. Captain E F Oakeley, who has been through the South African war with the let South Lancashires, has taken up the duties of adjutant. The Battalion will go into camp at Perham Down, Salis- bury Plain, from August 3 to 10, and the Probable num- bers going to camp are 25 officers and 650 men. By permission of Lord Gerard, the battalion will carry out an attack practice in Garswood Park on Saturday, June 7. •Major J T Hopgood and Lieut. F S Biram will be in charge of the regimantal part 7 which will visit London to take part in the Coronation proceedings. Captain and 803. Major J H Dickinson has resigned his com- mission, with permission to retain his rank and uni- form.</p>
        <p>SAD DOMESTIC TRAGEDY. A very sad discovery was made on Wednesday night at the Victoria Park, the chief pleasure resort of St. Helens, when the body of Mrs. Elizabeth Caldwell, aged twenty-four, wife of William Caldwell, of 15, Bronte- street, was found in the ornamental water in the centre of the park. It appears that Mr a Caldwell was only recently confined, and the baby died a week after. 'Since this time she had been very much depressed. Oa Tuesday afternoon she told her husband that but for him she would destroy herself. He did hii best to cheer her up, and suggested that she should go to her parents' house for a few hours. Caldwell was busy working up 'to 8 p.m., and when he went home found his wife was missing. Search was made all Tuesday night, but no trace was found; until at dusk on Wednesday night Mrs Caldwell's hat Was found by the side of the water, and later her body was dragged out. Much sympathy is felt for tile husband.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOwN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOwN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Earlestown came off second best in their en- counter with Wigan on Saturday, The relative scores were Earlestown 99, Wigan 144—mainly due to the wretched display made by the Earlestow&amp; team. Out of the 99 made by the latter, Husband Miller and Robinson made TO, so that it was left to the remaining 8 players to make the wretched total of 29. So far the play of the Earlestown eleven has not been encouraging and there will have to be an alteration if they intend to win any matches.</p>
        <p>The second eleven did much better, simply over- whelming the Wigan second striniz,by. over SO runs.</p>
        <p>I Newton scored an easy victory over Leigh on the , home ground, losing only 8 wickets for 16 runs. Turton and Henderson made big scores, and the bowling for Newton was to good for the Leigh batters.</p>
        <p>The Leigh second team managed to defeat the Newton second eleven by 43 runs. Wilkinson and Gee were rho only players on the Newton side to make double figures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS AT EAR LESTOWN.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS AT EAR LESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening an interesting gatbezing of Church workers and officials took place at the Mission Hall, Earlestown, on the occasion of presentations to three who have, for some time been connected with St. John's Church. The proceeding were initiated by a tea to which about 150 persons were present, the tables being presided over by members of the Church Army I Mission, and lady members of the Church con;regation. I The Vicar (Rev T R Matthews) presided, and the presen- tations were made by Dr Mouncey, and the Vicar, dur- ing intervals in an excellent musical propamme. The first recipient was Mr S. Findley who for some time acted as warden at S. John's Church and the gift consisted of an inkstand, on which was a plate in- scribed "Presented to Mr S. Findley by the clergy and church officials of St. John's Church, Earlestown, as a token of their esteem and in reccgaition of his services as sidesman and warden." _</p>
        <p>Dr Mounce7 in making the presentation said he was exceedingly sorry that Mr Findley was compelled by circumstances, over which he had no control, to resign his position as warden, for a period. He hoped he would accept the present as a slight recognition of the esteem and regard in which he was hAd. Mr Findlay in acknowledgment thanked all who had contributed to the present, and said ho had only done his best, but it was a great pleasure to him to know that he was appreciated. tante neat presentation was to Miss Egan, who is leav- ing to enter a training home ot the Church Army at London. The Vicar, in presenting her with a Bible, on behalf of the members of her class, a leather handbag and gold mounted umbrella on behalf of the Sunday School teachers, and a purse of money from the Mission Hall Bible Class, said he was sorry to lose such a valuable servant. He ba.l known her a long time and had always found her punctual and zelloua at her work. She had the sympathy of the Church members in her future work. . _</p>
        <p>Miss Egan very briefly but suitably acknowledged the gifts. [be other recipients were Mr and Mrs Edmund Matvlsley on the occasion of their marriage. Both were teachers at the Sunday school and members of the church choir, and the gifts &amp;insisted of a handsome clock from the choir, and a jam pot and spoons from the t_4unday school teachers. Tha Vicar in making the presentation said be was glad to think that Mr and Mrs Mawdsley would con- tinue to be connected with the churn work. -</p>
        <p>Mr Mavrdeley thanked the givers for their useful presents. The programme was then continued. and a must enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO COUNCILLOR GILMOUR.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO COUNCILLOR GILMOUR.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening, at the Wellington Hotel, Earlestown, Councillor W Gilmour was the recipient of a silver tea and coffee service. subscribed for by his constituents in the Town Hall Ward, and others. The service was a handsome one, and consisted of tea pot, coffee pot, cream jug and sugar bowl and tray, the inscription on the latter being :—" Presented to Coun- cillor William Gilmour by the township generally, as a small token in recognition of his highly appreciated ser- vices during the last 7 years. Earlestown, May 29, 1002."—Dr Patterson should have presided, but he was prevented from being present through detention in Liverpool. Mr W Sutton presided temporarily, and the vice-chair was occupied by Mr W Derbyshire. The presentation was made by Councillor E T Evans, who said he was not much of a speaker. He would let those beautiful things speak for themselves, and show the esteem in which Mr Gilmour was held. He was sure they would all agree with him in thinking that Mr Gilmour had proved himself worthy of that present, and be could speak of his work as a colleague. He was!a good member, and one who would net let things rest. In Earlestown they bad no need of a Mr Norbury Williams to expose them. They had a gentle- man present in the person of Mr Gilmour who would sift things our. He then formally presented him with the service and said he wished Mr Gilmour llong life to enjoy the articles, before they were handed down to his descendants. (Applause.) Councillor Gilmour in returning thanks said as long as he was a member of the Urban District Council he should endeavour to serve the township to the best of his ability. The evening was afterwards spent in a sociable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>HALF-HOLIDAY MOVEMENT IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HALF-HOLIDAY MOVEMENT IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Grocers' Association. At the Druids' Elall,Warrincton, on Thursday craning a meeting in connection with the Warringron and Dis- trict Grocers and Provision Dealers' Association was held Mr 'John E Richardson. president of the Association occupied the chair. This was the last meeting of the Amociation during the first year of its existence in the town and satisfaction was expressed at the progress which had been already made. _ . • .</p>
        <p>A deputation from the National Federation of Shop Assistants consisting of Mr Farquhar and Mr Owen attended in fulfilment of an arrangement made two months ago respecting the half-holiday movement in the town. The members of the deputation pointed out that the movement bad undergone a great change since the shop assistants called the attention of the Association to the matter. There now seemed every prospectlof a Thursday half-holiday being established in the town in commemoration of the Coronation. The deputation de- fired to thankthe members of the Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association for having treated the shop assist. ants in a sympathetic manner in the first instance. Mr Rictrannsos, in replying on behalf of the Asso- ciation, said the members had decided to adopt the Thursday half-holiday on and after June s:h. They hoped that this action would be followed by other traders, and that the Co-operative Society,sahich gave a half-holiday on Tuesday would fail int) line and make the movement general in the town. The deputation then withdrew. Toe Association proceeded to the elect'on of of-Beers for the ensuing year, with the following rcsult Presi- dent. Mr John E Richardson; vi,e-presidents, Mr Davey and Mr John E Gandy ; hon. secretary, Mr A Bowden; hon. treasurer, Mr Povey. The meeting terminated with an acknowledgment of the president's and secre- tary's services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30, 1902.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY MAY 30, 1902.</p>
        <p>THE SUPPOSED 'SHROUD OF OHRISI. A Remarkable Story.</p>
        <p>The existence of sacred relics, especially of those which have possessed or are supposed to possess, wonderful healing powers to those who had faith in them, has always provided science with subject matter for scoffing. In fact the mo3t dearly cherished beliefs of the Church have often been in opposition to the experimental observations of the scientific world. IL therefore causes something like a thrill of curiosity, if nothing deeper, to find that ta, at any rate, one branch of science i e., photography, we are appar- ently indebted for a confirmation of some of the statements of Holy Writ with recard to the death and burial of the Lord Jesus ''Christ. It is now many hundreds of years since it was written by St. Matthew that "when Joseph had taken the body, he wrapped it in a clean linen cloth." And by St. Mark that "Joseph bought fine linen, end took him down, and wrapped him in the linen." And by St. John that " There came also Nicodemus which at the first came to Jesus by night,and brought a mix- ture of myrrh and aloes about a hundred pound in weight. Then took they the body of Jesus, and wound it in linen clothes with the spices as the manner of the Jews is to bury." The description calls our attention to two facts: first, a linen shroud; second, the saturation of the shroud with aloes and myrrh and spices. In the old account these statements naturally passed as mere details, and although it was claimed that in the Cathedral of Turin the identical shroud itself was preserved, it was looked upon simply as a relic. But now science, in the shape of photography, has stepped in and revealed the fact that a veritable photo- graphic impression has been left on the shroud by the presence of the sacred body of the Lord Jesus Christ. And science also under- takes to explain how that impression has been made. First of all, look at the fact of the impression being there.</p>
        <p>I Tn.; shroud itself is a yellow-coloured sheet of linen bearing certain reddish-brown stains, and was contained in an urn over the altar of Turin Cathe- dral. According to tradition it was venerated in Constantinople until 1205, when it dis- appeared, being recovered in 1353. •In the fifteenth century it passed into the hands of the house of Savoy, and was deposited at Chamb6ry. Finally it was transferred in 1578 to its present resting place by Duke Emmanuel Philibert, who wished to snare Carlo Borromeo, the sainted Archbishop of Milan, the fatigue of a pilgrimage to its distant Savoyard shrine.</p>
        <p>The stains have long been considered to represent an impression of Christ. but it had been almost forgotten when it was taken out of its reliquary in 1898, on the occasion of an Exhibition of Sacred Art. Then some photographs were specially taken by Signor Secondo Pia of Turin, and the n.gative on being developed showed the exact positive image of a corpse. The assertion led to a great deal lof controversy. Some people contended that these extraordinary images were supernatural, while others declared they were nothing but common paintings dating from 1353. Eighteen months ago they came under the notice of M. Paul Vignon, doctor of science in Paris. and M Colson, assistant professor at the Polytechnic School of Government Engineers and Artillery Officers, and they began a series of experiments, the reSult of which was communicated to the Academy of Sciences by Professor Yves Delage the well-known zoologist and physicist. The apparent infer- ence was that the :marks on the shroud were themselves a negative imprint, and this re- markable fact led the investigators to begin researches. They found that the shroud bore a startlingly exact image of a dead body which Had been nailed by the wrists and feet, had bore flagella- tion by Roman scourges weighted with lead, and had received a thrust in the side, while the head and face distinctly showed signs of wounds from thorns and of having been smitten.</p>
        <p>As it had been suggested that they might have been common paintings transformed later on into negatives by a chemical action, he demonstrated that was impossible on the ground that no medimval artist would have thought of attempting such a realistic presentment of a corpse, in whin even the drops of blood were of the natural spheri- cal shape, The communication to the Academy proceeds as follows : Moreover, the images are infinitely superior from the anatomical point of view to anything. that could have been produced in the Middle Ages, and they correspond to all the geometries] conditions of the marks. They consequently do not constitute a pictorial work. The winding-sheet has really preserved the marks of a body which bore the stigmata, or marks, of astonishing realism." Dr Vignon finds in the image all the stigmata of the Passion. Ile demonstrates by the photographs of the shroud that the body of the man burie] in that shroud had worn a crown of thorns placed far back on the head. On the upper part of the fore- head blood had flowed from several wounds, and trickled slowly down in a furrow to the eyebrow, where it had coagulated. Another photograph shows the traces of the scourging. The whips found at Pompeii would have produced wounds and lacerations of the form found on the shroud. The mark of the lance is there, and also the marks of the nails driven, not through the hands, as they are generally represented in pictures and on crucifixes, but through the wrists. The weight of the body would, in most cases, tear the hand. 31. I:6nan affirmed, in his "Life of Jesus," that,generally,when criminals were crucified, they were beaten astride on a piece of w00d.1411 these marks are to be found reproduced on the shroud with surprising correct- ness and realism, These, then, are the facts and now for the ex- planation. The problem was how could the image have been produced on the linen by natural causes. At this stage Dr. Vignon started certain experi- ment*. He was given the clue to the solution of the problem ty an experiment made by Commandant Colson Professor at the Polytechnic School, who demonstrated the possibility of producing negative images by vap-surs capable of acting chemically on a suitablo substance. Ele found that a corpse shortiv after death emits ammoniacal vapours, and that thl latter react chemic- ally union oil mixed with essence of aloes, thP aubstalle9 with which the shroud of Christis rec3rded to have been imoregnated.</p>
        <p>Dr Vignon—proved that a linen she smeared with this oil will reproduce with the f xactness of a photograph the imprint of a body. giving out the ammoniacal vapours which permanont'y dye the Horn a reddish brnwr.</p>
        <p>- Proceeding, the writer of the paper maintains that there is a remarkable agreement with the historic cir- cumstances of the borial. It was necessary the body should be neither washed, anointed, nor bound in winding iiheets, and a oatmeal of the original text of the gospel will show that it wag tins,. Finally, it was also necessary that the body should not have remained in the shroud sufficiently long for it to decompose. Everyone no matter what may be his religious opinion, says the communication, k lows that on Easter Sunday the tomb was empty. The French Academy of Sciences, while displaying the keenest interest in Professor DAage's communication. declined, on the ground that the matter was controve-- sial• to follow his suggestion to appoint an official corn— mittne to inquire further into the question, and the " Times " wisely declines to discuss the validity of the I shroud, but the "Ls ncet," taking up the scientific study of thn facts, says</p>
        <p>_ • . We have frequently recorded in our columns the fact deduced in an elaborate research by Dr W J Russell. F.R.S., that almost all substances are able in the dark to act on the photographic plate and to produce a picture. The phenomenon would appear to he established alwaysin the presence of an oxidising process, and Dr Russell at length came to the concluaiom that reraxide of hydrogen was the main factor concerned. In the case of the sheet in which tradition save that the dead Christ was wrapped, we have the analogue probably of a photographic plate or sensitised film. The cloth was impregnated with oils and aloes. It is well known that fixed oils are sensitive to oxids- ul,,,aaotohcetr dead tion and aloes contain constituenta allied to the pyro- gallic acid series, which would probably turn brown in the presence of an oxidising tho ne was recorded which, therefore, rtehf re cloth d ppoPf:r o s' t e m I cal change rather than to the effect of light. On this explanation an exact image, even to minute details such as wounds produced by the thorns and the II marks of the blood drops and of flagellation by whips ef 1 a definite kind, is not by any means beyond the bounds of probability_ It is an inteaaely remarkable and inter- esting instance of the light which the very latest develop- ments of scientific search may throw on tradi- tions and controversial matters In history. We arc face to face undoubtedly with a set of new phenomena, giving diatinct indications of the existence of emanationa hitnerto not recognised from bs T d u h ht anrc.;tnsi both animate and inanimate bodies. The disovery of these emit' tions has hen due to the fact that they affect the aensitired ailver film, hut there is no doubt that there is a very large number of suhatances al l o whizh are affected in a similar way, thou 213 not to the same decree as silver in the presence of albuminou suhstancee. Natural photographs appear on al ides, as is proved by the simple experi</p>
        <p>ment of placing an opaque object on grass 1 or on fresh gravel exposeu to the light. An exact outline of the shape of the object will be found Cu removing it after a time. The property of radio-activity is different, the substance in this case actually emitting radiations itself. The images due to the emanation of vapour are yet another but not less; interesting pheno- menon, as is abundantly manifested in the case of the traditional winding sheet of Christ." Now for the objections. First, there is the historical objection to the authenticity of the shroud itself raised by Father Thurston, of Farm-street Church. He notes that the Abti Ulysse Chevalier claims to have proved ' to demonstration that the winding sheet is a spurious relic manufactured in the fourteenth century. rne abhe is a scholar of distinction, and of his perfect loyalty to the Catholic Church there can be no possible question. The Jesuit organ, "Analecta Bollandiana," states that the alibi's discussion of the subject is final, and that "it only remains to declare with a clear and unmistakeable voice,' as Pope Clement VII, declared it, that ' this figure . . . is not from the true sweat of our Lord Jesus Christ."</p>
        <p>It is affirmed, too, that the story of the "image of the shroud " giveo by Creoffroy de Lirey to the college founded by him in 1353 is not lost in the mist of ages, and does not happen to present any of those obscurities by which the historian who wishes to impart his own laboriously- acquired conviction to others must at times fled himself babied. We have, for instance, the document addressed to the Pope by Behop Peter d'Arcis, in which he denounces the fraudulent dealing of the Chapter of Lirey,who for motives of avarice pretended that miracles were wrought by this shroud, whereas his predecessor in the see of Troyes had officially investigated the matter and proved it to be a forgery. The Avignon Pope, Clement VII., permitted the exposition of the winding sheet on condition that it should be explained to the people that it is not the real shroud but a devo- tional representation of it.</p>
        <p>Father -Thurston concludes : "We are not, of courqe, in any way bound to believe that those responsible for the subsequent veneration of this alleged relic have been guilty of cronscious fraud. Just as in the case of so many facsimiles of the Holy Veils, what was in the firs instance a mere copy for devotional purposes has corn e in time to figure as an original—the wish, no doubt, be- ing father to the thought, but probably without any deliberate insincerity." Scientific objections have also been raised, for inst- ance :</p>
        <p>M. Roux asked Dr. Vignon why he had not attomptsd a decisive experiment with a wools body obtained from an anatomical institution. Dr Vignan ropded that he had thought of doing this, but he had at present been unable to do so on account of the difficulty of obtaining a body whicn united the accede sry.special conditions. . _</p>
        <p>Dr. Andrew Wilson, in the " Illustrated London News," also says:- 6 Those who have followed Dr Russell's experiments on photographing in the dark will remember that he demonstrated the possibility of an imprint being loft on a sensitive plate through contact witu any objct from which peroxide of hydrogen was evolved. There is, of course, no parallelism between a linen shroud and a sensitised plate, but it might probably be argued that science has not vet exhausted all the possibilities where- by an imprint might even be left upon the former in virtue of certain purely chemical reactions. There is nothing known in photographic science which can lend countenance to the notion that the shroud at Turin acting as a ' plate ' received a negative imprint thereon. As for a photograph of the shroud developing a positive imprint of the body enclosed in it, this is equally inex- plicable, unless we are to translate the description as meaning that on a photograph of the shroud there came out markings or appearances suggestive of the configura- tion of a human body. This, of course, is a very differ- ent thing from the asaumptioa that an actual negative photographed en tbe shroud was reproduced in the photographic obverse. Several theories may be held by those who regard the Turin shroud incident as worthy of further investigation. There may be, first of all, the perpetration of the very fraud of the possibiltty of which Dr Vignon appears to be so sceptical. The alleged exactitude of the markings on the photograph corresponding to these on the Saviour' a body, it must be confessed, rather favours this first hypothesis. In the Case of a fradulant imitation, ties chances are that the imposture would be made very deflate and exact. A second view is that already suggeatod—nainely, that an imprint of some body or other, photographed from the shroud and developed, has given rise to the supposition of the preservation of the marks of Calvary. This view presupposes, of Course that the shroud is really that in which Christ's body was enveloped. As for the expsriments of Dr Vignon, they will require confirmation. If it be true teat ammoniacal vapours combined with oil and essence of aloes will con- vey to a sheet saturate with the latter an exact imprint of is body enclosed in it, the sooner the fact is substanti- ated the more satisfactory will be Dr Vignon's position. Many persons may, and probably will, regard the wools matter as frivolous. Many orthodox Roman Catholics will assuredly adopt this position ; and there is, lastly, one point of extreme importance, which appears to constitute ' the little rift within the lute ' in the case of Dr Vignon's experimentation. As we have seen, he demands the existence of amtnoniacal vapours proceeding [torn the body enrolled in the sheet as a condition for the reproduction of its imprint. Ammonia is one of the products of aoimal putre- faction and decay. As it is an accepted dogma of the Chrittian religion that the Saviour's body suffered no decay, it may bo interesting to learn whence the aminoniacal vapours, postulared by the Freon say.iiit as the cause of the imprint on the Turin shroud, could have been derived. It has been suggested that the ammoulacal odours could have arisen fro:n the sweat of the body ea closed in the shroud. Perspiration is a more or less evanescent secretion, add that the small ameunt of ' ammonia whioh might be contained in the skin-produces could assist in producing an exact reproduction of the body, is, 10 my mind, almost inconceivable. Be that as it may, the whole case rests on theory and unverified experiment." As for the image on the shroud itself : it must be noticed that the expression of the features recalls the conventional image of Jesus of Nazareth as he has been generally depicted through the Middle Ages and in modern times. The treatment of the hair is the same, and there is the same expression of sadness which is often paurtrayed.—'' Temple Magazine " for June.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARRINGTON POLICE</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICE</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Beiore Alderfnen Fair°lough (In the chair) and</p>
        <p>Hutchinson. SCZNE: i PIERPOINC BTREET —.John Bennett, lodging at 72 Pierpoint-strea t, was charged with being drunk aud dis- orderly in Pierpoint-street on ruesciav afternoon, and also with assaulting Pollee-Constable rirewey. The offi?er stated that he Was called into the house by prisoner's grandmother, who told him that prisoner had come home drunk and was acting in such a mariner that she was afraid of him. Witness spoke to the prisoner, who rushed to the firegrate and seized the poker, which was, however, taken from him by some neighbours, At the grandmother's request the prisoner was put into the street, and he then became very violent and struck witness in the mouth. It was with the utmost diffi- culty that the prisoner was brought to the police station, The bench decided to flue prisoner 5a and cost 9 for belng drunk and 10s and costs for the assault.</p>
        <p>THURSDir,—Before Messrs John Fairclough (in the chair) J It Young and James Hepherd. AN OLD OFFENDER.—George Wetsby, of Croft, wee charged with being °runic and disorderly in Horsemarket- street.on Wednesday afternoon. Superintendent Monaghan said that the pnsoner was annoying farmers who had assembled at the Corn liachange. He was also 'lrking use of bad language. The prisoner had been convicted 44 times for various offences. Prisoner was now fined ifs and costs. _</p>
        <p>MAINTENANCE OASES -- Hilward Livesey. labourer, whose address was given as 'last from Lancaster," was Summoned to show cause why he should not be committed to gaol for non-payment of £4 163 65, due to his wife for her mainten- ance under an order of the Bench. Prisoner admitted the money was due, hut he said ha had been out of work. The wife: He does not intend to pay any- thing. He has not given me a penny this year. I —Prisoner was ordered to pay the money immediately or go to gaol for a month —David Griffiths, 98 Williamson-Square, was summoned for a similar offence. Mr Fowler appeared for. the complainant and stated that the amount due under the order of the Bench was £4 8s 6d. Prig mer said he was arrested last night and had had no chance to obtain legal assistance. He wished the ease to be adjourned. He and his wife had lived cogetler since the order was made. In th ase circumstances the case wss adjourned for a week. AN IMPUDENT BEGnAn.--Catherine Hands, an old woman win has no settled place of abode, was charged with begging in the Market—place lest night. —P.C. Clough, who was on duty in plain clothes In the market. said the woman used the most abusive and obscene language when she was refused assistance. She told one woman that " she was lamn kit not buried."—Prisoner was committed to gaol for seven days</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SHOCKING TRAMWAY FATALI TY_ AT Sr. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING TRAMWAY FATALI TY_ AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The little girl, Miry Millingtoa flighm, ag—Jl--s.even daughter of Mr Bighorn, who was Isnocked do fln by an electric-aar in North-road, St Helens, on Wednesday afternoon of last week, died ors Wednesday last. The child is stated to have run in front of tha car, arad she had her skull fractured and received other iojurie. Her state from the first was considered hopeless.</p>
        <p>After spending last Thursday at Alderley a F.liston agent, named Herbert Frith, weat to the Wiiton Arms in Cannon-strect, Manchester. Els was refussd drink. and as he was leaving tha place he fell, vriking his head against a fixad sett. .I+l,ct chy he died from a ractured skull,and Oil Wednesdays coroner's jury found fhat the occurrence was " accidential."</p>
