      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EFFECT OF SEAWEED</head>
        <p>THE EFFECT OF SEAWEED</p>
        <p>UPON THE STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BOWELS.</p>
        <p>W Roberts, 36, Park-s—treet,--R-hosddne, Wrexham, writes, November 4th. I write to thank you for the effect Veno's Seaweed Tonic has had upon me. I suffered in 1892 from influenza, which left use with nervous debility, coostipation, dizziness and headache, but now lam thankful to say I feel stronger', my appetite is , much better and my food digests properly; indeed every dose seems to make me stronger. VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC is prepared from natural Seaweed, combined with veget- able extracts, forming a high-class, pharmaceutical preparation of the highest medicinal activity. It cures deep-seated complaints of the stomach, liver, kidneys and blood, possessing all the healing, strengthening and curative power possible. It is especially good for " habitual constipation." Price is lid and 2s 9J. For , sale by all chemists and medicine vendors everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. The Peace Negotiations.</p>
        <p>A special correspondent of the " Daily Chronicle," telegraphing from 'Pretoria, states that the result of the Boer conference will probably be announced shortly. Any communication with the deiegates has been rigidly forbidden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Peace "Really at Hand."</head>
        <p>Peace "Really at Hand."</p>
        <p>The " Daily News," in its first leading article this naorning, remarks :—lf we take all the facts together— Lord Milner's departure to Johaanesburg, the assurance of Mr Balfour's tone, and the final assemb!a,ge at Vereeniging—we cannot retain a shadow of doubt that peace is really at hand. As we write our trustworthy , Parliamentary correspondent sends us a rumour from the House of Commons that peace is finally arranged and may possibly be proclaimed to-jay, on the King's birthday, from the steps of the Mansion house. It seems then that the Vereeniging conference has balloted and decided in favour of peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST HELENS' TOWN_ CLERK GOES TO</head>
        <p>ST HELENS' TOWN_ CLERK GOES TO</p>
        <p>BOURNEMOUTH.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Bournemouth Town Council (in committee) was held on Monday afternoon, to further consider the applications for the office of Town Clerk, and to interview the four selected candidates,v:z, Mr G W Bailey, Town Clerk ef St Helene; Mr G T Lee, Town Clerk of Dewsbury ; Mr It M Prescott. Town Clerk of Fulham(one of the new Metropolitan boroughs); and Mr J T Taylor, Town Clerk of Harrogate. Mr Leo is one of the selected candidates for the Town Clerkship of Derby, and withdrew from the contest hero rather than prejudice his chance there. Tho other three gentlemen attended, and in turn waited upon the Council, read their respective addresses, and advocated their respec- tive claims, The decision of the committee will be formally communicated to the Council at their meeting next week ; till it is formally adopted by the Council in open session it is not technically a decision of the Cor- poration, and is subject to review. Inasmuch, however, as every member of the 3 outwit was present, with the exception of Alderman Frampton, who was detained at lhome by illness ; and as the vote recommending the appointment was a unanimous one, the matter may safely be regarded as settled. The future Town Clerk is Mr George William Bailey, at present Town Clerk of St. Helens, Lancashire. He is 38 years of age, a solicitor, arid also a member of the Honourable Society of the Inner Temple. He claims to have had 20 years' experience in local gov(rnment work having—prier to going to St. Helens—for five years held the office of Assistant Town Clerk and Solicitor at Oldham, and also acted as Deputy Clerk of the Peace; and having, subsequent to that, for six years held the offico of Town Clerk and Solicitor to the Cornoration of the borough of Eccles; from whence in 1899 he pro- ceeded to Se. Helens. Mr Bailey has very high testi- monials, including one from the Mayor of Sr. Helens, Colonel W W Pilkington, who is yachting in Bourne- mouth Bay,—anchoring the yacht in Poole Harbour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LIBEL ACTION BY THE N.S P.O</head>
        <p>LIBEL ACTION BY THE N.S P.O</p>
        <p>In the King's Bench, on Thursday, the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Childr,”s and its founder and manager, the Rev Benjamin Waugh, sucd Mr A(3 Bruce-Pryce for damages for alleged libel in certain letters which the latter published in the " Cheltenham Examiner." In one of the letters com- plained of the defendant wrote "That the very worst use to which a guinea or a shilling could be put 17 as to give either to a soeiety which wracked this homes of the poor by false lying or exaggerated statements which were presented to magistrates and which taught children to give false evidence against their parents." Defendant denied that the statements complained of were libels, and pleaded that they were fair and bona fide comment upon matters of public interest. Sir E Clarke. KC. continuing his opening statement, said that no more eloquent testimony could be borne to the value of the work of the society than the fact that last year 2,900 persons were summoned for offences, and there were only 95 persons with regard to whom it warrinot found by the magistrates that the evidence sustained the charge. Counsel said defendant had a grudge against the society because of the prosecution of his gardener and his wife for reelecting their children. Mr B Waugh said prior to 1884 he was Congregational Minister at Greenwich, and he had served on the Lon- don School Board for six years. The National Society was 'started in 1884, the president being the late Earl of Shaftesbury. It was non trile that the society was founded to provide him with an income. He had an income of £5OO a year from the " Sunday Magazine," and £3OO as Congregational Minister at Greenwich. The "Child's Guardian," the society's paperowas started in 1886, and in 1893 and 1894 he received £l5O for his work in editing that pap-r. The work of trie society increased so much that in 1895 he was ask.d to devote himself entirely to it, and a salary of £5OO a year, in addition to the £l5O, was voted to him, but he declined to accept the latter amount, as the former represnnted what he had lost by giving up his other work. Police constables were treated by the society as any other wit- nesses for work done outside their ordinary duties. In the result all charges were withdrawn, defendant paying costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Mr F H Aukland, On; "(Y.t.he'relieving officers of the Warrington Union, is amongst the selected candidates for the position of relieving officer, vaccination officer and registrar in connection with the Atcharn Union, larew.sbury. The salary is £lBO per annum.</p>
        <p>ASK fo;-CUNNINCIHAMS HIGH-CLASS 3III4HHAL WATHRS,—Advt</p>
        <p>Good Ternplari. The usual weekly session ot the Temperance Mission Lodge of the IOG T, was held in the , Ebenezer Mission room on Wednesday evening. Bro J W Roberts C T, presided over a good attend- ance. A candidate was admitted as an ancient templar. A good programme was arranged for the next three months. the following programme was rendered by the members :—Ode " Pull for the shore Sister Nurse, recitation, "Dick's Courting ;" Bro Egerton, song The Tar's Farewell ;" Sister (4 Hughes. recitation," Comrade Jim; a story of the battle of Grasnan." The meeting closed with the singing of" God be with you till we meet again.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. Gratifying success attended the Sunday School Anni- versary services in connection with this place of worship which were held on Sunday. There were large congrega- tions, the church being crowded in the evening. The preacher at the morning and evining services was the Rev Owen Watkins, of Cardiff, and formerly of this town, who preached interesting arid earnest discourses. In the morning the rev gentleman spoke from the words ," and in the evening took for his text " Thy face Lord will I seek " (Psalm 27, Per 89 8.) In the afterloon a musical service was held, presided over by Mr Isaao Hayward, now of Wakefield, but who was connected with Rowsey-road Church since its erection and for many years was associated with Silver-street school. He impressed upon his hearers the importaY:ce'of culti- vating the intellect as well as the physique. Councillor Brou,gla delivered an address On " The Prodigal's return," and gave appropriate advice to parmts and scholars. The collection at this service realized £22, and was contributed mainly by school workers. It is interesting to note that the children collected between £7 and £8 in their classes, and the amount is considered highly creditable. The singing was a special feature of the day's services, and the scholars gave evidence of the careful training they had received from Mr Upson, and Mr Wadsworth. tbe former of whom conducted. The choir rendered the anthems Sine, 0 Heavens .Sullivan," " Hear my prayer" (Mead,elssohn) and Through the day 'thy love bath spared us" (Naylor) in excellent style, reflecting great redit on irg Wade. worth, the organist and choirmaEtlr. The chief solos were sung by Mrs D S Stone, Mrs Hanna n, sod Mr Baker. The collections amounted to £4B, which is expected will be augmented to £5O when the donations arrive from thoee Mends who were unavoidably absent. The church is at present undergoing re-decoration by Messrs Flesketh and Son, and it is expected that the work will be completed in a short time. The organ is also ng renovated b y Mr A Caldwell, of Et, Helms.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS FIRES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A Co-operative Stores Damaged.</p>
        <p>About 3.45 on Friday morning Mr. Robert Lowe dis- covered an outbreak of fire on the branch establishment of the Runcorn Co-cperative Society, in Balfour-road. He raised an alarm, and the fire brigade were soon on the scene under the command of Superintendent Robert Wright. On arrival they found the warehouse at the rear of the shop Premises ablaze, but by dint of hard , work they prevented the flames from spreading to the other portion of the building. It was not, however, until the warehouse had been practically gutted that all danger was at an end. The damage to the building and stock is e stimated at £2OO, which is fully covered by insurance. The fire is believed to have originated through rats igniting matches. It seems that a large quantity of matches were placed in a sack, but the water poured upon them by the firemen in extinguishing the fire rendered them non-combustible, otherwise they might have been a source of considerable danger. Superintendent Ennion and several police officers were early on the scene and rendered prompt assistance. :Fire at a Boat-repairing Yard. About 11 20 on Saturday night Mr Percival Buckley, clerk, was passing along the Runcorn footbridge, when he discovered the buildings belonging to the boat-repair- ing yard of Mr Albert Abel, alongside the Ship Canal, on fire. With all possible haste, he raised an alarm, and the fire buzzer was sounded. This promptly brought out the brigade who lost no time in reaching the scene of the conflagration. They discovered the fire to be located in a building next the office, used as a store room for keep- ing oil, tools, kn. A good pressure of water was obtained by fixing the hose to a hydrant in Lord-street, and under the direction of Superintendent Robert Wright and Deputy Superintendent Cousins, the firemen soon got the fire under such control as to prevent its screed- mg to the adjoining office buildings, and at 1 30 all danger was at an end. A portion of the shed which is built of wood, and its contents were destroyed,including a quantity of oil and oakum. P C Bowyer was on the scene soon after the outbreak was discovered, and he was followed by Superintendent Ennion, Inspector Egerton, Sergeant Cooper and Constables Wilson and Gregory, who rendered assistance in keeping back the crowd of people who had been brought on the scene by the sounding of the fire alarm. Mr Abel estimates his damage at several hundred pounds, which is well cover- ed by insurance. The origin of the fire is not known. There had been a fire in a stove used for besting purposes same hours previously, but there was not a sign of an outbreak at 1030,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>•• IUNCOi-{N SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>•• IUNCOi-{N SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly rneeting of the School Board was bell on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr Pritchard : Present, Canon Wood, Messrs F J Norman, S Taylor, E W Handley,R H Pestle% J W Lightburn (cleric) and A Lightburn (assistant clerk).</p>
        <p>ANOTHER ALTERATION. I Resolved on the proposition of Messrs Norman and Imison, that the future meetings of the Board be held on Wednesday at five o'cleck instead of Tuesday in order to convenience Mr. John Littler. THE SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE. The above committee reported that the resignations of Mr John Cragg and Miss Jessie Ashbrook bad been tendered and accepted, Mr Brown being appointed senior assistant in Mr Craeg's place at the Victoria-road School at an increase of £5 per annum. The coronaticn holidays were fixed for the 26th and the 27th June in the absence of an order sanctioning the full week. Re- commended that a committee consisting of Messrs Pritchard, Imison, and Handley be appsinted to deal vr,th the staff at Victoria-read girls school. Accounts amounting to £242 4s Id were passed for payment. I Passed on the motion of Messrs Handley and Taylor. BOARD SCHOLARS TO THE FRONT. Mr Handley said a pleasing item for the Board to know was the result of the examination of the County Council Schelarships.--So far as the Victoria- road Boys School was concerned six boys gained the ' necessary 70 per cent, of the marks. Three of them had been awarded the scholarships. The other three had not got a scholarship because there were 63 who got 70 per cent, while there w.,re only 36 scholarehips to be given to the elementary schools, which had to he allocated in accordance with their rule. Their Board School stood very high and he wished the next boy, George Ralph Clare from the National Schools had , been on the list as he was only two marks behind. He would suggest they should have an honours board. - according to a previous resolution,on which could be put the names of those who had been awarded scholarships. Since 1894 about seventeen lads from the Victoria School had won scholarships excluding those who had won the scholarships but.had int got the quali- fying marks.He also thought that it would be niceif they were to issue a certificate which would be prized by both parents and scholars in after years. He was pleased to inform them that the 36 scholarships were to be increased to 80 next year which was directly due to the exertion of. Mr Imison in placing - the matter before the County Council. Mr Norman endorsed everything said by Mr Handley and seconded the motion. ' Mr. Imison thought it advisable that a supplementary Board be purchased on which could be invcribed the names of those who had gained the qualifying marks but had not won a scholarship. The suggestion was agreed to and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>I</p>
